KANT_TR['wiener/kemp'] = {"wiener-s01-001":["Kant's [Logic, taken down] by a society of auditors",[]],"wiener-s01-002":["Table of Contents",[]],"wiener-s01-003":["Prolegomena",[]],"wiener-s01-004":["790",[]],"wiener-s01-005":["History of Logic",[]],"wiener-s01-006":["796",[]],"wiener-s01-007":["*Tractatio Logices* [Treatment of Logic]",[]],"wiener-s01-008":["805- 940",[]],"wiener-s01-009":["Of Knowledge",[]],"wiener-s01-010":["805",[]],"wiener-s01-011":["Of Assent and the Withholding of Assent",[]],"wiener-s01-012":["859",[]],"wiener-s01-013":["Of Probable Knowledge",[]],"wiener-s01-014":["879",[]],"wiener-s01-015":["Of Concepts",[]],"wiener-s01-016":["904",[]],"wiener-s01-017":["Brief Recapitulation of the Foregoing",[]],"wiener-s01-018":["908",[]],"wiener-s01-019":["Division of all Definitions",[]],"wiener-s01-020":["913",[]],"wiener-s01-021":["Of Logical Division",[]],"wiener-s01-022":["925",[]],"wiener-s01-023":["Of Judgments",[]],"wiener-s01-024":["928",[]],"wiener-s01-025":["Of Inferences of the Understanding",[]],"wiener-s01-026":["938",[]],"wiener-s01-027":["Of Inferences of Reason",[]],"wiener-s01-028":["939",[]],"wiener-s01-030":["[Vienna Logic]",[]],"wiener-s02-001":["Everything in the world takes place according to rules. As we perceive this in corporeal nature, so we find it even in the exercise of our own powers, although we are not even conscious of the rules. We attain to this exercise through mere trials; and from just these trials, as in teaching someone to speak or to walk, we can gather how many rules there are. In this way we can learn many rules from ourselves. Grammar consists of innumerable rules, and for this reason some have even held language to be a divine inspiration. This much, however, is certain, that all languages can, as regards their first principles, be brought together into one grammar. Grammar is, indeed, a doctrine of the understanding [Verstandeslehre]; for as our soul combines concepts, so also must words be combined. It is, however, too abstract for the capacities of schoolboys [Schulfähigkeit]: one need only take the abstract rules concerning the *genus substantivum*, etc. Let us examine more closely these rules according to which our powers act. The understanding is itself the faculty of rules, and the understanding alone can examine these rules. According to what rules does it do so? This is difficult to discover, because, when it itself errs, it cannot state the correctness of the rules according to which it proceeds.",[]],"wiener-s02-002":["All rules for the employment of our powers are either absolutely necessary or conditionally necessary. Without the former no employment of the understanding whatsoever takes place; without the contingent [rules] the employment of the understanding will cease in a certain respect. The necessary rules must be such that they hold for the understanding without distinction of objects. They must concern merely the form of the understanding.",[]],"wiener-s02-003":["Remark. In all thinking there is matter and form. The matter concerns the object; the form, the manner of treatment. Thus physiology and psychology differ as regards their matter; as regards their form they may, if treated empirically, be the same. Our senses supply knowledge with its matter. In the understanding there lies [erg. nicht] intuition. Words are the matter, grammar the form, of language. A science, therefore, which occupies itself with the form of the understanding is entitled logic.",[]],"wiener-s02-004":["Our understanding has manifold objects of knowledge and of science, such as history and mathematics. General logic, however, abstracts from all this content, from all difference of knowledge, and considers in everything only the form of concepts, of judgments and of inferences. In short, it is one of those sciences which prepare us for others.",[]],"wiener-s02-005":["Προπαιδευτική (*propositiones*). Every man observes a rule before he can bring it into formulas; gradually, however, he comes to attend to what he is doing. The sum of all these rules is entitled *logica naturalis*; the science which expounds these same rules systematically, [*logica*] *artificialis*. It is affected to bestow the name of a science upon this natural propensity; one might as well have a natural optics, a natural mechanics, and so forth. Logic, as has been said above, is divided into *naturalis* and *artificialis*. This division is a poor one, because [natural] logic is supposed to be the sum of those rules of the understanding which we exercise without being conscious of them. But since we do not know these rules, it cannot be a science; the division therefore involves a contradiction. *Logica naturalis* is the knowledge of the rules of the understanding *in concreto*; *logica artificialis*, *in abstracto*.",[]],"wiener-s02-006":["With us, therefore, only *logica artificialis* is ever entitled logic. We can impart the laws of our understanding in the following manner:",[]],"wiener-s02-007":["1. Rules as to how we think.",[]],"wiener-s02-008":["2. Rules as to how we ought to think.",[]],"wiener-s02-009":["Sometimes we think quite perversely. Such an employment can never accord with the rules. It is the misuse of the understanding, and is here excluded. Logic teaches the latter, namely, how to employ the objective rules of our understanding. Logic has this peculiarity, that its subjective laws are also objective rules, since the universal rules are the sole condition of our thinking. We sometimes give assent to something from habit or inclination. This is no universal law; consequently the subjective laws of willing cannot be objective laws of the understanding. Some logicians presuppose psychology in logic. Since psychology is an empirical science, there would arise from this a science of how we think under manifold hindrances, not of how we ought to think. Its laws would be wholly contingent laws of nature. But that is not what we are asking. Logical rules must be derived from the necessary employment of the understanding. In a grammar we consider the universal rules without which no thing [Sache] whatsoever can exist.",[]],"wiener-s02-010":["Knowledge is of two kinds: the one *a priori*, which is independent of experience; the other *a posteriori*, which is grounded on empirical principles. Now since experience teaches us only contingent things, whereas general logic abstracts from all content and consequently rests on *principia a priori*, logic is counted among the *scientiae rationales*. Logic is entitled [rational] on two grounds:",[]],"wiener-s02-011":["1.) It is drawn from reason.",[]],"wiener-s02-012":["2.) It has reason for its object.",[]],"wiener-s02-013":["But strictly only this belongs in the definition, that it is rational as regards its object.",[]],"wiener-s02-014":["The aim of every science is either to extend our modes of knowledge or to elucidate them. Logic only elucidates, since it considers the form, which does not admit of extension. A universal precept resting on *a priori* grounds is entitled a canon. Logic is so entitled because it rests on *a priori* rules. It is a canon of all thinking, as morals is a canon of all willing. – [ Textlücke: AA: Die ] Logic is a rational [ Textlücke: AA: -e Wissenschaft], a canon for the understanding, just as grammar serves solely for judging language as regards its form; words are the matter. A science which extends our knowledge is entitled an organon. Logic cannot be so entitled, since it abstracts from all content. Just as little can it be an [ Textlücke: AA: Kunst ], since an art is an organon of a [mode of] knowledge. Some put forward the art of making definitions as an objection. But definitions must always be very carefully examined; and taste, [the capacity] to judge according to laws of the senses [ Textlücke: AA: zu ], has no canon, because it derives *a posteriori*. Logic and aesthetic are thus distinguished by the difference of their objects.",[]],"wiener-s02-015":["Definition. *Logica est scientia regularum universalium usus intellectus* [Logic is the science of the universal rules of the employment of the understanding]. If logic is to be a science of the universal laws of the understanding, these must be necessary rules, since they are to relate to everything without distinction of objects, and without them nothing whatsoever can be thought. All necessary rules must be derived *a priori*; rules of experience are *a posteriori*. Gravity is to be met with throughout the whole world. But I shall never have insight that all bodies are heavy until I have insight *a priori* into the necessity of gravity. A science resting on *principia a priori* is entitled a demonstrated science. Experience cannot be demonstrated, because we must first prove its necessity.",[]],"wiener-s02-016":["Logic is therefore a demonstrated science. A science which can be taught from *principia a priori* is entitled *doctrina*; logic, consequently, is so entitled. When the rule of judgment precedes the act of judging [Beurtheilung], [the science] is entitled *doctrina*; when the judging precedes, critique. H Hence [a science] is entitled critique [ Textlücke ] *doctrina*, according as the rule or the judging comes first. For once I have the rule of experience, I can also judge of it. We now ask: what is logic, a critique or a doctrine? Since it is to be the touchstone, and is to precede [the employment of] our understanding, it is a doctrine. Critical judging always presupposes a doctrine. Logic, therefore, is no critique at all; but it serves for one. –",[]],"wiener-s02-017":["Logic is divided into two parts.",[]],"wiener-s02-018":["1.) Analytic,",[]],"wiener-s02-019":["2.) Dialectic.",[]],"wiener-s02-020":["1.) Analytic, or logic as critique of knowledge in respect of truth, is that part which, through the dissection [Zergliederung] of our understanding, exhibits the universal rule of the form of our understanding, and so the necessary rules of all truth. It is consequently merely the canon for judging [the employment of] the understanding.",[]],"wiener-s02-021":["2.) Dialectic is the misuse of this canon, when we make it into an organon and seek to know truth from it. Analytic is the art of separating in respect of our judgments. There is a strong semblance [Schein] that through the canon for judging the understanding, since it [ Textlücke ] is correct as regards form, we can know truth. But since analytic abstracts from all content and considers nothing further than the form, it cannot be an organon of new truths. Among the ancients the dialecticians were those who disputed *pro et contra*. They were sophists, and sought to deceive. They assumed false propositions, accommodated everything to the formal laws of the understanding, and thereby gave themselves a great semblance of truth. It is possible to speak in logical form of a thing of which one understands nothing, namely, by heaping inferences upon inferences and the like; and thereby the hearer is deceived. Logic is thus misused as an art, and becomes an organon, but not of truth. So long as this art flourished, logic was nothing but a cultivation of dialectic as an art. Even a century ago the best professor was he who expounded dialectic as an art. We entitle dialectic a means whereby it can be known that something runs counter to the formal laws of the understanding. It is consequently only a cathartic [Reinigungsmittel]. Through the senses we are subject to illusion [Schein] in our judgments, inferences, etc. Further, there are certain means of truth which can be imitated without having anything else in common with truth; for example, an author who imitates the form of thoroughness while propounding untruths. By such skills we are often deceived when we are ignorant of the content. Agreement with the logical rules is indeed a *conditio sine qua non*; but it is not sufficient for finding truth. How dialectic arises may be seen in the schools. A school *chria* is full of [ Textlücke ] words without content.",[]],"wiener-s02-022":["Our author holds that dialectic is the logic of probability. But probability is a judgment about truth according to correct but insufficient grounds; and if its grounds are correct, it belongs to analytic. Dialectic is the logic of illusion, where the illusion is regarded as empty [vor leer angenommen]. Logic can be applied only to objects; but general logic treats [of thinking] without distinction of objects. Consequently the canon must be theoretical. General logic, moreover, cannot be applied for this further reason, that practical logic presupposes modes of knowledge and sciences, whereas general logic, being a propaedeutic, can presuppose no sciences.",[]],"wiener-s02-023":["For there is no practical part in logic, as our author supposes; otherwise dialectic would be pure illusion. There can be no practical logic, such as [there is] an art of education [Erziehungskunst]; for this already presupposes the study [der ] all [ Textlücke: AA: -gemeinen Logic ] [of general logic]. All sciences, however, are a *praxis* of logic, since without logic nothing can make progress. Logic must therefore be divided not into a theoretical and a practical part, but into a dogmatic and a technical part. The dogmatic part is the canon; the technical is the prescription of the rules of the school. General logic is to consider the form of the understanding. It therefore abstracts from all speculation, and considers the logic of universal human reason; here it departs entirely from the sciences. Logic, like all sciences which have their concepts *a priori*, is divided into the logical doctrine of elements and the doctrine of method. The doctrine of elements is the dialectic or analytic of reason, or theoretical logic; for it is propaedeutic.",[]],"wiener-s02-024":["The doctrine of method is the logic of the form of a system of modes of knowledge. A special doctrine of method can be drawn up for one science or another; and this is then technical logic, or the organon. This organon can come only at the end of a science, because only then do I know the nature of the science. It is the completion of the rules to their perfection, and contains all the *termini technici* by which we distinguish the logical directions in critique. It is what, in oratory, the chapter on metaphors, metonymies, etc., is. It considers not so much the form of the understanding as the form of systems, and is for the most part dialectical in content. It speaks only of different kinds of the form of the system. In art one employs manner, in science method; in the former I act according to examples, in the latter according to grounds. [The doctrine of method] contains the sum of all formal rules of the employment of the understanding.",[]],"wiener-s02-025":["There is a logic of common reason and a logic of speculative reason. We may have the capacity of knowing rules *in concreto*, but not all [of us] the capacity of knowing them *in abstracto*. The capacity for knowing rules *in concreto* is the common understanding, which is sound understanding in so far as it is correct. Knowledge of rules *in concreto*, that is, judging according to an experience or an example, is the go-cart of those who are not of age [Unmündigen]. It is necessary to know rules *in abstracto*, and to recognise how far these rules extend. Sound understanding serves us for thinking soundly and correctly. If it confines itself to what is grounded on experience, it will never overreach itself. But if one wishes to have not merely a sound but also a perfect understanding, one must possess a speculative understanding; for there is a great number of concepts which are too high for sound understanding, but which it nevertheless needs. The logic of speculative reason is therefore the capacity for having insight into rules *in abstracto*.",[]],"wiener-s02-026":["Exposition [Vortrag] in a science looks not so much to the intelligibility of the object as to the activity of the subject. In logic it can be of two kinds.",[]],"wiener-s02-027":["1.) Scholastic, when it is suited to the desire for knowledge, for the sake of culture, of those who wish to treat this knowledge as a science. A science requires proofs, explanations, etc.",[]],"wiener-s02-028":["2.) Popular, for the benefit of those who do not wish to make a science of it, but only to set their understanding in order. This is very beneficial to the commonwealth, but requires much genius, because popular logic belongs among the descendent modes of knowledge. The ancients had more popularity in their exposition, because they spoke before the people. Among the moderns the French have carried it furthest. Yet scholastic presentation is the foundation of the popular, since it is the foundation of all sciences; the latter is only, as it were, an ornament of it.",[]],"wiener-s03-001":["Logic comes from λόγος (*sermo*), and has the meaning of reason. Epicurus entitled it *Canonicon*, a science of the cautious and correct employment of the understanding. Aristotle may be regarded as the father of logic. His logic, however, is too scholastic, full of subtleties, and has at bottom been of little use to the human understanding. It is a dialectic, and an organon of the art of disputation. There is much acuteness in his *Organon*. From him come all our logical terminologies. For the rest, it smacks of mythology and hair-splitting, and has been banished from the schools; yet its chief ideas have been retained. This is because logic is occupied with no object, and can therefore soon be exhausted. His logic flourished in all the schools for many centuries, until Petrus Ramus first wrote a logic in two parts, of which",[]],"wiener-s03-002":["1. [the first] [treats] *de Inventione*,",[]],"wiener-s03-003":["2. [the second] treats *de Iudicio*.",[]],"wiener-s03-004":["Through his refutations of Aristotle he drew upon himself a deadly hatred. After them come Malebranche and Locke. The latter wrote a treatise *de intellectu humano*. But both works treat not so much of the form of the understanding as of its content; they are preliminary exercises for metaphysics. Among the moderns Leibniz and Wolff are to be noted. Wolff's logic is the best that is to be met with. It was afterwards condensed by Baumgarten, and Baumgarten was in turn expanded by Meier. After them Reusch and Knutzen wrote logics; Reusch is a philosopher at Jena. About this same time the *Récherches de la vérité* appeared in France. The logic of Crusius is crammed with things drawn from other sciences, and contains metaphysical and theological principles. Lambert has written an organon of pure reason. It is remarkable that of a science, even when it has almost reached its perfection, no precise definition can be given. The cause is that our reason can develop its ends, with regard to their truth, only late; but in the end a science thereby obtains its proper perfection. Since logic is a propaedeutic to all the philosophical sciences, the concept of philosophy must here be established. Modes of knowledge by reason from concepts are philosophical modes of knowledge; modes of knowledge by reason from the construction of concepts are mathematical. The system of the former is entitled philosophy, of the latter mathematics. Knowledge by reason [Vernunft-Erkenntniß] is opposed to historical knowledge. Knowledge is historical as regards its form when it is knowledge *ex datis*; knowledge by reason is knowledge *ex principiis*, drawn from *a priori* grounds.",[]],"wiener-s03-005":["A [mode of] knowledge may have arisen from reason, and yet the way in which I know it may be historical, namely, when I merely acquire it as it was given to me; for example, the polyhistor who studies the philosophy of the ancients. Here the knowledge is *objective* knowledge by reason, but *subjective* historical. To learn philosophy and to learn to philosophise are two different things. It is one of the greatest faults in instruction to have the systems of an author's philosophy learnt by heart without allowing [the pupil] to judge of the author. It is therefore necessary to employ more reason in the method of reason. Many philosophers thus boast of nothing but the imitation of another's reason.",[]],"wiener-s03-006":["It is commonly believed that mathematics and philosophy have two different objects, that philosophy is knowledge of quality and mathematics knowledge of quantity. We maintain that philosophy and mathematics, and the former especially, extend to all objects. Magnitudes are discussed in philosophy just as in mathematics. The specific difference between these two sciences is that philosophy is knowledge by reason from concepts, mathematics knowledge by reason from the construction of concepts. Construction from concepts is when I exhibit a concept *a priori* in intuition. Through philosophy from concepts nothing in mathematics can be understood; but in intuition one has insight *ex principiis* into the construction of this or that proposition. Mathematics therefore has a great advantage over philosophy, since all its modes of knowledge are intuitive, whereas those of philosophy are",[]],"wiener-s03-007":["discursive. The reason why in mathematics we always consider only magnitudes is that magnitudes alone can be constructed. Qualities cannot be constructed, but can be known only from concepts. The difference of mathematics rests, therefore, not so much on the objects as on the form. Philosophy is the system of philosophical knowledge. Here I omit 'speculative', for knowledge by reason under a system must be speculative throughout, since a system is from *principia a priori*. This is philosophy in the scholastic sense. But there is also a philosophy according to a *conceptus cosmicus*, and it is then a science of the ultimate ends of human reason. Speculative knowledge by reason is distinguished from common knowledge by reason in this, that the former is knowledge by reason according to rules *in abstracto*, the latter according to rules *in concreto*. Knowledge by reason according to rules *in concreto* is the popular mode of exposition, suited to the common man; if it is correct, it is sound reason. Philosophical knowledge is the speculative knowledge by reason -5 °from concepts; mathematical [modes of knowledge] are modes of knowledge by reason from the construction of concepts. I can exemplify [belegen] a concept, that is, give an intuition of a true concept, when I exemplify it *a posteriori*. The mathematician, however, exemplifies his concepts *a priori*. For example, if I have a circle that is not quite correct, I can still demonstrate; for the representation already lies in me. Scholastic philosophy is an instruction in skill; true philosophy is a doctrine of wisdom, which must be the highest good of our striving. It is the legislation of reason. Artifices of reason can be distinguished from the legislation of reason. The artist of reason, or the philodox, as Socrates calls him, is he who adapts reason to any ends whatever. The mathematician is an artist of reason, as are the logician and the physicist. In the end, however, one sees that philosophy as a doctrine of wisdom is necessary. Philosophy is therefore an idea of the most perfect legislation of the human understanding, and the philosopher is the one learned in the laws of human reason. Since this title is so exalted, no one can rightly let himself be called a philosopher. The reason why we hold in high esteem someone who orders his actions according to the strictest laws of morality, and never leaves the straight path, is perhaps that morals is, in the end, always the end at which all speculations aim. It constitutes a unity of the whole of knowledge by reason, and he alone who follows its rules can be called a philosopher. Whence comes the name philosophy? It was first ascribed to Pythagoras, not as a teacher of wisdom, but on account of the lofty concept which he had of the wisdom of the exalted God.",[]],"wiener-s03-008":["Two things belong to philosophy *in sensu scholastico*: 1.) a sufficient store of modes of knowledge by reason; 2.) a correct connection of them, or a system. For a system is the connection of many modes of knowledge according to one idea. Our historical modes of knowledge serve to enable our reason to make a purposive use of them. Since ends are subordinated to one another, there must be higher ends; and so there arises among these ends a unity, or a system of ends. The true worth of our employment of reason can be determined only through the connection which this knowledge has with the ultimate ends. It is therefore a science of wisdom. If we entitle the inner *principium* of the choice of different ends maxims, we may say that philosophy *in sensu cosmico* is a science of the highest maxims of the employment of our reason. And here the philosopher is marked out more by the maxim of his way of thinking than by the connection of his modes of knowledge. Philosophy in the scholastic concept (philodoxy) is an organon of skill; and the philodox stands to the philosopher as he who carries on a trade in the state stands to the legislator. If the philosopher is to know the connection of all modes of knowledge by reason with the ultimate ends, he must",[]],"wiener-s03-009":["1. [determine] the sources of human knowledge [Wissen],",[]],"wiener-s03-010":["2. [determine] the beginning of its employment,",[]],"wiener-s03-011":["3. determine its limits. This is one of the most difficult, but also one of the most sublime, things in philosophy, which as yet few have attained.",[]],"wiener-s03-012":["And philosophy *in sensu cosmico* is for this reason also entitled philosophy *in sensu eminenti*. Philosophy cannot be learned, because every philosopher erects his own edifice upon the ruins of another's. Even if a system were actually given me so clear that it contained none but incontestable propositions, I should still be no philosopher if I learnt all its propositions by heart. I should then not be learning to philosophise, but should possess a historical knowledge, without knowing the sources from which it had been drawn. One can say here just what can be said in jurisprudence: the *legis peritus* of the supreme laws of reason is the true philosopher. The *leguleii* of the supreme laws of reason are sophists and dialecticians, who give themselves a certain semblance of wisdom, and with it seek to force through certain ends.",[]],"wiener-s03-013":["Man requires two things in philosophy:",[]],"wiener-s03-014":["1. A culture of our skill. This is necessary because for all ends we require a facility in the use of all the means to those ends. Without knowledge one can as little be a philosopher as one becomes a philosopher through mere knowledge. The best principles of our way of thinking have no permanence unless a science of wisdom secures them. Innocence is lovable, but not secure against seduction, unless there is added instruction about everything that can allure, and about the origin of the errors into which it can fall. He who bears a hatred towards all sciences, and pretends that wisdom alone should be held in high esteem, is entitled a misologist. Epicurus is blamed for having wished to produce a doctrine of wisdom whereby all sciences would be abandoned and misology introduced. Misology commonly arises from a man's feeling himself empty of all such skills. But there is also a misology among those who have extensive knowledge, and here it arises because this knowledge had been unable to satisfy them. Philosophy alone can provide this inner satisfaction. It closes the circle, and then sees how all modes of knowledge hang together regularly in one edifice for such ends as are suited to humanity.",[]],"wiener-s03-015":["2. In regarding philosophy as skill, we shall look more to its method than to the purpose at which it is directed. We shall, indeed, turn our eyes also to the supreme maxims. But since through the method we are guided as to how we are to learn to philosophise, and since philosophy consequently sets the human mind in the greatest freedom, it deserves the greatest attention. No people in the world began to philosophise earlier than the Greeks, since no [other] people thought through concepts, but all through images. They were the first to begin studying rules *in abstracto*. What people has ever investigated the concepts of virtue, of the highest good? The Egyptian",[]],"wiener-s03-016":["wisdom is mere child's play compared with the Greek; and more recent authors have shown that the Egyptians indeed had many land-surveyors, who measured fields according to a certain accepted measure, but understood nothing of mathematics. The Greeks were the first originators of mathematics, who demonstrated it from first grounds and elements. They are the kernel of the human race, and its benefactors. In Greek history it is obscure where philosophy arose. The Thracians seem to have been the most discerning and the oldest. The fragmentists among them are Orpheus and Musaeus. The wise men of Greece had nothing but sentences and moral maxims, which had long been known among the people. Sentences are thoughts gathered into a familiar expression, in order to impress them more readily on the memory. One among them is noteworthy, and from him all philosophy dates: Thales, who on account of his knowledge of natural science is also entitled *physicus*. He is the founder of the Ionian sect; his pupils are Anaximander and Anaximenes. It is remarkable that the language of poetry was the first in which things that are objects of reason were expressed, so that philosophy was greatly hindered by poetry. Pherecydes was the first philosopher [to write] in prose, and soon after him Heraclitus. Their writings seemed very obscure to everyone, because philosophy was then still quite new. The language of poetry is astonishingly rich in ideas, as the works of Homer and Hesiod prove; but at that time ideas were not yet freed from their corporeal connotations. After these followed the Eleatic sect. Its founder was Xenophanes, and after him Parmenides. They taught that in the senses there is mere deception, and that truth is only in the understanding. Here philosophy already becomes much clearer, and frees itself entirely from the poets. Zeno, a man of great acuteness, belongs to this sect. – Under the name of dialectic was then understood the pure employment of the understanding; hence among the ancients there are great eulogies of dialectic. To it were reckoned the doctrine of the highest good, of the being that thinks in us, and so forth. When the understanding frees itself from the guiding thread of the senses, it runs the risk of losing its way in labyrinths. It is therefore comprehensible how such an employment of the understanding could have fallen into mere subtleties. From these arose the present-day name of dialectic, which pedants know well how to use. About the time of the Ionian sect Pythagoras established the Italian school in *Magna Graecia*. He is the only one among all the philosophers who had something peculiar to himself. He was a great mathematician, and knew how to bring music under mathematical laws. He founded a society of philosophers bound to one another by secrecy. Geometrical doctrines he expounded only to his friends. This philosophy spread through the whole country. The essential points which they taught were, it would seem, to give more moderate concepts of the gods than the people had, and to set something against the machinations of princes. It was in fact a kind of freemasonry. At length they came into such repute that all the cities requested their rulers from among the pupils of Pythagoras, who lived in Croton. – He believed in the transmigration of souls; but not much can be said of this, since he had nothing but secrets. – [He] played with numbers. About this time there arose in Greece a man who opened an entirely new course among speculative minds, as to how one is to attain the highest good: Socrates, whom Xenophon describes as one who comes nearest to the idea of a wise man. He possessed great skill in driving the dialecticians into a corner by questions and answers; this is called the irony of Socrates. His pupil was Plato, and Plato's pupil Aristotle. The former cultivated the practical part of the Socratic philosophy; the latter brought speculative philosophy to greater perfection. Now follow the moral philosophers. The Epicureans placed all virtue in an ever cheerful heart; the Stoics placed virtue in the renunciation of all the pleasures of life, and in loftiness of soul. Although the Epicureans taught many erroneous things, they were nevertheless the best natural philosophers among all the Greeks.",[]],"wiener-s03-017":["The principal schools6 * of the Greeks had special names. – Plato's Academy was not a single building, but an open place under the open sky near the most notable buildings. It divides into three parts.",[]],"wiener-s03-018":["1. the Academy of Speusippus, who still remained a faithful pupil of Plato, and taught quite dogmatically.",[]],"wiener-s03-019":["2: ......... [the Academy of] Arcesilaus. He was a mere doubter; hence it comes that the *Academici* are also called *Sceptici*. Plato expounded his doctrine in dialogues, and in such a way that he did not then decide, but let [the speakers] dispute on both sides. Now a method readily finds approval which suspends the matter and makes it probable that it could be otherwise.",[]],"wiener-s03-020":["3. ....... [the Academy] of Carneades, who was likewise an extremely subtle and dialectical doubter.",[]],"wiener-s03-021":["The Lyceum or Gymnasium was the school of Aristotle; and because he commonly walked about the place with his pupils, they are also called *peripatetici*. He had no famous pupils. Theophrastus and Demetrius Phaleraeus wrote no books, nor did they in any way extend Aristotle. The Porticus was a covered walk, a picture gallery, and the school of the sect of the Stoics. These were for the most part dialectical in speculative philosophy, but very practical in morals. Their founder was Zeno of Citium; his most famous pupils are Cleanthes and Chrysippus. This sect laid the foundation of the most magnanimous principles in the world, although its teachings are ideal and too lofty for practice.",[]],"wiener-s03-022":["*Horti*: the school of Epicurus. Its adherents disputed with the Stoics, and were in the end displaced by them, since everyone held such lofty principles in high esteem. The book of Lucretius, *de natura rerum*, is the only work written in accordance with their principles. Besides the *Sceptici academici*, the scepticism of Pyrrho is also noteworthy. ( H — —) The *Dogmatici* maintained that truth admits of exact proof. He, [Pyrrho,] maintained that philosophy consists in the equilibrium of our judgment, and in the skill of being able to expose all false illusion, without, however, being able to prove anything decisive. But since men are more fearful [of uncertainty] than they are eager to pull down, this sect found little favour. Sextus Empiricus is noteworthy because he collected all these doubts.",[]],"wiener-s03-023":["When the philosophy of the Greeks passed to the Romans, it made not the least advance. In speculative philosophy Cicero follows Plato; in practical philosophy, the Porch [the Stoa]. In natural science Pliny the Younger [*Plinius secundus*] alone accomplished anything of distinction.",[]],"wiener-s03-024":["When the Roman Empire had been overthrown by the barbarians, all culture vanished, until in the tenth century the Arabs spread into the West and began to cultivate the sciences. All philosophy was taken from Aristotle, who was followed in a slavish manner. In the tenth century there arose the scholastic school, which did nothing but compose commentaries upon Aristotle. Since, as is well known, Aristotle worked out nothing but speculative reason, men occupied themselves solely with the astonishing subtleties of logic and metaphysics. About the time of the Reformation the remnant of the Scholastics was swept away. Immediately after the Scholastics there arose, as *Sceptici*, Huet, Bayle and Hume, who were entitled *antilogici*. Then came the *eclectici*, that is, philosophers who adhered to no sect in particular. This improvement is to be ascribed to no circumstance more than to the study of nature, with which mathematics was combined. Order in thinking was thereby furthered. To this the Lord Chancellor Bacon of Verulam contributed in his *Organon* of the sciences, by directing attention to the method of physics, namely, to observations and experiments. Descartes was the first to enter upon this path. Guidance was given to speculative reason by Leibniz in Germany and by Locke in England, in that they sought to purify it of all scholasticism and to bring everything to distinct concepts. — No philosopher, perhaps, has ever possessed so extensive a skill in philosophising dogmatically as Leibniz. [Such philosophising] is dangerous, however, because, owing to the illusion of experience, there is much that is false in our dogmatic propositions. It is therefore better to subject truth to criticism. — Locke treats philosophy psychologically, that is, as an anatomist of the human faculties of knowledge. From his time onwards men in our fatherland began to study the human soul. In our own times natural philosophy flourishes in the highest degree. In morals we have not advanced much beyond the ancients. Some place [the principle of] morality in the will, others in self-love, etc. etc., as with the ancients. As regards metaphysics, we stand perplexed, after having gone through all its parts; and owing to its far-reaching difficulties a kind of indifferentism towards this study has set in among us. *Status anceps* [a doubtful state]. This is the age of criticism for this study, and the time is near when its edifice will be pulled down and an entirely new one erected upon the ruins of the old. For the rest, metaphysics alone is the true philosophy, and in it lie the genuine sources from which the understanding derives its employment of reason.",[]],"wiener-s04-001":["All our modes of knowledge can be considered in a twofold relation.",[]],"wiener-s04-002":["1. In relation to the object. This is the representation.",[]],"wiener-s04-003":["2. In relation to the subject. This is the consciousness of the representation.",[]],"wiener-s04-004":["Representation cannot be defined, because for that we should always require further representations. Every representation is either sensation or knowledge. It is something which has a relation to something in us. Sensations do indeed affect us, but they also soon pass away, because they are not modes of knowledge. For when I sense, I know nothing. Knowledge is of two kinds, either intuition or concept. The former is singular, the latter universal. For the concept belongs to all [that fall under it].",[]],"wiener-s04-005":["In all knowledge matter and form are to be distinguished. Matter signifies the object; form, the manner of knowing the object. In regard to the latter everything depends upon consciousness. A mode of knowledge of which I am conscious is entitled clear; if I am not conscious of it, it is entitled obscure. Consciousness is the authoritative condition of all logical form in our modes of knowledge. Obscure modes of knowledge are therefore not an object of logic, since no logical rule can help us in what is obscure to us.",[]],"wiener-s04-006":["All our clear representations can be logically divided into distinct and indistinct representations. An indistinct representation is the consciousness of a representation as a whole, without, however, distinguishing the manifold contained in the whole. Distinctness is clarity which extends also to the parts. All the Wolffians entitle an indistinct representation confused. — But the opposite of confusion is not distinctness, but order. Indistinctness is, indeed, an effect of confusion, not the confusion itself. There are also indistinct modes of knowledge which are indistinct not through confusion, but through deficiency of representation. The concept of something [*etwas*] is entirely simple; into it neither order nor confusion can be introduced. There is a twofold distinctness of modes of knowledge",[]],"wiener-s04-007":["1. A sensible distinctness in intuition, when we are conscious of the manifold contained in the intuition.",[]],"wiener-s04-008":["2. A distinctness of the understanding in concepts: when a mode of knowledge is developed not in respect of its content but in respect of its form, and the various parts of the representation that lie in the understanding are developed.",[]],"wiener-s04-009":["All our modes of knowledge are either intuitions or concepts. The faculty of intuition is sensibility. The faculty of concepts is the understanding, and to know something through concepts is entitled thinking. Intuition, however, refers only to something singular; the concept, to what several things have in common. From another point of view sensibility is explained as a *receptivitas*, a capacity of being affected by objects; the understanding as a spontaneity, a faculty of representing things as they are, not as they affect us. Sensibility is accordingly the lower faculty of knowledge, because sensibility supplies me with the material for thought, whereas the understanding disposes of this material. There is a twofold perfection of modes of knowledge.",[]],"wiener-s04-010":["1. Perfection according to the laws of sensibility: aesthetic [perfection].",[]],"wiener-s04-011":["2. Perfection according to the laws of the understanding: logical [perfection].",[]],"wiener-s04-012":["One of the aesthetic perfections in exposition is that a case *in concreto* supplies intuition to the rules *in abstracto* and renders them sensible. This perfection must not, however, be put in the place of the understanding, for the understanding would then be corrupted; it must be its companion. Concepts without sensibility have no object whatsoever. The condition of all our concepts lies ultimately always in the senses. Sensibility is therefore a very necessary element. And though it sometimes misleads the understanding, the fault properly lies with the understanding itself, for not examining it more carefully. A mode of knowledge is perfect according to the laws of sensibility when it is new, easy and lively; according to the laws of the understanding, when it is thorough.",[]],"wiener-s04-013":["Logical perfection rests on the agreement of knowledge with the object; aesthetic perfection, on its agreement with the subject. The rules of the agreement of knowledge with the object must be necessary, and must hold for all knowledge and for every understanding; for since my knowledge is to agree with the object, it must also agree with the knowledge of others. || V Aesthetic perfection rests on the particular laws of human sensibility, and is therefore not universal for all beings. But since objects are represented not only through concepts but also through intuition, there must also be necessary and universal laws of sensibility. Herein lies the concept of the beautiful. The ground of sensible satisfaction is indeed subjective, but subjective in respect of the whole of mankind; for example, music, symmetry. What agrees with the laws of the understanding, on the other hand, holds not merely for men but for all thinking beings. Modes of knowledge which are logically perfect can, however, at the same time possess an aesthetic perfection; for certain philosophical expositions are entitled beautiful.",[]],"wiener-s04-014":["Among all the properties of aesthetic perfection none is more in keeping with logical perfection, and none more essential to aesthetic perfection, than intuition. It is the form of sensibility; whereas sensation and charm never yield such universal laws. — Beauty, distinctness and universality are three essential elements of logical perfection. He who would convince his hearers must not arouse their sensations, but must present the matter to their intuition.",[]],"wiener-s04-015":["Sensations, for example charm and emotions, are the matter of sensibility; intuition is its form. Imagination therefore concerns only the form of sensibility, not its matter. Essential aesthetic perfection is the opposite of logical perfection. Distinctness of intuition is nothing more than liveliness in aesthetic truth. [In aesthetic truth] the chief point is not that the knowledge agree with the object, provided only that it agree with our laws of sensibility. What is poetically true is not what is logically true, but what accords with appearance [*Schein*]. Common prejudices possess aesthetic truth.",[]],"wiener-s04-016":["The universality of concepts is the opposite of what belongs to aesthetic perfection. In the latter, things are to be represented universally *in concreto*; in the former, universally *in abstracto*. Aesthetic perfection, when it is favourable to logical perfection, contains the essentially beautiful; when it is detrimental to it, the extra-essentially beautiful. What belongs to sensation, and not to intuition, contributes nothing to concepts. What a man feels he cannot even relate, unless the other feels it with him; this therefore belongs to the extra-essentially beautiful. Charm belongs to the agreeable; it often mingles with the judgment of taste. Thus we love woman for her delicate build, which yet, according to universal rules of taste, is an imperfection as compared with the strong build of men. Emotions belong to the sublime, but are very deceptive. The preacher must therefore not seek to attain the end of his sermon by means of them, but through distinctness, truth and universality. For sensations are at all times contrary to logical perfection. When the matter moves us, it hinders thorough inquiry, and the judge can be corrupted by tears. Agitations of the mind do harm, inasmuch as they often promote the very opposite of the end proposed. The preacher must therefore make use of intuition, and speak in images and similes, whereby an object is presented to us, but without any consciousness of the alterations in our own state being combined therewith. || V Logical perfection is the *conditio sine qua non* and the *basis* of all thinking. Aesthetic perfection cannot subsist by itself, but is only the adornment of a logical perfection already correct. Something may indeed be relaxed in logical perfection, but it must not be mutilated. One may also, as it were, ignore something of the truth, in that one cannot make it wholly universal, without in other respects allowing logical perfection to be in the least deficient. Poetry is the first to be reckoned under aesthetic perfection. In poetry logical perfection is less in degree than aesthetic perfection, and every aesthetic perfection at all times contends with logical perfection for a place. But the need of human nature requires that sensibility and understanding go together in pairs, and the greatest scholar cannot free himself from all sensibility. Hence arises popularity, or the condescension of the understanding, when it relaxes something of scholastically correct knowledge in order to become more comprehensible. Only, in the case of aesthetic sensations, we must take care that our attention is not diverted from the object to ourselves as subjects. Gentle emotions can indeed give occasion for further reflection, but they are not sufficient for conviction.",[]],"wiener-s04-017":["|| V Our intuition is enriched by history, which supplies us with cases in which what the concept states *in abstracto* has been presented *in concreto*; further, by observations from common life, for which good guidance is given in Spalding's sermons, provided only that they are not shouted down by sensations. — Of late there has been much talk of sensations. But he who talks much of feeling cannot think; whereas everyone can feel.",[]],"wiener-s04-018":["From what has been said there follows a union of the [ Textlücke: AA: Ästhetischen ] [aesthetic] and the [ Textlücke: AA: Logischen ] [logical] in modes of knowledge. For a mode of knowledge can be thorough and logically perfect, but at the same time dry; it can be beautiful, but at the same time shallow. Modes of knowledge whose end is to instruct may be dry, and in that case they must be thorough. Others are meant only to entertain, and these may well be shallow. Some are to contain both; and to accomplish this much genius is required. For the understanding demands insight; sensibility, ease. Between the two there is accordingly a kind of conflict, in which one must make the choice that comes nearest to the end and best satisfies it; for example, || V mathematics can acquire elegance of taste only late, since everything in it must be expounded convincingly.",[]],"wiener-s04-019":["The perfection of modes of knowledge in general is",[]],"wiener-s04-020":["1. Logical.",[]],"wiener-s04-021":["2. Aesthetic.",[]],"wiener-s04-022":["3. Practical perfection.",[]],"wiener-s04-023":["|| V Logical perfection concerns the understanding, and is the knowledge of objects through the understanding. Aesthetic perfection concerns feeling and the state of our subject, namely, how we are affected by the object. For through the beauty of knowledge I seek knowledge not of the object but of the subject. Practical perfection concerns our desires, whereby activity is brought about.",[]],"wiener-s04-024":["The perfection of a mode of knowledge rests on four principal points.",[]],"wiener-s04-025":["1. On the quantity of the knowledge, in that it is universal. A mode of knowledge that serves as a rule must be more perfect than one that holds only in particular cases.",[]],"wiener-s04-026":["Logical perfection in respect of quantity, or in respect of judgment, is universality; that of aesthetic perfection, general validity [*Gemeingültigkeit*].",[]],"wiener-s04-027":["2. ... Quality: the distinctness of the knowledge. It contains the *quomodo*? Logical perfection in respect of quality is distinctness; that of aesthetic perfection, liveliness.",[]],"wiener-s04-028":["3. ... Relation: the truth of the knowledge. Truth is the relation of knowledge to the object. || V If a mode of knowledge passes over into emotions, the truth concerns only my subject, and is aesthetic truth. Logical perfection in respect of relation is objective truth; that of aesthetic perfection, subjective truth.",[]],"wiener-s04-029":["4. ... Modality, in so far as it is certain and necessary knowledge. Logical perfection in respect of modality is the necessity of modes of knowledge according to the understanding; that of aesthetic perfection, empirical necessity – –",[]],"wiener-s04-030":["|| V In aesthetic perfection we can think",[]],"wiener-s04-031":["1. Subjective truth. It is the agreement of knowledge with the subject's way of thinking. Ghosts, apparitions and the like are aesthetically true. The sun, etc., has aesthetic truth; logical truth is here not demanded at all. The poet requires only aesthetic truth, [things] as they present themselves to our senses and seem to be. The sun dips into the sea, says the poet. Were he to say that the earth turns upon its axis, he would be conforming himself to logical truth, and would be no poet.",[]],"wiener-s04-032":["2. Subjective distinctness (*relatione*) in intuition, when I establish the rules of concepts through examples: description [*Schilderung*]. If I wish to know something according to aesthetic perfection in respect of liveliness of intuition, it must be very lively. If, for instance, someone wishes to know spring logically, he judges according to quality, and through a single mark has everything distinct. The poet, on the other hand, here speaks of a multitude of things, of the twittering of birds and the like. Yet it is very lively.",[]],"wiener-s04-033":["3. Aesthetic universality, that is, *popularity*: that a mode of knowledge be suited to the *sens commun*. I must presuppose that the way in which I judge is the way in which everyone else can judge. Here, therefore, there is subjective ( H — —).",[]],"wiener-s04-034":["4. Necessity and certainty of aesthetic perfection: that a mode of knowledge of the senses be necessary, that is, that experience and the voices of all men confirm it. Subjective necessity is custom.",[]],"wiener-s04-035":["|| V Manifoldness and unity constitute every perfection. Our faculty of knowledge strives greatly after manifoldness; but it has the need of possessing unity. Otherwise it would not satisfy us, since knowledge without unity, in which one thing is not connected with another, does not increase what we know. In aesthetic perfection, for instance, the painter must know how to group the manifold of his figures so that a unity results, if his painting is to please. Truth is the pre-eminent ground of unity, and the most necessary and most excellent element. Without truth no knowledge whatsoever is possible. In logical perfection it is the greatest positive condition. In aesthetic perfection it is the *conditio sine qua non* and the chief negative condition, since there it is not the principal end; that end consists in agreeableness and agreement with sensibility. But since no satisfaction can arise unless the understanding comes in and exposes errors, no contradictions can coexist with aesthetic perfection. No one, therefore, can make progress in matters of taste unless he has laid logical perfection at the foundation. Most of the artists of taste in Germany are extremely distasteful when one reads the products of foreign peoples, who have not studied merely belles-lettres. True aesthetic perfection is to be found in the *Spectateur*, in Sulzer and in Wieland, of whom one can see that their heads are full of ideas, and that they add every device for persuading the mind, so as then to accommodate themselves to the ease that taste demands. Certain books have nothing attractive about them, however thorough they may be, because their authors never thought of giving their exposition a certain verve and enlivening it. — Logical perfection is the skeleton of our modes of knowledge. Hence he who has learned something sound can easily relax somewhat of logical strictness and introduce beauty. — Novels are figments of the brain, which overstep the course of things and make the heart wither. History, geography, the reading of the ancients, who unite both perfections, and anthropology must be our teachers, and make the mind more alert. No science can be beautiful. || V For it contains universal rules, which precede practice. Feelings can be combined with the beautiful; they can please and move. But they contribute nothing to the play of our representations as faculties of knowledge, though they do so as sensation. Sensations, however, are not so universally communicable as representations and concepts. Charms and feelings belong to the essentially beautiful somewhat as a gilt frame belongs to a beautiful picture.",[]],"wiener-s04-036":["|| V The beautiful, however, must be judged rather from its effect upon sensibility than from the understanding, since beauty is agreement with sensibility, whereas the understanding alone is the faculty of rules. Taste, therefore, cannot be brought under laws. For a law serves not only for judging but also for compliance. The rules of taste are empirical; it is not they, however, that make our judgment true; they serve only to bring our judgment, when it has been cultivated through much practice, under certain concepts. Taste can therefore in no way be treated as a science; hence there are also no beautiful sciences. The fine arts are arts which proceed not according to logical rules but through empirical trials. For the effect alone distinguishes the rules of sensibility, which no rule of the understanding recognises; and for this reason we cannot have rules of taste. In aesthetics, accordingly, there can be no canon. The trials of the fine arts always come first, and then follow the rules, which serve only for criticising the art. We must therefore make ourselves acquainted with the models of beauty, in order [ *doch nicht*: sic ] to acquire taste. For in matters of taste man follows fashion and is inclined to please. A man has taste when what pleases him pleases all. And so the social inclination of all has produced taste and modified it. Now the ancients have withstood the criticism of many centuries, and thereby retain their authority; and whoever reads them, and on whom they leave an impression, acquires taste, provided only he does not seek to imitate them. — || V Taste is an effect of the power of judgment. This comes later than wit, and even later than understanding. It shows how we can make use of the judgment of sensibility; consequently, in order to choose, one must have gathered a multitude of modes of knowledge. Our knowledge requires [ein ] certain [ Textlücke: AA: -s Mittel ] [medium], and that is language. This [ Textlücke: AA: Mittel] [medium] is, however, subject to many changes. One must therefore write in a dead language. || V Forced constraint and grimace in the beautiful displease in the highest degree. Better is natural understanding in its simplicity. || V We can occupy ourselves in two ways",[]],"wiener-s04-037":["1. *per otium* [by way of leisure]; 2. *per negotium* [by way of business].",[]],"wiener-s04-038":["All occupation of taste is play; occupation of the understanding is true business. Regarded as play, the former deserve every approval. But when the play seems to be a business, it is overdone; for example, when someone in his speech hunts after wit, his play becomes a business. In no language are there two words which are really *synonyma*. Least of all are philosophy and learned knowledge synonymous, as our author would have it; they are worlds apart. A science is a system. Thereby it is distinguished from common knowledge, whatever it may be in respect of its object. Naturalists in the field of learning are those who lack the systematic and possess nothing but *rhapsody*. In oral exposition the system can be better grasped than from books, since here there is not, as there, argument about it, but one proceeds along a certain thread.",[]],"wiener-s04-039":["A science can be:",[]],"wiener-s04-040":["1. A science of reason, which can be known only according to universal *principia* of reason; for example, mathematics.",[]],"wiener-s04-041":["2. A science of erudition, which can be learned historically; such as histories, languages, philology, the knowledge of all the instruments of erudition, literature.",[]],"wiener-s04-042":["The philosopher is not a man of erudition; he merely considers to what use all erudition is in the end to be put. He must, however, possess erudition in order to be able to make use of philosophy. He is therefore not an artificer of reason, but one who studies the laws of reason. To become learned is entitled imitating another in what he knows; consequently the pupil can never become more learned than his teacher. Mathematics can be learned; but many new things can still be discovered from it. Philosophy, however, cannot be learned; only philosophising [can be learned], since we expound the judgment upon the matter rather than the matter itself. He who begins to learn philosophy by heart removes himself ever further from it. For there lie in man judgments which, if they have not been infected by adopted propositions, are clear and pure.",[]],"wiener-s04-043":["Our author divides imperfection into *defectus* and *vitia*.",[]],"wiener-s04-044":["In metaphysics we have",[]],"wiener-s04-045":["1. *defectio privative dicta* (*defectus*), which is blameworthy only in slight measure, namely when dryness, but otherwise thoroughness, prevails in a treatise. Lack is the non-being of a mode of knowledge. — Ignorance is lack of knowledge.",[]],"wiener-s04-046":["2. *defectio contrarie dicta* (*vitia*), when, for example, *vitia grammaticalia* and affected things occur in a letter. There are logical essential [ Textlücke: AA: und ] [and] extra-essential *vitia*. In our language many contradictions are current: I went out alone with another. The English call this a bull. Such a fault belongs to the essential *vitia*.",[]],"wiener-s04-047":["Error is a *vitium* of judgment. Since logic expounds the universal formal rules, and abstracts from every object, aesthetic and practical perfections cannot properly belong to it; they are regarded as episodic elements not peculiar to logic. Practical knowledge properly bears upon our will. It is, however, taken in so that we may thereby the better know logical knowledge and distinguish it from the other modes of knowledge. Practical perfection is the determination of the ends of human actions; aesthetic perfection is *popularity*. The horizon is the congruence of the limits of our knowledge with the ends of humanity and of men. It is therefore a sum of the modes of knowledge which, taken together, are adequate to our ends. Now we can consider either the ends of humanity as a whole, that is, the absolute horizon, or the particular horizon, that is, the horizon of a [single] man, which is consequently a relatively determined horizon. As regards the particular horizon, the horizon of an [ Textlücke: AA: Ungebildeten ] [uncultivated man] is other than that of the connoisseur. The former learns sciences without proposing to himself any definite end in their application, at most so as to be able to join in conversation in company; the latter, so as thereby to become useful to others. The horizon of a religion is a catechism, that is, a sum of the modes of knowledge necessary to man as man. To determine the knowledge proper to all persons, and the *nexus* of all useful modes of knowledge with one another, presupposes, however, a cultivated understanding, and further a power of judgment matured by years and experience. — || V To determine logically the horizon of universal human knowledge is not everywhere possible. In metaphysics the limits of human knowledge can in many respects be discerned; in natural science, however, this is not feasible, owing to the great extent of that study. It is possible, however, that it may some day be done. In the aesthetic determination of the universal horizon, everything depends on how the aesthetic stands in relation to its neighbour, the logical. The logical requires distinct truth and certainty, which need not be found with the same exactness in the aesthetic, if the latter is to be generally useful. We must, however, look more to the logical, and at the least the measure between the two must be exactly determined. Some things I cannot know: that is above my horizon. Other things I may not know: that is outside my horizon. For example, the physician, as physician, need not know all legal procedures. But what is useful to know, and what can I dispense with? On this, Gesner, in his lectures on his *Isagoge*, commenting upon the frivolous questioning of those who ask of everything, What is the use of that? What is the use of my knowing the ancient dress of the Hebrews, H of my knowing the [ Textlücke ] and their nature? expresses himself thus: *quaerit delirus quod non respondet Homerus*. What is not useful now may be useful hereafter; as, for example, when a new law of nature is given through an experiment. Only extend and correct your knowledge aright, and the knowledge of its use will follow afterwards. This answer suffices in determining the universal horizon of men. For even he who collects the absurdities of men, and so, of all human endeavours, produces the least use, setting up as it were only an inventory of the rubbish in the world, can yet in respect of history afford some small use.",[]],"wiener-s04-048":["The horizon is the concept of [the sum of] our modes of knowledge which, taken together, are adequate to the ends that we have. It is",[]],"wiener-s04-049":["|| V 1. Logical: in relation to our faculty of knowledge, how far we can advance and how far we must go. Everything must be directed towards perfection. Hence we may go as far as we can reach.",[]],"wiener-s04-050":["2. Aesthetic: the horizon in relation to taste. This is *popularity*. One seeks to win the approval either of the *public* or of connoisseurs. In the former case one seeks current erudition, which is suited to and pleases everyone. In this way, however, one falls into shallowness, as is the case with the French.",[]],"wiener-s04-051":["3. Practical: in relation to utility. But in respect of particular ends we must always ask after the more excellent purpose, and have only a universal and determinate ground for doing this or that. For example, if the theologian wishes to appear as a man of learning, theological erudition is the greatest of all. He must study natural theology well, in order to meet the freethinkers; further, *physico-theology*, on account of the difficult *eventus* in the Holy Scriptures; mathematics, *theologia sacra*, etc. etc. But as preacher and teacher of his congregation he need only have clear ideas of the truths he expounds, in order to discern their importance. The expression ‘above the horizon’ is not quite suited to the matter; for what is at the horizon of the sky I can, after all, see. Plainly it is meant to represent what lies outside the plane in which all possible modes of knowledge are contained. Historically, human knowledge has no limits; for experience continually extends it. The limits of mathematics cannot be determined, since new things are always being added to it. But the horizon of the knowledge of physics in general is very restricted, because it is occupied solely with objects of sensibility. A horizon can be assigned in respect of how far our reason can depart from all experience. Below our horizon is what we ought not to know. This can be said *relative*. For example, when someone brings religious controversies into the pulpit, the public gets into its head things it ought not to know. The most important employment of this doctrine of the horizon is the relative one. Relatively I can always say: this is above this man's horizon, [even] if he is cultivated. Yet one may err herein, if one does not proceed with due care. In the extension of his horizon man stands, as it were, upon a mountain, and describes a greater radius of his circle. But beware of ceaselessly altering your horizon and adopting another centre; for all coherence of your modes of knowledge will thereby fall away. We must therefore have a definite centre, upon which all our modes of knowledge converge. And this must be done in the first years of study. Moreover, one must enrich one's understanding with historical modes of knowledge, if one wishes to gather a store for the whole of life. For only at that time is memory capable of taking in things which will never be extinguished. With the growth of the power of judgment, however, the sharpness of memory ceases. We must further know how to determine the horizon of our modes of knowledge duly at the outset. But as a rule men attain this insight too late, only when they see where it ought to have been determined. And this as a rule arises more from the negligence and amusements of youth than from ignorance. The horizon of other men can be determined according to the diversity of their modes of knowledge. In instruction the exposition must be arranged according to what my pupil takes an interest in at his present age; at a later time, as a youth, he has again another interest; and so I must always take in the horizon of my pupil. Nor must I make it too narrow, and, for example, in the pulpit trust too little to the comprehension",[]],"wiener-s04-052":["of common people. Sciences have a horizon. This is the *territorium* of the science; hence much does not belong within the *territorium* of a science which it is nevertheless necessary to know in connection with that science.",[]],"wiener-s04-053":["The imperfection of our knowledge is",[]],"wiener-s04-054":["1. Ignorance: the imperfection of lack, which thus constitutes an empty space.",[]],"wiener-s04-055":["2. Error: an imperfection of enrichment, when I have gathered ideas that are contrary to the truth.",[]],"wiener-s04-056":["The lot of one who errs is therefore worse than that of one who is ignorant, for he bars the entry of truth. To remove his error, the man must first be reduced to ignorance. Error is thus a twofold imperfection: it is not merely a negative lack of truth, but also a cause that resists perfection. In all knowledge [Wissenschaft] there is danger. Nevertheless we must venture it at the risk of error, since otherwise no advance could ever be made. Two things give rise to error: ignorance, and the desire to know, which is what properly produces it. Ignorance is no great reproach where opportunity to know certain things has been lacking. The name 'idiot' is meant to signify an ignorant man, just as the ancient sophists distinguished the wise from idiots, that is, dullards. But the pride with which an idiot is regarded is ill-founded. The name belongs not to the ignorant man, but to him who presumes to judge of all things and yet knows nothing. It has been remarked in ordinary life that it is very useful that one man should not make the whole of a thing. An ignoramus is one who has not so much knowledge as he requires in respect of this or that end. For example, in the making of a pin more than four to ten men take part. This division makes everything perfect and everything easy. So also is it with knowledge [Kenntnisse]. One man finds pleasure in rummaging through archives; another holds this to be empty. Ignorance is therefore no reproach, since there is much that we partly need not know and partly cannot know. Knowledge of one's own ignorance is itself a science, for it requires knowledge of the empty space in which possible knowledge may be met with. The common man is ignorant in respect of astronomy, but he does not know his own ignorance. It is therefore very necessary to lay bare to man the abyss of his ignorance; and only those can do this who have extended their knowledge, and therefore perceive how little it is. Helvétius says that nowadays everyone knows everything except what Socrates knew, namely, nothing. Opposed to this ignorance is much-knowing [das Vielwissen].",[]],"wiener-s04-057":["1. Historical *polyhistoria*: erudition extended within determinate limits.",[]],"wiener-s04-058":["2. The knowledge of reason, extended without determinate limits: *polymathia*. Both together may be entitled *pansophia*.",[]],"wiener-s04-059":["In the sciences, then, there is a distinction between those which can be drawn out of reason and those which cannot be learned from it, such as geography and the like. In the past century the inclination of most men was towards *polyhistoria*. For this philology is further required, that is, knowledge of the instruments of the ancients, in which the models of historical knowledge have been transmitted to us. Philology, under which the linguist and the humanist are comprised, together with the *litterator*, make up polyhistory. The philologist must be a *litterator*; he must be a humanist, that is, he must form his taste and be able to communicate it to other men. For the formation of his taste he takes the classical authors, that is, those who are models and exemplary. A linguist is one who studies the ancient languages critically; and if he chooses them as models of taste, he is entitled a humanist. The ancients alone will always remain models of genuine taste, because their languages are dead languages. The *litterator*, again, is one who can name many books of the ancients, their editions and their authors. Whoever knows many books is a *litterator*; whoever knows many languages is a linguist; and the two together make up the great scholar. This is the much-knowing of which Paul says that it puffeth up. For when the polyhistor has acquaintance with so many books, he believes that he knows as much as those who wrote them, although he knows them only historically. Philosophy can humble this pride and set before him his true ends. Erudition without philosophy is a Cyclopean erudition. Philosophy is the second eye, which sees how the knowledge gathered by the one eye accords with the collective end. || V A part of philosophy is entitled *Humaniora*. The character of humanity consists in sociability. The *Humaniora* are liberal arts. Liberal art is mere play; bread-winning art is labour. What belongs to fine art is, however, not yet wholly free, for painters and the like are still always mechanical. Poetry and eloquence are freer. A humanist is one who treats of the beautiful and cultivates his mind in the study of the ancients, in order to drive out its wildness. From this arises that urbanity which we perceive in the ancients; and in this respect, that it drives out rusticity, historical knowledge is of importance.",[]],"wiener-s04-060":["All these modes of knowledge [Kenntnisse] can be gathered together under one principal science, and are regarded as means to it, as also, perhaps, to the bread-winning science which one studies. It is usual among men to do a thing from inclination rather than under the constraint of duty. Many a man's inclinations often fail him and do not carry him far. This is a man's hobby-horse, which he would rather ride than the cart-horse of his office. Since, then, duty is something from which you gladly free yourselves, learn something with which you can play rationally, for example, the study of botany, of geography, and so forth.",[]],"wiener-s04-061":["These subsidiary sciences must be thought out in accordance with the plan of our knowledge. Often they are the necessary means for a science. For example, a physician must have natural science as an aid, and philology as well, because wide reading supplies a multitude of experiences. Often, however, they are merely a play of entertainment, and are left to each man's pleasure. Among men of learning there is a distinction between bread-winning art and liberal art; and accordingly scholars are either guild scholars [Zunftgelehrte] or scholars free of the guild [zunftfreye Gelehrte].",[]],"wiener-s04-062":["The scholar is often contrasted with those who practise mechanical arts. The scholar is thus taken to be one who plies no handicraft. So in China the scholars let the nails of their hands grow, in proof that they do no manual work. A science is a bread-winning art when it is a means of livelihood; if it is meant merely to interest the mind and to civilise the talents, it is a liberal art. Bread-winning arts are therefore great incentives to activity. But the sciences are much restricted by them, since one must there conform to general opinion [Wahn]: even when truths are of no use whatsoever, one must still expound them, because the public has grown accustomed to them. Thus much might be struck out of logic which has once been received and comes up in examinations. In short, guild scholars are bound to their trade [*métier*]. Liberal art has here many advantages, in that it is not restricted to the conditions of a trade, and the question is not at all about the opinion of the multitude, but about the inner content of the sciences. On the other hand, it has not incentive enough to compel men to apply themselves to it with great effort. Faults in the use of our knowledge are galantry and pedantry. The word 'pedant' is Italian, and has its origin in the *magister pedarius*, who was set over a young man as supervisor and followed him at every step. He had to instruct him in the sciences and in school learning, but was never drawn into the society of the young man's father; hence he never learned to make use of his knowledge in intercourse with other men.",[]],"wiener-s04-063":["In its use, our knowledge may be suited either more to the school or more to the world. The school contains precepts and methods, that is, means of teaching and learning. Knowledge is therefore scholastically correct [schulgerecht] when it contains all the forms according to which one is to teach. Every science has two perfections, a scholastic and a popular.",[]],"wiener-s04-064":["Scholastic perfection, then, consists in the didactic form, which serves to make knowledge easier for a beginner. It constitutes the essence and the ground; it is the agreement [of our knowledge] with the use of our reason, and punctiliousness in [observing] the logical rules. It is, therefore, the exposition [Vortrag] which can be either scholastically correct or popular. Popularity consists in the accommodation of a science to the comprehension and taste of the commonalty. Such knowledge is not merely for the school, but also for the world. –",[]],"wiener-s04-065":["The scholastic method habituates itself to certain painstaking formalities, such as *termini technici*, demonstrations, definitions, which are not suited to a dilettante. Nevertheless the scholastic cut is the greatest perfection, and always comes first, since it contains the determinate rules of our procedure; without it all thoroughness would fall away. Scholastic perfection must go before, and popularity follow; for to begin with popularity is very absurd. If, however, we devote our knowledge to human society, we must leave out these rules, and yet retain as much of the science as is suited to the comprehension and inclination of others. Popularity therefore requires knowledge of the world and intercourse with men; and he certainly understands his subject best who can make it intelligible and clear to another who is unlearned. Only, in popularity we must not abandon thoroughness, since it is nothing but a change in the manner of exposition. – Pedantry, now, is a restricted capacity in the use of knowledge, when this use is confined to the school alone. It is reckoned pedantry when someone, in dealing with abstract concepts, begins with Adam. Yet it does indicate a certain thoroughness. Painstaking punctiliousness in what is formal, micrology, petty-mindedness [Kleinigkeitskrämerey], is pedantry. The pedant is a poor copy of the methodical mind, which sets before itself the right end for its knowledge and knows also how to attain it therewith. The pedant, on the other hand, never reaches his end with all his great learning. There are pedants in all walks of life, for example, hunting pedants, military pedants, pedants of dress and of politeness, who are always talking of their own concern and busied with it. The learned pedant is the most tolerable. For he is open only to the reproach of lacking knowledge of the world; for the rest, one can learn from him, even though one finds no pleasure in doing so. Pedants in other fields, however, are empty heads. Besides pedantry, galantry may also attach to knowledge. He is galant who, from the superiority of his refinement, concedes to another merits to which that other himself makes no claim. || V Among the ancients the concept of galantry was wholly unknown. Horace (*Carmina*, Book II, Ode 16, *ad Grosphum*), when praising his patron, ascribes to him the advantages of possessions and to himself the advantages of mind, and thus plainly sets himself above him. In general there is not to be found among them the least delicacy in sparing others. – No people has more galantry in its style of writing than France. || V Most books there are accommodated to the taste of the fair sex, and in consequence women are there also judges of scientific matters. They must, indeed, give the tone of conduct in social intercourse; but to water down whole sciences for their sake until they are suited to women's capacity produces shallowness, and scholastic perfection thereby loses its substance. || V The Germans come to grief, and show something constrained in their manner, when they seek to equal the French in galantry. This galantry is dangerous, because it has about it something charming and seductive: since the whole public cheers us with applause, we come to value this more highly than the censure of learned men.",[]],"wiener-s04-066":["The magnitude of learned knowledge is either extensive, according to the quantity of knowledge, or intensive, according to its importance, that is, in respect of its consequences and effects. This importance may be considered",[]],"wiener-s04-067":["1.) in a logical regard, since it is that which contains logical perfection, namely, that whereby modes of knowledge become more thorough, more distinct and more certain;",[]],"wiener-s04-068":["2.) its practical importance consists in the utility of the knowledge. This cannot be foreseen, and must therefore be called forth. Logical perfection teaches the practical, in that utility is thereby more determined. It is therefore the first perfection. Yet a mode of knowledge may have logical perfection wholly in a relative sense [*respective*].",[]],"wiener-s04-069":["Fruitfulness of knowledge concerns the magnitude and quantity of knowledge. The content of knowledge lies in the manifold, and indicates its parts. It belongs to the perfection of knowledge that it be true. Truth is the agreement of knowledge with the object. But this is properly only a nominal definition [Worterklärung]; and it is one of the strangest phenomena in a science when one cannot answer a question which constitutes its essence. Such a question has long been posed in logic, namely, What is truth? Pilate was the first to raise it. Here logic seems to founder at the very name, although it would give the understanding rules of truth. The above definition does not fit. For the object is not with us, but only the knowledge of it. Hence we cannot compare our knowledge of it with the object, but only with our knowledge. Since, then, no knowledge is true save that which agrees with the object, no knowledge of the object is true save that which agrees with our knowledge of the object. The ancients called this *diallele*, a fallacy; as, for example, when someone, in order to vouch for his honesty, should appeal to the honesty of another. Thus Lavater has asserted much on the basis of inferences into eternity, and has spoken of the constitution of the future life in a way which has not the least appearance of incorrectness and contradiction. But has this knowledge an object agreeing with it? Such modes of knowledge are then entitled empty. So, [it is said,] knowledge is true when it agrees with itself. But in that way all lies could be true, if nothing further were needed for confirmation than the — [AA: der Übereinstimmung ]",[]],"wiener-s04-070":["[agreement] of the knowledge [AA: mit sich ] [with] itself. – Is there, then, a universal criterion of truth, a rule whereby universal truth can be distinguished? It is impossible. For it would have to be a universal criterion of truth without distinction of objects; were it a criterion only of certain objects, it would not be universal. But it must abstract from all distinction of objects; and all truth is the agreement of knowledge with the object, whereby the knowledge of one object is distinguished from that of another; while the knowledge of universality has no mark whereby an object is distinguished. A material criterion of truth is therefore impossible. The matter of all knowledge is the object; the form is the manner of treatment. Logic deals with the form, truth with the object. Hence it is, in the first place, impossible and self-contradictory to give a universal material criterion of truth. But since logic abstracts from all content, there can only be formal criteria of truth. Two things belong to truth.",[]],"wiener-s04-071":["1.) Agreement of the knowledge with the object.",[]],"wiener-s04-072":["2.) Agreement with itself.",[]],"wiener-s04-073":["The agreement of knowledge with itself, irrespective of objects, is a formal criterion of truth. For it is this that makes knowledge possible as knowledge; without it no knowledge whatsoever is possible. It is therefore always indispensable, although it is not sufficient for a material criterion. It is a *conditio sine qua non*; the principle of contradiction, for example, is a formal criterion of truth. But for one who expounds a logic so subtly that it agrees with itself, this criterion does not suffice. The answer to the question, What is truth?, would thus be: the agreement of a mode of knowledge with itself; and this is one ground for holding it to be true. A universally sufficient criterion of truth cannot be demanded. – If I abstract from all the various objects, I retain only a concept of an object [in general]. A universal criterion of truth deals only with the form of thought, which is the agreement of knowledge not with the object, but with itself.",[]],"wiener-s04-074":["The rules of agreement with itself are",[]],"wiener-s04-075":["1.) A mode of knowledge must not contradict itself. This is the *conditio sine qua non*.",[]],"wiener-s04-076":["2.) *Affirmatio*: when modes of knowledge are connected as grounds with their consequences. If, for example, I adduce as the ground of the wetness of the west winds that they come over the sea, this is one ground of truth. It belongs to the inner criterion of truth that a mode of knowledge be grounded. A criterion which rests on the *principium* of sufficient ground must always infer from the ground to the consequence.",[]],"wiener-s04-077":["3.) Between which modes of knowledge there is to be unity, that is, the relation of many modes of knowledge to one.",[]],"wiener-s04-078":["Opposed to truth is falsity. When falsity is taken for truth, it is error.",[]],"wiener-s04-079":["1.) Falsity, that is, a lack of agreement with the object.",[]],"wiener-s04-080":["2.) Semblance [Schein] of truth.",[]],"wiener-s04-081":["Is truth possible? Truth in logic is an agreement of knowledge with the laws of the understanding. Since the understanding here acts according to its own laws, there is nothing contradictory here which should prevent truth from being possible. Is error possible? That is, how far is a form of our thinking that runs counter to the understanding possible? It is hard to comprehend how a power should deviate from its own laws, since it acts only according to certain laws. If these are essential, it cannot deviate from them; and so, if among the formal laws of the understanding which logic expounds an essential one is possible, the understanding cannot deviate from it. A heavy body, for example, cannot cease to fall. – If we had no other sources of knowledge than the understanding, and were nothing but understanding, we should suppose that the understanding would always judge only according to its laws, even though it might at times judge in a restricted manner. But through the understanding we have no object at all, since it contains merely the form. Sensibility, that is, the faculty of intuition and of sensation, supplies the material for thought, and is the other source of knowledge. If, now, knowledge is to be related to the object, both cooperate: sensibility yields the intuition, and the understanding the concept. When a body is driven by two forces, there arises a third motion, in which it keeps to the track of neither the one nor the other, namely, the diagonal force. For example, a ball which would have gone straight to the centre of the target goes somewhat towards the edge of the target if it is driven sideways by the wind, whither it would be driven neither by the force of the powder nor by that of the wind alone. Now sensibility flows into the acts of the understanding, and from this arises the diagonal direction of the understanding, in which it obtains now truth, now semblance [Schein]. Sensibility, therefore, is the cause of semblance, and the understanding [is so] in so far as it passes judgment upon it. Sensibility in itself, however, is no source of errors; for in so far as it relates to its objects, there is agreement with the laws of this cognitive faculty. The reason why the senses do not judge erroneously is that they cannot judge at all; for only the understanding judges. Error, therefore, lies neither in the understanding alone nor in the senses alone, but always in the influence of the senses upon the understanding, when we fail properly to distinguish the influence of sensibility upon the understanding.",[]],"wiener-s04-082":["Logic, since it abstracts from all content, can speak of the influence of sensibility only so far as to set forth the subjective ground of our judgment. The understanding is the objective ground of our judgment. But when something subjective, which yet belongs to sensibility, flows into our judgment, sensibility has intermingled; and this is the source of errors. From this intermingling arises *cognitio hybrida*, a bastard knowledge composed of two parts. Here sensibility does not serve the understanding but confuses it, and from this arises the diagonal direction of the understanding. For example, an adherent of an opinion holds the opinion to be something probable. Probability is an objective ground. But the subjective attachment produces the effect, which is taken to be objective. – Since in every error the understanding is still always at work, men, when they judge at the risk of error, are always still exercising truth. Those, therefore, who have many errors always have something true; for they have used their understanding and cultivated their powers.",[]],"wiener-s04-083":["Nor, in error, can we complain of the limits of our understanding; for [in so doing] we accuse nature. The limits of the understanding are indeed the cause of ignorance, but not of errors; for where I am ignorant, I need only refrain from judging. Every error is consequently self-incurred, when our inclination draws us beyond our bounds. In all our judgments there is always something true. A man can never err wholly and entirely. If it sometimes seems so to us, we have not understood the man; for, according to what he perhaps presupposes, he may always have some truth, even if only partial. A total error would be a complete conflict with the laws of the understanding. For then it could not arise from the understanding,",[]],"wiener-s04-084":["which can produce nothing that conflicts with its nature. What deviates from its laws is the influence of sensibility. In every error there is still always a true judgment; but if much is foisted upon it by sensibility, there is indeed falsity in it, but it is only partial.",[]],"wiener-s04-085":["Since we abstract from all relation to the object, and consider only the rule of the accord of the understanding with itself, the criterion of truth in logic can only be the agreement of the laws of knowledge with themselves. The rule of the accord of knowledge with itself is the principle of contradiction and the principle of sufficient ground. These are not indeed sufficient *materialiter*, but they do suffice for judging *formaliter* of a thing whether it agrees with itself. The principle of contradiction is a negative principle: a mode of knowledge is false if it contradicts itself. But a mode of knowledge which does not contradict itself is not on that account true. The principle of contradiction is indeed such that nothing can be opposed to it; but as a positive *principium* for knowing truth it is not sufficient. It is positive only in so far as through it I can perceive the necessity of the truth in the case of a necessary mode of knowledge. – The principle of sufficient ground applies when a mode of knowledge is connected with a ground, or when from a mode of knowledge nothing but true consequences follow. Both principles, therefore, insist on unity. – If a mode of knowledge does not contradict itself, while its opposite would contradict itself, it is true. If a mode of knowledge does not contradict itself, it is possible; but from the *principium contradictionis* not all the unity of a mode of knowledge can yet be inferred. For example, that my thoughts should penetrate into the souls of other men is possible, but not on that account true. If one mode of knowledge is the ground of another, there is a connection of the two. If the ground is true, the consequence is also true. If all the consequences are true, the ground must also be true. The principle of contradiction is treated expressly in metaphysics, [as is the doctrine] that the criterion of truth is the *principium rationis*.",[]],"wiener-s04-086":["1.) *A priori*: the connection of a mode of knowledge with its ground.",[]],"wiener-s04-087":["2.) *A posteriori*: the connection of a ground with its consequences.",[]],"wiener-s04-088":["A mode of knowledge without a ground is groundless, but not yet false, for example, that plants have souls. But for it to be true, grounds are required, and the knowledge must be connected *a priori*. From the consequences we infer to the truth of the knowledge",[]],"wiener-s04-089":["1.) negatively: if even a single consequence is false, the whole mode of knowledge is false. For were the knowledge true, this consequence too would have to be true. Among false consequences dangerousness is also commonly cited. But dangers are often only relative dangers, and often rest merely on external circumstances, as when, for example, the times are so bad that even truth is endangered. Here, therefore, what matters is not the dangerousness but the falsity of the proposition. Whoever begins with dangers is seeking artifices to win someone over before reason can be used.",[]],"wiener-s04-090":["2.) positively. In knowing the truth of a proposition it will not do for me to infer from the truth of some consequences to the truth of a mode of knowledge. The knowledge may have something true in it, and from this the truth of this one consequence arises; but the rest may be false. I cannot, therefore, infer the truth of the knowledge on such a ground. If indeed all the consequences are false, there is nothing true and nothing sound in the knowledge. The positive inference thus carries the *onus probandi*: I must show the truth of all the consequences. In the negative inference, on the other hand, I need only show the falsity of some consequences, and this is therefore a sufficient criterion. Only, it is difficult, indeed it cannot be done at all, that I should be able to know all the consequences; for were I to know them, my knowledge would be certain. Yet we do employ it, and in so doing make this probable inference: if most of the consequences are true, all are true. For when a mode of knowledge does not contradict itself, I seek its ground and examine the consequences of this ground. If many, but not all, of the consequences can be discovered, I accept it as an incomplete mode of knowledge, inferring from the truth of several consequences to the truth of the proposition. For since it is impossible to show the truth of all the consequences, it follows that here too the criterion is not strictly sufficient in its use, but can be employed only as a ground of probability. The principle of sufficient ground is treated here not as in metaphysics, where it holds of the object, but as a logical principle. For since the principle of contradiction is not yet a sufficient criterion of truth, the connection of knowledge must nonetheless be investigated by means of [the principle of sufficient ground]. The principle of contradiction is the principle of coexistence and of accord; the principle of sufficient ground is the principle of connection.",[]],"wiener-s04-091":["A mode of knowledge is either totally false or partially false. A mode of knowledge may be false and yet partial truths may be discovered in it; there is, for example, more truth in polytheism than in atheism. – In disputations there are certain subtle fallacies designed to confuse others. For example, someone says: All Moors are men; the respondent is a man; therefore he is a Moor. The respondent is indeed a man, though not a black one, and so the speaker does not speak wholly falsely. It is often good to seek out what is true in this way, and not at once to throw away everything that contains an error. We can dispute in two ways: either because we have different interests, and then we dispute as enemies; or because we have a common interest but do not agree on the means of furthering it, and then we dispute as friends. All men have a common interest in the investigation of truth; and in such a dispute we must therefore be sympathetic participants, not dwelling on showing where the other has erred, but where he is right. This sympathetic disposition is, indeed, moral, and does not properly belong to logic; but logic prescribes its rules as correct maxims. When we believe a proposition to be wholly false, we need only recall our principle stated above (p. 85), that no proposition can be wholly false. One may, indeed, feign for oneself a total falsity which no one holds to be true; but that is then no error. When a thing is so false that there is not even any interest in seeking a degree of truth in it, and it is thus beneath all notice, it is absurd. When, therefore, the falsity is evident to the common understanding, the knowledge is absurd. Between total and partial truth the distinction concerns either the object or the knowledge. The defects of knowledge are either a partial falsity of the knowledge (*vitium*), or else the knowledge is wholly true but does not cover the whole truth of the object (*defectus*). In partial falsity the fault lies in the lack of totality of the truth of the knowledge, not in the totality of the truth of the object. If this incorrectness does not touch the essential end of the knowledge, the knowledge is *tolerabiliter* true; prejudices in religion, for example, which we must tolerate and seek to amend. – – We have remarked that a mode of knowledge is either so constituted that in it, as knowledge, not everything is true, or that the whole knowledge is indeed true, but the whole truth of the object is not present. For example, one who relates a story and conceals a part of it does not on that account lie. He here merely employs a stratagem; and it is sometimes necessary and unavoidable to pass over a part in silence. One dissimulates when one merely conceals, and the knowledge is then *totaliter* true, although the whole of the object is not contained in it. – This examination, how many errors are to be met with in a mode of knowledge, and whether this or that mode of knowledge must on that account be contested, is of great importance, and presupposes much prudence, which must be founded on a power imparted by experience. – In respect of both one distinguishes between exact and rough knowledge. Exactness concerns the relation of the knowledge to the object, whether the object is known wholly or in part. For example, the determination of distance by paces is sufficient and exact for the determination of a journey, but not for the determination of the boundaries of an estate. Fractions in numbers may be omitted if [the reckoning] is to be exact enough for payment. But a merchant closing his books may not omit even 1/10, because then the balance, which is the proof of the correctness of the account, is not exact. Thus a mode of knowledge may in one regard be entitled exact, in another a rough estimate. Exactness often looks like pedantry when it is objectively exact in matters where it is not at all required for my purpose; for example, the ancients, in fortification, determined how many cubic inches had to be carted away in making a ditch. Scrupulous exactness is distinguished from *exactitudo*, or precise exactness, in this, that precise exactness is suited to its end, and for that very reason receives the name *exactitudo*. In the sciences which rest on *a priori* principles, exactness is of extraordinary importance, as, for example, in mathematics. Most ways of thinking almost never aim at what is exact, and there are few minds so thorough as to observe exactness everywhere; yet this exactness has great worth and must never be neglected, since it elevates the faculty of judgment, even if it is often not suited to the end, and it requires but little effort.",[]],"wiener-s04-092":["There are judgments *en gros* [in the gross] and *en detail* [in detail]. || V Madame Geoffrin was a great patroness of scholars, though she was not herself a great *genie*. She encouraged merit, and used to say that one ought to judge every man *en gros*, since he would otherwise lose a great deal if he were judged *en detail*. But anyone can judge *en gros*. For example: the book pleases me greatly, it is fine. But what in the book [is fine]? To find fault and to praise *en detail*, that not everyone can do. Our new great geniuses, as they are called, learn the sciences *en gros*. Hence all their judgments and modes of knowledge are *en gros* and never *en detail*. From this arises the superficiality of knowledge, on account of which it can never become exact. From exact knowledge we must distinguish the subtle, the scrupulous, that is, an exactness which extends to the parts: the skill of noticing what more easily escapes ordinary attention. This is also entitled the microscopic element in our judgment. Knowledge is micrological when it clings to what is small, and for the small cannot see the great. Subtle exactness is not on that account already micrological. For the subtle mind can none the less be broad in its concepts in respect of knowledge, since we must distinguish objects not only at great [ Textlücke: AA: Entfernungen ] but also at [single] points. Yet many contract all their knowledge to what is small, and the subtle mind is commonly micrological. The diametrical opposite of subtle knowledge is coarse knowledge. [Two things are] diametrically opposed when, in escaping one fault, we fall into the opposite one. Both are blameworthy. For he who clings much to subtleties is blind in regard to the whole. Coarse knowledge, which is directed to the great and the whole, seems, however, to promise more of itself. – Coarse knowledge is not gross error, which contradicts the universal laws of the understanding. It is coarse in so far as it is not yet extended and is still crude, and bears the marks of a merely common understanding. Our author says: all truth is dogmatic. – All our modes of knowledge are either historical or rational. Modes of knowledge are historical when they are communicated to us from elsewhere; subjectively rational in so far as they are drawn from principles of reason; objectively rational in so far as, in respect of their true sources, they lie in reason. Objectively historical knowledge is possible only by means of the senses, of communication and of instruction. Philosophy is with many subjectively historical; objectively all philosophy is knowledge of reason. – All knowledge of reason is at the same time apodictic, that is, it carries with it the proof of necessity. By experience alone I can know only contingent things; through reason, on the other hand, I can also have necessary knowledge. Philosophy and mathematics are accordingly apodictic modes of knowledge. That the stone is heavy I know, indeed, through experience; but the proof of the necessity of its weight is given me by reason. Apodictic propositions are of two kinds: mathematical in so far as they belong to mathematics, dogmatic in so far as they belong to philosophy. Accordingly all knowledge of reason from concepts is *dogma*, and all knowledge of reason from the construction of concepts is *mathema*. When I say that every body is divisible, this is an apodictically certain proposition. – In revealed religion propositions containing doctrines which reason cannot know are entitled *dogmata*. This is already an improper use of the term. Were our reason, however, extended, it too would have this knowledge, and these would then be *dogmata* also, although at present they are only historical modes of knowledge.",[]],"wiener-s04-093":["Our author entitles a system an aggregate [Inbegriff] of dogmatic truths. – But the object makes no difference between systematic and common knowledge; the two differ only in form. A system is that in which everything is subordinated to an idea which must be directed to the whole and determine the parts. For example, someone may know many histories without having a science of them; for he lacks the form. He has made for himself no outline of the whole, and has not ordered everything according to an idea. It is this idea, therefore, which constitutes the systematic form. We can think the system as an *aggregat* or as a whole of knowledge. Totality is the determination of the whole, and it lies in the idea. In an *aggregat*, by contrast, nothing is determined, for I do not know what may yet be added to it. In a system, on the other hand, everything is already determined. – A mode of knowledge can be rhapsodical and yet not tumultuary. For the tumultuary is opposed to the methodical, and without method knowledge is tumultuary. But knowledge which is set forth methodically, yet without system, is rhapsodical; as, for instance, when we adapt ourselves to the capacity of comprehension of the person to be instructed.",[]],"wiener-s04-094":["Some have presented their subject in aphorisms, without exhibiting the articulation of the whole. The articulation is indeed always present, but it is not clearly visible to one who lacks the idea of the whole. It is therefore necessary to compose things systematically, in order to obtain a concept of completeness and to have a touchstone for the various parts, [showing] how they agree with the whole. In the sciences we must always have their skeleton before our eyes, so as to judge by it whether this or that belongs to the science. – Aesthetic truths are those which are suited to general comprehensibility; logical truths are scholastic, or, as our author says, learned.",[]],"wiener-s04-095":["A false mode of knowledge and error are to be distinguished. When I propose and examine a false judgment, that is not yet error; error is the holding of what is false to be true [Vorwahrhaltung der Falschheit]. Illusion [Schein] must therefore be added, and indeed logical illusion. In optical illusion the senses are deceived. Moral illusion is when what serves our own advantage seems to us to spring from duty. Logical illusion arises from the mere form of the understanding. Our knowledge may well conform to the understanding in respect of form; for I can easily imitate the form without the propositions possessing inner correctness. Thus the logical form itself yields an illusion of truth, of which the sophists frequently avail themselves. No error is in itself unavoidable, since we need only refrain from judging of things of which we understand nothing. Ignorance may indeed be unavoidable, for it does not always rest on our will, but often on the limits of our nature. In error, however, we are always ourselves to blame, in that we are not cautious enough in weighing a judgment for which we lack sufficient knowledge [Kenntniß]. Yet there are cases in which a judgment absolutely must be made, and here it happens that one falls into an error which in this relation was unavoidable. For example, a man wishes to cross a frozen river; but by some accident the ice is thinner in one place, and he is drowned. Had he been able to judge this beforehand, he would not have crossed. Error is always something positive, whereas not being true is something negative; for what is positive in error is that it is nevertheless thought. Illusion is the subjective ground of an error. An incorrectness or falsity is manifest when it is clear to common human understanding. When the incorrectness is not clear to every understanding, it is concealed, but can yet be made manifest; and to this all refutations are directed. In refutation it is a duty first to show whence this or that incorrectness in human knowledge has come, that is, I must discover the source of the error, namely the illusion; and only afterwards can I prove to [the other] its falsity. For if I do not do the former, and do not make sufficiently clear to him what has misled him into holding this or that to be true, I leave him still in uncertainty and doubt. I must not, therefore, call anyone absurd out of hand. For he would certainly not have accepted this or that mode of knowledge had he not been brought, by this or that concealed illusion, to hold it true. A man who forthwith calls another absurd gives him grave offence, and mistakes the interest of universal human reason , H on which point let us recall that true saying of the. —What contains a manifest falsity is an absurdity. But we cannot prove a manifest incorrectness to anyone; for to him, owing to some misunderstanding, it is not manifest. The expression 'absurd', 'preposterous', concerns, first, the error itself, and secondly has reference to the person who holds it; for by it I declare him unworthy to be admitted into the class of common human understanding. If I have declared my refutation against the judge [Richter] whom I regard as preposterous, I cannot have the intention of instructing him. For in denying him common human understanding I have declared him unworthy of instruction, and should myself be acting preposterously. My refutation would then have to be addressed to others. But others who have sound common sense will see the absurdity for themselves. These expressions are therefore not to be used. It is better to let an absurdity pass, unless it carries with it something odious which I must keep away from it.",[]],"wiener-s04-096":["He who merely has sensations and does not judge does not err. Every error, therefore, lies in judgment. Judgments are acts of understanding and of reason. Speaking *generaliter*, we may say that objective truth is agreement with the object, and subjective truth agreement with the laws of understanding and reason. The understanding thus agrees with its own laws, the laws of the understanding. We have a faculty [of knowing] the necessary and the contingent; we therefore have activities of pure understanding. This part is entitled transcendental philosophy. Our understanding is of a peculiar kind, in that it is not capable of any intuition; for only the senses are capable of that. Here the second power comes into play, namely sensibility. We have spoken of the understanding in respect of error in judgment. We have now so far cultivated our understanding that by itself it does not err. Nor do the senses err by themselves. Why? Because they do not judge at all. In our sensibility there is something which is related solely to the object, namely intuition, and something which is related to the subject, namely sensation. To clearness of knowledge belong marks, *notae*. A mark is a ground of knowledge, chiefly also in the comparison of things. In comparison I attend to the *identitaet* or *diversitaet* of things. Marks are used not merely to distinguish things, as our author holds, but also to discover their agreement. For example, I wish not merely to distinguish a sheep from a goat, but also to know in what it agrees with another sheep. – Marks may be regarded as a positive representation [forming part] of the whole representation of the thing. A mode of knowledge is accordingly distinct when we are conscious of its partial concepts and represent them clearly. If these partial concepts are in turn divided, I obtain marks of marks. These parts of marks are entitled subordinate marks; through them our modes of knowledge ascend to universal and higher marks, which are common to most things. For example: what is virtue? A mastery over the inclinations. What is mastery? The power which one has over another. What is power? And so on. Through these subordinate marks arises profound distinctness, in which we penetrate ever further. – We may also have marks by coordination, both marks being *compartes* [co-parts]. For example, a body is an impenetrable whole: 1.) a whole, 2.) impenetrable. This does not bear on the whole [as its part]; both bear on the concept of body, which they together comprehend as its *complementum*. The series of coordinate marks can go on to infinity. For example, gold is heavy, ductile, fire-resistant, does not rust, etc. etc. Here there is not yet completeness; for we might find a thousand more such marks. A mode of knowledge, then, becomes distinct through clear marks. Knowledge may become distinct through an *aggregat* of coordinate marks, and it then grows extensively through the addition of each new mark. I take together so many new marks, adding a new one for as long as I can, until they seem to make up the whole *aggregat*. This is how we proceed in definitions. In a certain respect, namely for this or that end, they may indeed have limits. But knowledge grows also through the series of subordinate marks, when we analyse these and draw from them new marks which already lay in it, but which we did not yet clearly represent. To extend one's knowledge by coordination is more agreeable than by subordination. The latter is difficult, since one must hold one's attention fixed upon a concept whose marks become ever subtler, until at last they admit of no further division. This division none the less has limits. This is the intensive magnitude of distinctness, which, though it gives the understanding more trouble, extends knowledge further and renders it exceedingly correct. Here marks are not added; I only decompose the concept, taking no mark in addition to the mark, and so adding nothing, but dividing. This way of pursuing the series of marks has a limited progress; for in the end I arrive at partial concepts which are irresoluble, and I come to the concept of the simple. A mode of knowledge is distinct when the marks themselves are clear. If I can specify clear marks, I can explain myself concerning a thing. If I can specify no marks, I cannot explain to another what he does not understand, but must perhaps make shift with examples. The magnitude of distinctness through coordinate marks is extended (extensive) distinctness; that through subordinate marks is profound (intensive) distinctness. – || V Poets seek to exhibit everything distinctly in intuition, and with this aim they coordinate marks in a thing, so as to render the distinctness extended. The poet differs from the philosopher in that the latter works for the understanding, the former for sensibility. The poet too, it is true, must observe logical correctness so far as to have unity; but his proper end is the delight of sensibility. || V The reading of poetry is therefore of great use, since it procures for our understanding the capacity for extended distinctness, which is the easiest and most entertaining distinctness of all, because it is acquired historically. Profound distinctness is the business of philosophy; what matters there is that from one mark I bring out another, and continue so until they become microscopic. Knowledge is not thereby extended; rather we descend into its depth. It is used chiefly in metaphysical investigations, in which I ask always for the ground of the ground without adding new [marks]. It is intensive distinctness, which renders our knowledge very dry, indeed, but thorough. For if we always remain with the whole and never touch the parts, we merely swim on the surface and have superficial knowledge [Kenntniße]. Marks serve either as an internal or as an external ground of knowledge. A mode of knowledge serves for internal use in so far as it serves to discern the manifold in the object; it serves for external use when we wish to distinguish the object from others, or to see its agreement with others. For external use a few marks may easily suffice which are yet of little consequence for internal use. Marks for the purpose of internal use are of greater importance. For if I know the thing from within, these marks will certainly suffice also for external use, although the external does not suffice for internal use.",[]],"wiener-s04-097":["1.) Negative and affirmative concepts.",[]],"wiener-s04-098":["2.) Negative and affirmative marks of concepts.",[]],"wiener-s04-099":["If the object is itself a lack, I can have only a negative concept of it. For example, freedom is [the condition] that the will does not stand under the constraint of men. In logic, which abstracts from all content, we can treat only of negative or affirmative marks; and the way in which concepts are made distinct is precisely by affirmation or negation. By negation I have not extended the concept, and cannot thereby gain more distinct insight into it. The affirmative concept must be added and procure a profounder distinctness. Through it we see into knowledge more distinctly as regards its content, and with the greatest clearness. A negative mark, therefore, is not used to increase our insight; it serves only to exclude a concept from other things, so as to prevent errors. All affirmative marks serve to ground and produce knowledge. – The understanding is properly directed to furthering the perfection of knowledge; but it is also to its credit when errors do not creep in. Negative marks are here a *conditio sine qua non*. For since we are in danger of running into falsity, they must serve to keep errors off, and they are among the most excellent means of making our knowledge perfect. All negative marks which guard against errors into which no one has fallen are of no use whatever; for they are useful only when they guard against a possible error. There are certain sciences which contain established negative marks. It is to be wished that we could draw round every science a rampart of negative marks, without leaving a gate open here and there by which errors could slip in. Theologians begin to speculate by reason about things which God says in the creation, and to inquire into the relation and constitution of this or that revealed doctrine; this is the source of many errors in theology. It is thus that negative marks keep errors off. Ignorance of things does no harm; errors, however, not only fail to increase the knowledge of truth, but hinder it. It has been mentioned above that in many respects we are ignorant, and also that we cannot always ask about utility, but should, without circuitousness, go straight for everything worth knowing, so far as we can. From ignorance we must distinguish voluntary ignoring, when we take no notice of certain circumstances which contribute nothing to the purpose of the matter. What lies outside our end we need not know. In conversations and in affairs we ignore, that is, abstract from, many things which we know well enough, but leave out because they do not belong to the end. Ignoring therefore calls for much prudence in religious instruction. We should ignore the errors of the common rabble; for if we stir up the rubbish that is smouldering under the ashes, it bursts into flame. Only, what we ignore must not be prejudicial to the main end. But actually to give occasion for errors is true deception; it is the *peccatum philosophicum* of the Jesuits, who believed that, if their intention was good, they acted well even in bad actions. Ignoring is therefore something quite different from representing [a thing], as the matter itself requires. Importance concerns the relation to use and end. A mode of knowledge is important when it is a great ground of use, or serves for many kinds of ends. Importance, however, is always relative. For example, to a minister politics is a matter of great importance; to another it is unimportant. Few place importance in serious and weighty things; most place it in matters of secondary concern. Many hold it unimportant to speak of the most important objects of religion and morality, and act honestly either from custom or from resolve, and so on. To inform someone of the importance of a thing is very difficult. Of the importance of the end I can indeed speak; but the importance of the means is difficult. For example, to form a courtier: for this end I can recommend speaking French, wide reading, etc. etc. But the importance of the means I ignore. Logic, however, abstracts from the importance of the content, and speaks of relative, formal importance. A mode of knowledge is logically important when it is a ground of great consequences. When a mode of knowledge is the ground of many and numerous consequences, this is the fruitfulness of knowledge. For example, what makes up the cultivation of our manners is unimportant, yet it is so frequent that it is useful and fruitful. Virtue is more than *conduite* [good conduct], and therefore belongs to importance. Modes of knowledge are sufficient for internal use when from these marks everything belonging to [the thing] can be understood; sufficient for external use when in comparison they serve to show how things agree with, or differ from, others. Absolutely, no mark is sufficient, but always only in this or that respect. For example, marks may be sufficient for practice, but not for speculation.",[]],"wiener-s04-100":["Necessary marks cannot be separated at all from the concept of a thing, but belong *ad esse* [to its being]. Contingent marks are not constituents of the thing, but *accessoria*. For example, reason is necessary in man; learning is contingent. – Extra-essential marks are those which do not belong to the thing as *constitutiva*; essential marks are *constitutiva* of the thing. Extension *ad essentiam corporis pertinens est* [belongs to the essence of body]. Motion is extra-essential, and an *accessio* to the concept of body. All *praedicata* may therefore be divided into *ad essentiam pertinentia* [those belonging to the essence] *et extraessentialia* [and the extra-essential].",[]],"wiener-s04-101":["The essence is the *complexus notarum necessarium interne sufficientium* [the complex of necessary marks internally sufficient]. In logic we speak of the essence of concepts, not of things. The necessary marks of a thing are distinguished [as follows]. Some belong to the thing *ut rationata*, as consequences of other marks of the same thing; *aliae notae conceptus alieni non nisi ut rationes competunt* [other marks of a concept belong to it only as grounds]. For example, that man has a language is an internal mark, but it derives from other marks, namely that he has reason, a tongue, etc. etc.; that he is an animal, however, I can judge from nothing else. *Ideae conceptus quia sunt rationes, minime derivatae sunt essentiales* [the ideas of a concept which, being grounds, are in no way derived, are essential], that is, those which lie in the concept not as consequences but as ground. Those *quae ad essentiam ut rationata pertinent* [which belong to the essence as consequences] are *attributa*; these must be derived from the essential [marks]. *Essentia ipsa est complexus essentialium* [the essence itself is the complex of the essentials],",[]],"wiener-s04-102":["the attributes also belong to the essence, but as consequences, and not as constituents of the essence.",[]],"wiener-s04-103":["*Extraessentialia* are of two kinds: first, *interna*: *modi*, or contingent properties; 2.) *externae relationes*, external relations. All relations are contingent, whereas the internal [extra-essentials] may sometimes be *essentialia*. For example, learning is extra-essential, yet in many a man it is an internal determination; on the other hand, to be master or servant are *relationes*, concepts of relation. For on a desert island one is neither master nor servant. The external marks taken together are therefore outside and inside things.",[]],"wiener-s04-104":["We speak of the essence of things according to the concept which we have of things, that is, according to the logical concept. But we can also inquire into the essence of the thing in and by itself. This is what constitutes the essence and necessarily belongs to it, even though it is not contained in our concept: in short, the first constituent of a body.",[]],"wiener-s04-105":["This is the real essence; the other is the logical essence. It is complained that the essence of things is unknown to us, namely the ground of all that properly belongs to things. Yet we can very easily know the logical essence. The logical essence signifies nothing more than the sum of all the marks which primarily constitute a given concept: *complexus omnium notarum conceptum aliquem primitive constituentium*. *Complexus omnium notarum*, that is, a concept which is complete in respect of the marks that, taken together, constitute the concept. To the logical essence, therefore, belongs the *totalitaet* of the marks. They are to be primitive marks, that is, marks which belong to the concept not as consequences but as *constitutiva*. For example, if I wish to know the logical essence of bodies, I find many marks. Among these some belong to the essence as consequences; others are the constituents, the *essentialia* of a concept, which is entitled the *complexus essentiae*. Extension, impenetrability, figure are primitive; divisibility, etc. etc., on the other hand, are attributes. It is possible to give the logical essence of all things. For of all things of which we speak we must have concepts, and these we must explain, in order to be able to find their *essentialia* and *rationata*, and what belongs to the essence primitively and derivatively.",[]],"wiener-s04-106":["To have insight into the real essence surpasses the human understanding. Of no single thing can we specify a complete ground. That would require a universal, complete experience; and to institute all possible experience regarding an object is impossible. *A priori*, and without any experience, we can explain no thing of nature, since the understanding cannot speculate about what it does not know. The real essence is also entitled nature. When, therefore, I distinguish essence and nature, I distinguish the logical essence from the real essence. When we abstract the marks of our concept, we have the logical essence. But if we investigate the innermost ground of a body, I shall know its nature, that is, its real essence.",[]],"wiener-s04-107":["The matter of distinct and clear modes of knowledge does not belong to logic [in every respect]. Logic is to teach the rules of the understanding in regard to representations which are found in us. We must therefore be conscious of the representations in order to know the rules together with their connections. If we are not conscious of them, we cannot deal with them in that way. Clearness, then, in which I am conscious of my representation, belongs to logic; but logic is not able to make obscure representations clear. That we assume obscure representations in the soul is therefore no concern of logic; its concern is how clearness is brought to distinctness, and this to completeness, how many clear representations are to be made universal and compared, and so on. Of obscure concepts psychology treats. Representations are called obscure in comparison with those which [...] have the degree of clearness that is required: when a representation distinguishes the thing from some, but only a few, things; when the matter is suited to the comprehension of one person, but not of all. There is therefore also a logical obscurity, which is, however, distinct from the psychological, of which we are not conscious at all. The logical is comparative obscurity, the psychological absolute obscurity. For example, the concept of instinct is an obscure concept. We know well enough that it is an impulse to act, but that does not exhaust everything. – Comparative obscurity we may have in relation. In one relation, where it suffices for distinguishing, [the representation] is clear; in another, where it does not suffice, obscure. For example, if a mode of knowledge *conscendirt* [rises to the level of] certain subjects, it cannot but *transcendiren* [exceed] the capacity of others. This is quite unavoidable; but one must be very attentive in the matter. A teacher may speak very distinctly though he understands nothing at all, by accommodating himself to the capacity of comprehension of his hearers and usurping their terminologies, and on that account distinctly obscure ( H— ) [AA: ist ] to them. We must always speak with relative obscurity if we wish to rise to a clearness which is in some measure universal. The teacher must by obscurity draw people up to himself, so that they may thereby learn [to think] with universal distinctness; he must not let himself down entirely to them. Distinctness is relative in respect of men; it must be accommodated to the speculative understanding, and not to the common human understanding. Our object must be so constituted as [to accord with] the way in which we grasp concepts by abstraction. If we cannot do so, we must let it be, and not complain of obscurity. We must study what is suited to our capacity of comprehension, and not find absolute sciences blameworthy, even though others can be drawn further down. In regard to total and partial obscurity it is to be noted: a mode of knowledge cannot be wholly clear, *sed obscurae quaedam notae in conceptu claro universali notae singulares possunt esse obscurae universalis autem clarae* [but in a clear universal concept certain marks are obscure; the single marks may be obscure, the universal, however, clear]. For example, one spark of light does not illuminate, but many make it bright. So too with concepts. A representation which is clear as a whole, but not in respect of its parts, is an indistinct representation. For example, blue and yellow make green; but in the colour green we are not always conscious of these parts which lie in it. So too with concepts: for example, the concept of right [Recht]. It is meant to signify what is straight [gerade], taken without circuitousness or cunning; yet it is not as with a straight line. For each sees his own advantage, without also harming others. In short, much is required to set out the manifold of a concept, and then in turn to be able to unfold the parts of the parts; and we have many concepts which we can know only as wholes. It is a part of philosophy to give the profound distinctness of the parts of parts. But in the end we always come to partial concepts which are simple and can only become clear to us; for example, reality, the mathematical point. When we ourselves arbitrarily put a concept together, it can become wholly distinct. For since we have made it ourselves, we also know what we have put into it, and are conscious of the partial representations. But if it is given to us, we cannot always gain distinct insight [into it].",[]],"wiener-s04-108":["|| V All clearness can be understood either subjectively or objectively. Through objective clearness the object becomes clear. It is the greater logical perfection; its first degree is that we are conscious of the representation, and its second degree is clearness in the parts, that is, distinctness. Knowledge grows subjectively through the magnitude of logical clearness when the representations in the subject, in relation to the rest of our knowledge, excite more attention and occupy the power of the mind [Gemüthskraft] more strongly. || V When a representation, instead of being obscured by others in the mind, itself obscures others, this is that clearness which excites a greater alteration of state in the mind. We often need to affect our state through a representation, and to bring the springs [Triebfedern] of the mind into tension. This subjectively greater clearness is entitled vividness [Lebhaftigkeit]. || V The vividness of knowledge accordingly belongs to aesthetic knowledge and to sensibility. Here we must attend carefully to how vividness is to be brought in where there is obscurity. A certain degree of vividness, for instance an apt expression, can be used everywhere without detriment to the obscurity [Dunkelheit; perhaps to be read Deutlichkeit, distinctness]. Indeed it is good, for without any vividness no attention would be aroused. – || V In the sermon there must first be distinctness and correct exposition, and then vividness must also be given to the delivery, in order to arouse attention and to move [*commoviren*] the soul. This vividness has its degrees. The intensive degree is the strength of the knowledge. || V The extensive degree depends on intuition, not on feeling. Here the matter is set before our eyes through examples from common life and through cases *in concreto*. The more marks we bring forward, in order through intuition to disentangle the matter from the mere idea, the more extensively greater subjective clearness we introduce. This is what is most necessary, since through it concepts become more suited to our understanding and more distinct. For the understanding must direct [itself] purposively by means of sensibility. – || V Intensive vividness rests on feeling; it rests not on intuition but on the strength of the knowledge. It belongs to feeling that it depicts beauties and produces emotion [Rührung], that is, agitation of the mind. Such agitations are astonishment, fear, admiration, etc. Through them we are put into the state least fitted for judging. || V When intuition is already present, feeling may well be added. For we are then already convinced, and could not easily lose the intuition through a stupefying feeling. Everything that we do must be done from principles. Otherwise it is equivocal, so that the same emotion leads me today to good actions and tomorrow to bad ones. || V Feeling excites tears, but nothing in the world dries sooner than tears. || V When the judgment has penetrated deep into the soul, sensations may well be taken along with it, but as subsidiary causes and, as it were, as parts. || V But the majesty of religion is too great for one to play with feelings in the pulpit. Feelings must be not the nourishment but the companion of the soul, so that when our soul has been enlightened by knowledge a moderate emotion arises and cheers it. Only this emotion must not shout down the intuition. This is the vividness which the aesthetic has in respect of form. In that vividness which is directed to intuition, not much emotion should be introduced. For it acts upon the understanding alone, and the understanding pays no regard to [emotion]. || V For exact instruction as to how intuition and sensation must be combined, that is, how one can give intuition to all conviction and yet also admit sensation, one should read Sulzer, and especially his Theory of the Fine Arts, or his Vorübungen. One should strive for knowledge of history. Rousseau likewise shows much understanding in his writings, but lets himself be carried away too much by his enthusiasm. Yet he has so very much that is well meant and true that one must leave it to each reader to distinguish this from what is merely enthusiastic in him. Hume too can be used for this purpose.",[]],"wiener-s04-109":["The greatest logical perfection after clearness is distinctness. In the Wolffian logics only the analytic kind of distinctness is ever considered. There is, however, a far more extensive kind, namely the synthetic production of distinctness. No analytic production nourishes knowledge; it only resolves the knowledge that is given to me, so that I now learn to distinguish better what was already contained in that knowledge. The knowledge does not grow in content; I merely know it with greater consciousness. Philosophy is chiefly occupied with this kind. Through morals, for example, I do not enlarge my knowledge; I only set it in a better light. Socrates said that he was the midwife of his hearers, that is, he brought them to reflect better upon what they already knew and to become better conscious of it. If we always knew what we know, namely in using certain words and concepts which are so subtle in their application, we should be astonished at the treasure of our knowledge. Philosophers and jurists, for example, have never yet been able to develop and expound the concepts of right [Recht] and equity [Billigkeit]. But one need only give someone a case *in concreto*, and he will soon say how far it is right and how far it is equitable. There must therefore be something in my concept of equity which is distinct from that in my concept of right, and I make use of this mark, though it is veiled in obscurity, *in casu*. It is one of the greatest skills of a learned man who is not merely scholastically correct but also possesses popularity, that when he speaks with people who are quite unable to bring their concepts to light, he can draw everything out of them and tell them what they think. It is in this way, for example, that the preacher satisfies his hearers in the most agreeable manner. Should someone say, 'But I was not taught so' – [the answer is]: Yes, but were you properly conscious of all that you knew? – This making distinct of the concept takes place chiefly through definitions. But once this has been accomplished, it is also necessary to enlarge one's concept; and then distinctness grows synthetically, in that new marks are added. Platonic or Socratic questions are of such a kind that they draw out of the other's knowledge what lies in it, by bringing him to consciousness of what he really thought. They are therefore very different from catechism questions, in which one asks only for what lies in the memory. – Definitions have the highest degree of analytic distinctness; they are the final aim of all our concepts.",[]],"wiener-s04-110":["Sound understanding has this peculiarity, that it runs so far ahead of speculation that we have always carried matters a good way forward before we begin to speculate about them; for example, in the concepts of happiness, equity, etc. When someone makes an incorrect use of the concepts he has had, the concept is not on that account to be held captious [*chicanisch*] and blameworthy; for our concepts always contain much reason in them, but if we do not develop them properly we may sometimes stray from the right path. To make a distinct concept is the synthetic method; to make a concept distinct is the analytic. When a distinct concept is to be made, one must first adduce marks. For example: a certain object has impenetrability, weight, etc.; this I will call body. In this way I feign [*fingire*] a concept for myself, for example a republic without punishments, in which everyone acts well for the sake of his own best interest. Such a fiction is a distinct concept which I have myself made. Mathematical distinctness is wholly synthetic. The mathematician says: whatever all men may have represented to themselves under 'circle', I think only that it is to be [the figure produced] when a straight line moves about a fixed point. Here I have not made the concept of the circle distinct, but have made a distinct concept. Some say that with distinctness in respect of logical form, that is, with analytic distinctness, the energy of knowledge vanishes, and aesthetic perfection loses its weight [*pondus*], when I always make known distinctly to someone what he thought confusedly. In the case of illusion, for example in the case of beauty, this may be true. But where the question is of truth, the analysis of knowledge cannot in this respect be a hindrance; it can only be a help. The analysis must only not continue so long that the object itself is in the end lost to sight by those who are not aware of the analysis. For in going into the *minutissima* I often lose the representation of the whole. In analytic knowledge I make a given concept distinct. Synthetic knowledge gives the concept together with the distinctness. The mathematician makes concepts together with their distinctness. The philosopher makes concepts distinct. In giving the concept, the mathematician at the same time specifies all the partial marks of which it consists. Hence it is evident that the making distinct of objects takes place synthetically, the making distinct of concepts analytically. In synthetic [distinctness] I add new marks to the concept of an object through experience. It arises in such a way that the parts of the knowledge precede the whole knowledge. In analytic [distinctness] the whole precedes the parts. Virtue, for example, I cannot define synthetically. For I am to say what we all think under the concept of virtue, not what I perhaps understand by it according to my own caprice.",[]],"wiener-s04-111":["Begreiffen, that is, to know through concepts, the author entitles *concipere*. This, however, is not quite right. For we have no expression in German for *comprehendere*, that is, to know through intuition *per apprehensionem*. The degrees of distinctness are",[]],"wiener-s04-112":["1.) The lowest degree is to represent something to oneself. When I know what refers to the object, I represent the object to myself.",[]],"wiener-s04-113":["2.) To know [erkennen], *percipere*, is to represent something to oneself in comparison with other things, and to discern its identity with or difference from them. It is thus to know something with consciousness. For animals also know their master, but they are not conscious of it.",[]],"wiener-s04-114":["3.) To understand something, *intelligere*, is to know something in the understanding, not merely with consciousness. The understanding is the faculty of concepts. Consequently to know something through concepts is to understand something, *concipere*.",[]],"wiener-s04-115":["4.) To have insight into something [einsehen] is to know through reason; not merely to understand, but [to know] according to such concepts as are universal in their determination. For example, [to know] not merely what a house is, but also the usefulness, arrangement, etc. of the house. – How few there are who have insight into something, even though they understand it. The maker of gunpowder, for example, knows that it has a propellant force, and that it has been produced in such and such a way. Very many attain to understanding. This is because they possess certain rules of the understanding. But they have no insight into it: *non perspiciunt*.",[]],"wiener-s04-116":["5.) *Comprehendere*, to comprehend [begreiffen], is to have sufficient insight into how far something serves a certain purpose. [To comprehend] without qualification is when I know from reason that it is sufficient for every purpose. But in this way we can know nothing. The mathematician has insight into the properties of a thing for many purposes, but not into their absolute sufficiency. Relatively, however, one may well have such sufficiency. – This comprehending is a very delicate matter. Men must really comprehend what they learn, at least for the purpose for which it is comprehensible; for example, in matters of religion, the mysteries of nature, and morals. For if a man does not have sufficient insight into them for his purpose, he can make no use of them. We must not, therefore, at once speak of the incomprehensible, even though it is not possible to know something for a speculative purpose. There is in us a peculiar use of reason, in which reason seems to us sufficient for insight into something once the thing is given, so that we persuade ourselves that we could also have had insight into it through our reason. But when we ask ourselves whether we could also have known it *a priori*, it nevertheless appears that we could not have done so. To this belong all experiments in natural science. Reason is indeed sufficient to comprehend what I have determined *a priori*. I can comprehend an eclipse of the moon, for example, because I know the course of the moon. But if I did not know that saltpetre has such a propellant force, could I have known beforehand that gunpowder would burst in this way, however well I might be able to define or to manufacture the powder? In the end, when one goes into the matter, it is found that there are only a few things of which we can say that we have sufficient insight into them. Very many can explain the concept; but most are very far from comprehending. – Men have tried to demonstrate *a priori* what lies in experience. The property of the lever, for example, is very well demonstrated in mechanics. But such modes of knowledge always show something defective, from which it is evident that, had experience not given us this, we should never have arrived at it. What I cannot determine *a priori*, therefore, I cannot comprehend or have complete insight into. The refraction of a ray of light in water, for example, can now be explained, and reason can make things in experience quite comprehensible; but that we flatter ourselves that we could have had insight into such things *a priori* is false.",[]],"wiener-s04-117":["A concept is distinct whose marks are clear. Marks are subordinate or co-ordinate. Subordinate marks constitute a series; and when we proceed in the series of subordinate marks, this is *profunditas*, thoroughness. We here ask first: what is the immediate mark of body? Extension. What is extension? And so on, until we at last arrive at marks which constitute the fundamental concepts. Profundity is a progression in the series of subordinate marks until we reach its limits. In the co-ordination or aggregate of the marks of a concept there is no determinate limit, except when the concept is to become analytic. For example: virtue is a readiness in actions conformable to law; further, a readiness in actions that are suited to the idea of the highest good; further, mastery of the inclinations; and so on without end.",[]],"wiener-s04-118":["The distinct concept which contains all the marks that, taken together, constitute the whole concept is a complete [ausführlicher] concept; and this perfection, which contains all the marks taken together, is entitled *completudo*. In all knowledge, accordingly, we attain a completeness, namely that all the marks are present which constitute the whole concept. – Even when no mark is lacking to a distinct concept, there may still have been an oversight in respect of it, namely that one or more marks are superfluous. But in a mode of knowledge through which my understanding is to be set in its proper activity, we wish to have all the marks, but not too many. [Superfluous marks are] a needless waste of the attention of the understanding. This perfection, in so far as the concept does not abound in marks, is precision. The author entitles only what is determinate precise, or as it were cut off [beschnitten]. Mathematics is most excellent on account of its precision. – The perfection that arises from *completudo* and precision united is adequate perfection. *Quando adaequatur objecto*, knowledge is adequate when it contains neither less nor more than is contained in the object. Precision in a concept might be entitled logical elegance. Precision is properly only a negative perfection, in that I remove whatever marks are superfluous in a concept. Wolff calls a mode of knowledge adequate in so far as it has profundity and intensive distinctness, and in so far as it is distinct through subordinate marks whose marks are in turn clear. We say: *notae simul sumtae adaequant conceptus*, and indeed [the marks must be] correct. For the Wolffians, [knowledge] is already adequate when the marks of the marks are clear; whereas this is not sufficient, because I can again make the marks of the marks distinct. The Wolffian school has therefore an unsettled concept here. Precision is a perfection [that enables us] to appraise our modes of knowledge and to become certain that there are exactly neither too many nor too few marks in them. This exactness serves as a guarantee. For whoever says less than he ought here will say more than he ought there. Lack of precision rests on tautologies, that is, on my positing a mark twice or in different ways. For example: God is a being who is omnipotent, infinite, etc. Here omnipotence already lies in the infinite. There are cases where precision cannot suitably be sought, and where it causes indistinctness, although for a capable mind it provides all the more distinctness. In the catechism, for example, one may well say a thing twice; for of two words that mean the same, one is often easier than the other. – The sum of the marks that are complete and measured [abgemeßen] in a given concept is suited to the concept, adequate. Among experts lack of precision is a great fault. But whoever renounces the demands of those who understand, and wishes to teach only those who do not, may relax somewhat in precision. – The author now treats of the clear head. Many can reflect thoroughly upon things, but cannot give them lucidity [Helligkeit], that is, popularity. Scholastic perfection consists in the agreement of knowledge with the rules mentioned above, namely that it be adequate to the concept. This is logical or scholastic perfection, that which is determined by the rules of the understanding. Popular perfection is aesthetic distinctness; and something becomes aesthetically distinct through examples, in that it is presented through intuition. The more I can combine my abstract concepts with representations *in concreto*, the more popular my knowledge becomes. Popularity, therefore, is where knowledge *in abstracto* is combined with knowledge *in concreto*, and so understanding with sensibility. Scholastic perfection, however, is always the chief thing. There are authors who are very popular, but if logical perfection is lacking they are unreliable, however engaging they may be. – What is intelligible even to the commonest man has the greatest degree of that perfection which is aesthetic. What is clear to the commonest man is certainly also clear to the speculative one. This highest degree of popularity must have place in religion and morals. But in this logical perfection must in no way be neglected. For it is the foundation through which a mode of knowledge has place as knowledge, and is entitled to be adopted by the understanding. For many purposes only a certain degree of logical perfection is required, and the lowest degree of logical perfection is the highest degree of popularity. The moralist must by no means neglect the distinctness of concepts; but he must also have examples at hand, and, as a good anthropologist, represent human nature and show the agreement of his teachings with it. – When these two perfections, combined, can be grasped even by the commonest understanding, this is the greatest degree of lucidity, which is impossible for many men. Many a man can know the rules *in abstracto*, but lacks the facility of wit and the strength of imagination to choose the most penetrating [tiefesten] examples for the insight of others. Of the Certainty of Knowledge. There are three degrees of holding-to-be-true [Fürwahrhalten]. Holding-to-be-true and truth are distinguished as subjective and objective. If holding-to-be-true is subjectively incomplete, it is entitled persuasion. A subjectively complete holding-to-be-true is conviction: the state of mind in which I give assent to a mode of knowledge, [that is,] holding-to-be-true. This is the phenomenon of the human soul which is here reasoned about, and not whether the knowledge is objectively true.",[]],"wiener-s04-119":["The three degrees of holding-to-be-true are opining, believing, and knowing.",[]],"wiener-s04-120":["Opining is not yet conviction; for otherwise it would have to be at least subjectively sufficient for me in the state of mind in which I find myself. In opining our judgment is problematic, that is, I decide nothing, but have only a degree of holding-to-be-true, although that degree is not sufficient. Opining has nothing harmful in it, provided only that I am conscious of the insufficiency of my holding-to-be-true.",[]],"wiener-s04-121":["Opining is a holding-to-be-true on a ground of knowledge that is neither objectively nor subjectively sufficient. If I hold something to be true, for example that there is a passage through the Arctic Sea, but am conscious that my knowledge of the matter is still wavering and insufficient in respect of the object, it is an opinion. Where this opinion does not satisfy even myself, and I should not care to wager anything on it, it is also subjectively insufficient. Brockes says: man is an animal that opines; the philosopher, a man who holds his opinion to be true. Opinions make up the greatest part of our knowledge. The first thing in a mode of knowledge is always a will, an obscure foreseeing, which produces in us a holding-to-be-true. In some sciences it is not permitted to opine at all. In matters of duty, for example, one must not opine but know. On mere opinion regarding a mode of knowledge one cannot venture to do anything.",[]],"wiener-s04-122":["In speculations one may perhaps opine something. In metaphysics opinions are spoken of in so far as they can be brought to the touchstone of experience; for example, regarding pure spirits. Here it is shown that, since there would be no end to opinion in this field, it is not permitted to opine. About possibilities one must not opine; they must be certain. Suppose one speaks of how we live after death. Can I opine here? No; I must know, or believe. Here nothing can be settled through experience, nor, unfortunately, *a priori*. What can be proved *a priori* one must not opine but know. Whatever can in any way be settled by reason I must either know or believe; opining counts for nothing here. – When I say that I opine, I have to make a claim to knowing. In opining I always take a step towards knowing. For [opinion] is an insufficient ground, to which complements must be added in order to make it complete. I here think a *complementum ad totum*, a piece supplementing it to the whole. When a mode of knowledge is of such a kind that knowing is impossible for it, opining is impossible also. For example, from the need of my reason I see that men must have been created by a rational being. For men are born of their parents, and so on; yet there must have been a first, who was born of no man. This is only a belief; for the grounds here are subjective. Why do I assume this? Because my reason requires it. No knowing is possible here, and therefore no opining. Similarly, it is impossible that man should know *a posteriori* the continuance of his soul after death. Here again it is belief. Since everything in nature is purposive, and man has speculative reason as a need, through which he is distinguished from the other animals in that he asks after the first cause, after [the cause of] motion, after the sun, etc., [and since] he cannot here with his reason fathom the first germs to their final development (for life is too short, and in old age reason no longer has its proper strength), there must therefore be yet another place where human reason can attain its proper development. So runs the belief. –",[]],"wiener-s04-123":["In believing we judge assertorically, that is, we declare ourselves for the truth, although it is subjectively sufficient only for ourselves and we cannot convince everyone else of it. I can have a subjective ground for holding something to be true, and this ground may be constant; yet it is not sufficient for me at all times. My holding-to-be-true is restricted to the situation in which I find myself, for I may come to better insight. This [belief] is therefore only relative. There is, however, also a belief which cannot be entitled theoretical belief; it is a practical belief, in which I cannot alter my holding-to-be-true at all. It is therefore fully as great as conviction, and is the greatest apodeictic certainty. This practical belief is moral belief. It consists in my assuming, in accordance with my moral disposition, something which is necessarily connected with that disposition and which is regarded as wholly unalterable. That there is a God, for example, I can indeed see from the purposiveness and order in the world. Yet much also seems contrary to purpose. For this, speculative grounds of that kind are not sufficient; morality alone is. But if I am to convince someone of my belief, it is presupposed that he has the same moral dispositions. Theoretical belief is indeed not unchangeable [wandelbar; the sense requires 'is changeable'] if it is not supported by moral belief. For I may come to better insight and meet with something adverse in nature, and then my holding-to-be-true ceases. – To accept propositions of reason on belief is admissible in one way only, namely in mathematics, because owing to its evidence one is not easily mistaken, and one at once discerns the error. In the philosophical sciences, however, it is absurd to accept asserted propositions without proof. In history too I can have knowing, if it is of such a kind that it can be communicated to everyone; for example, that London lies in England, because we hear the same thing by every post and from so many people who agree.",[]],"wiener-s04-124":["Believing is a holding-to-be-true that is subjectively sufficient but objectively insufficient. Sense is always subjective. Knowing is a holding-to-be-true that is objective [and held] with consciousness. Whoever undertakes something must, according to his opinion, venture something. This belief can be a wholly rational belief, and I can subjectively have so sufficient a ground for holding something to be true that I can take my decision [Erkenntnißentschließung] in accordance with it. Sowing, setting sail, etc., for example, are based on this. One believes that the corn will come up, that the east wind will blow at such and such a time, and the west wind at such another. Yet one cannot be certain of any of these things, and has only subjective grounds of holding-to-be-true. But although I may on my own account hold many things to be true, even if they are not objectively so certain, I can never have a [merely] subjective ground of holding-to-be-true in morals; objective truth must always prevail there. The judge can condemn no one to death before, by someone's testimony, he has deserved it and no contrary case is possible. That a man has perhaps been found covered in blood is indeed subjectively, but not objectively, sufficient for holding-to-be-true. A man can hold something to be true on a very rational ground, and yet of the very thing he holds to be true he cannot say that he knows it. – In knowing, it is not the ground of holding-to-be-true that matters; for whoever believes something perhaps holds it to be true with greater attachment than one who knows it. What matters is the right kind of holding-to-be-true. Many cling to practical truths with much zeal, even though there is no logical truth in them. – The wager is a test by which one tries whether the other also holds what he believes to be true, and knows it, with firm belief. Whoever will not wager wishes to assert something boldly without its costing him anything. Often a man will wager only a ducat [ Textlücke ]. Two [ducats] already deter many from the wager. Whoever wagers life and limb is completely convinced subjectively. And there are such cases. For example, whoever lets certain advantages of life go and lives a holy life because he knows he will be rewarded has a strong belief. When a man believes so strongly that he gives his life for it, this is as good as the greatest certainty. But one cannot say that he knows it; he only believes it, as, for example, in the case of a mystery of religion. In mathematics there is no place for belief; there must be knowing. For it would be ridiculous if someone said: I believe that a triangle is a figure consisting of three sides and three included angles. — [ Textlücke: AA: Der Glaube an ] the truth is firm only practically, and its ground is only subjective. He attains to knowing through the conviction of his understanding, [a conviction holding] for the most indifferent understanding, whereby he becomes able to make what he knows distinct and certain to others also. Accordingly knowing is not indeed necessary for the very firmest conviction. But one knows only when one can give the same certainty to everyone, even to the most indifferent understanding. In short, we can state the difference between believing and knowing thus: opining is a holding-to-be-true that is subjectively and objectively insufficient; believing, a holding-to-be-true that is subjectively sufficient but objectively insufficient. Believing is therefore the opposite of opining; knowing is a holding-to-be-true that is sufficient both objectively and subjectively.",[]],"wiener-s04-125":["When a holding-to-be-true [*Fürwahrhalten*] concerns only what is subjective, without regard to the object, by reference to which another person could also be convinced, I say that I am persuaded. Of my subjective grounds of holding-to-be-true I can give no account; they are valid only for me and for no one else. When I say that I cannot persuade myself of the matter, this holds only for me. I quite see that another, who does not know things so well and cannot so well distinguish them, might well be persuaded. Every language has marks of sincerity, from which an honest man at once believes that the matter is meant from the heart. But I cannot convey to another how what is truthful in the tone and in the words affects me, and what moves me to hold [the thing] to be true; I say that I am persuaded of it. When I say that I am convinced, this too is still a subjective ground, for it concerns only the subject. But there is here also a subjective necessity, and so a ground must after all lie in the object; otherwise my state might well change. There is something in my holding-to-be-true which lets me believe that I shall never depart from it. I believe that I find a ground in the object, even though I cannot make it distinct. Who is most easily persuaded? Children. For their judgments are merely subjective: they believe what they wish. Their knowledge seeks to extend itself, and inexperience therefore brings it about that they accept what pleases them, and do not even inquire into what the understanding requires. But people can also be persuaded who are preoccupied [*praeoccupirt*] by something; that is, one opinion has gained a footing in a man, and another now no longer finds the place empty. Men are very commonly preoccupied. For the judgments of our youth come first, and later ones therefore no longer find room. The understanding cannot entirely clear them away again, since it is difficult to achieve anything against all impressions; just as in law [*im Jure*] the *prior occupans* is always the most fortunate. To preoccupation belongs predilection [*praedilection*]: when out of affection one is too favourably inclined to someone, when what our patrons, friends, etc. have asserted flows into our judgments, so that we hold these judgments to be objective. When a man gives assent to such a judgment, he does not know what really determines him, whether understanding, idea or sensibility. Sensibility can exert only a mixed effect, together with the understanding. If only we could always distinguish what sensibility and what understanding do, the influence of sensibility would do us no harm. But if the judgment rests upon preoccupation, these influences are hard to detect. In holding-to-be-true, then, the hardest question is whence it arises. If sensibility has a large effect, the matter is not on that account to be rejected; for the understanding also has its share in it, and knows grounds enough as regards the object. But even if our assent has for the most part objective grounds, we must nevertheless be distrustful, especially when men are divided over the matter; and we must then inquire which of the two opinions may have subjective grounds. How, then, can conviction and persuasion be distinguished? We distinguish the two even by feeling. One says: I am persuaded [*persuadirt*] that this story cannot have happened so; so far as I can judge from the course of things, I must declare myself against it. I am convinced when the matter is so logically perfect that I can communicate it to another. If, when I imagine myself in all dangers, I should still hold the matter to be true [I am convinced]; were I then to waver, I should not really be convinced. But if I believe I risk nothing even when I stake my whole interest as a pledge, then I am convinced.",[]],"wiener-s04-126":["When, therefore, we opine [*meinen*], this is a mode of knowledge which has not even subjective grounds [sufficient]; it is the beginning of holding-to-be-true. Belief [*Glaube*] already signifies a holding-to-be-true that is subjectively sufficient, which does not appear to be objectively sufficient, but which yet has various grounds determined in the understanding. Belief always presupposes conviction, for there is something firm in it. When someone says, ‘I believe that this could happen’, he is merely opining, and this is only a confusion of words; for he would also gladly let himself be taught otherwise. The cause of belief, for example in the hope of another world, is that no one else can prove to us the opposite, and its impossibility, while we have a ground for it in the understanding, for instance the unequal distribution of good and evil. The subjective ground, however, which is based on morality, cannot be added to the objective ground. That belief is firm which carries a reasonable man so far that he sets the advantages of his life below it. He whom duty and hope together impel to renounce all advantages believes and is convinced. This holding-to-be-true does not yield to the very greatest certainty as regards its effect on the subject, and practical conviction is the highest possible. This practical conviction may fall upon certain propositions, and these are then morally certain propositions. They are the ground of all morality, and accord with our greatest conscientiousness when we live in conformity with them, and so subordinate our actions to them. As regards the belief that the apparent disharmony of good and evil will cease, everyone can see that no one will rob him of it; and I therefore ask: have I ground enough to accept it? Yes, a practical ground, but not a logical one. Yet it is very firmly bound up with reason. For [in face of it] the greatest mathematician could establish nothing, since I would not for my life let myself be robbed of this belief.",[]],"wiener-s04-127":["[This holds] even though this conviction serves not for speculative but only for practical convictions. For example, the concept of God is given not for speculation but for practical exercise. If, then, we so lower the claims of certain propositions which men pass off as a science that we grant them practical but not logical conviction, we have taken nothing from them; we have only taken from men the self-conceit of believing that they can have insight [into them] through speculative grounds. The practical grounds thereby appear in a clearer light, since men are led back to their interest. Sensible certainty is divided into certainty of the senses and empirical certainty. The senses alone do not yet yield experience; experience is the judgment of the understanding upon the connection of our sensible representations. Sensible certainty is accordingly nothing but the certainty of the known relation of the senses. Senses and understanding make up all our modes of knowledge [*Kenntniße*]. The senses give appearance. The understanding connects appearances, and this yields experience; experience is therefore the known connection, or unity, of appearances. Men must therefore connect intuitions objectively in order to be able to say anything of them. There are logicians who speak only of the certainty of the senses: whether our sensible representations are in themselves true, that is, whether they have objects, or whether they are for the most part only a certain sense corresponding to the object. For example, we dream, and hold this to be just as true as when we are awake. Whether such representations are real or ideal as regards the object, whether they are as they seem to us, or give us only the manner in which the senses are affected, or are mere illusions of our imagination: this question belongs not to logic but to metaphysics, where the origin of illusion [*Schein*] and of truthfulness from sense is discussed. In the end, it comes to this, that there is neither truth nor falsity in the senses, and that both lie only in the use which our understanding makes of sensible representations. For the senses do not judge at all; and they therefore contain no errors, not because they never judge falsely, but because they do not judge at all. The question here, then, can concern only the certainty of empirical knowledge. This is a knowledge of objects through the understanding, in so far as they are presented to the understanding. All empirical certainty concerns only the relation of sensible representations, how certain representations can be compared with one another. This sensible certainty we distinguish from rational knowledge. The latter can be distinctly expressed in two ways.",[]],"wiener-s04-128":["1.) A knowledge of reason [*Vernunft-Erkenntniß*], when its object is a mere object of reason.",[]],"wiener-s04-129":["2.) A rational knowledge [*vernünftige Erkenntniß*] concerns the form in which I know an object through reason, whether its object be empirical or rational.",[]],"wiener-s04-130":["Some modes of knowledge are merely objects of reason, for instance morals; these are material rational modes of knowledge. Metaphysics treats of the character of rational knowledge as regards its object. Logic treats of the second kind, namely the formal. Our knowledge may comprehend objects of experience, and yet our certainty may be either empirical or rational. For example, the proposition concerning the power [*Potenz*] obtained by means of the lever has empirical certainty, but also rational certainty; for even if experience taught us nothing of it, I should still be able to know it through reason. That certain things are unequal experience can tell us; but that they must necessarily be so, reason teaches. Reason thus heightens certainty, in that it represents the knowledge as necessary. Sometimes we can have no rational certainty, and must then content ourselves with empirical certainty. This is especially so in natural science. But knowledge of which we can obtain rational certainty is the more excellent. In matters at which we should never have arrived had we not learned them from experience, the certainty of experience is the best. But if we know grounds which are necessary, and through which we could also have foreseen the matter, rational certainty has the advantage. All our knowledge is either from concepts or from the construction of concepts. The former is entitled discursive, the latter intuitive; and so all rational certainty, too, is either discursive or intuitive. Rational certainty, in so far as it is intuitive, is mathematical certainty; in so far as it is discursive, philosophical certainty. Mathematical certainty is thus distinguished from philosophical. Both are certain, and both are known through reason; but the one certainty is discursive, the other intuitive.",[]],"wiener-s04-131":["A moral proposition and a mathematical proposition are both apodeictically certain, that is, they are bound up with the consciousness of necessity, in contrast to empirical propositions, which are only assertoric. Both can accordingly convince completely, although they are distinguished as discursive and intuitive. Mathematical certainty is therefore entitled evident, manifest [*augenscheinlich*]; that is, its objects are presented not *in abstracto* through concepts but in a single intuition. But when the question concerns the degree of certainty, philosophical propositions cannot be changed into mathematical ones. Some come very near to intuitions, but are still distinct from them. Wolff committed the error of seeking to judge discursively in mathematics, for example in his definition of the similarity of triangles. The proposition ‘Do right’ need not be constructed, for it is already clear. Conversely, by no concept could one arrive at the proof that 2+2=4. Philosophical and mathematical certainty can therefore not be confused at all. Our author now speaks of the *modi judicii veritatis et falsitatis*, that is, of the modes of holding-to-be-true.",[]],"wiener-s04-132":["1.) A mode of knowledge is said to be generally accepted [*durchgängig angenommen*] when it meets with assent from everyone, although it is not yet in itself certain. This is thus a merely external mark, from which certainty cannot yet be inferred.",[]],"wiener-s04-133":["2.) A mode of knowledge is settled [*ausgemacht*]; that is, it is accepted as a universally acknowledged truth.",[]],"wiener-s04-134":["3.) A mode of knowledge is undeniable, indisputable: it is impossible to doubt its truth.",[]],"wiener-s04-135":["A mode of knowledge is settled in a twofold sense: first, when it is objectively certain, that is, indisputable; or again subjectively, when it has been agreed that it is to be acknowledged as certain. In natural science there are many settled propositions, but also a multitude of merely accepted ones. Propositions that are beyond doubt are not entitled settled, because there can no longer be any dispute about them; for example, mathematical propositions. Against some problems of physics objections can still be found. Since knowledge of nature is only contingent, one may not claim so apodeictic a necessity for one's judgment. A mode of knowledge is entitled thorough [*gründlich*] when it is adequate to the nature of that knowledge in general.",[]],"wiener-s04-136":["Thorough modes of knowledge differ according to the difference of their nature. A moral discourse is thorough in another way than a physical one. Thoroughness is not a logical perfection, or completeness of certainty, but only that perfection which is appropriate to the nature of the knowledge in question. A man may be a very thorough teacher of his congregation if he adapts the truths to the capacity of comprehension of his hearers; and this very discourse might be less thorough for doctors of theology, if it were meant as a test-piece in which regard would be had to those principles which, in accordance with its nature, must be laid at the basis of such knowledge.",[]],"wiener-s04-137":["Shallow [*seichte*] modes of knowledge signify something other than superficial ones. Shallowness indicates a lack of depth in knowledge: one does not go out into the deep water with one's knowledge, but stays close to the shore, and does not pursue a mode of knowledge back to its first grounds. Certain modes of knowledge float, like stubble, on the surface, but are comprehensible to the common understanding and suited to their purpose; these are shallow modes of knowledge. If one wishes to have a mode of knowledge which is meant to go beyond the limits of the common understanding, for instance if one wished to be instructed in the precise subtleties which reason must possess in metaphysics, it would be superficial if it were suited only to the common understanding. If I wished to impart to someone merely the first concepts of metaphysics, that there is a God and an immortal soul, I should be giving him a shallow knowledge, but one suited to its purpose. A mode of knowledge is also entitled superficial when someone knows the titles of books without himself understanding anything of the science.",[]],"wiener-s05-001":["From our expressions, ‘I accept this’, ‘I concede this’, or ‘I withhold my assent’, we can see that there must be something in our assent which is voluntary [*willkührlich*], where we must determine ourselves whether we will hold the knowledge to be true or not. We shall therefore inquire how far our judgment here depends on the will. The understanding can subject itself to our will on no other ground than in so far as it is put to use. Otherwise its conviction rests on grounds, not on resolutions. Now since the will is a great cause through which certain convictions arise, the will has an indirect influence upon the understanding. If we could at once convince ourselves through the understanding of whatever we gladly wished, everyone would make for himself, through the understanding, chimeras as happy as he could, provided, that is, that these chimeras produced by the will did not have to give way to refutation by the understanding. Certain modes of knowledge",[]],"wiener-s05-002":["are such that, even though we find nothing faulty in the motives [for assent], and nothing suspicious in them, we are nevertheless distrustful in our judgment on account of the resemblance of this knowledge to other knowledge in which we have often erred. This is *suspensio judicii*: the resolve not to let a provisional judgment become a determining one. There may well be grounds here for holding-to-be-true which are more certain than the grounds of the opposite. We can have grounds and yet not hold them to be determinative; and in this way we distinguish the provisional from the determined judgment. This withheld assent can take two forms. *Suspensio judicii indagatoria* is when one still means to seek out the grounds of the determined judgment. This is properly a postponement of judgment, in order meanwhile to inquire further into the matter. Or there is *renunciatio judicii*, when we renounce our judgment and give up all hope of determining anything. Scepticism renounces all judgments in respect of various things, for example whether the soul is corporeal. The philosopher holds that this matter is not yet decided, and suspends his judgment.",[]],"wiener-s05-003":["To withhold one's assent is a capacity of a practised power of judgment. It is not found in beginners and in youth, but only with advancing age. For our faculty of knowledge is so eager to spread itself that, as soon as an occasion offers, it seeks with the greatest impatience to extend itself. This propensity to accept what is plausible [*das scheinbare*] as true is so great in young minds that they find it very hard to withhold their judgment. But when they see how one can be led into passing a judgment that one must retract, that is, when they have been made wary by much experience, they suspend [judgment] more. To withhold one's judgment, and to maintain oneself long in that state, is a difficult act, even for one who has a strong power of judgment. For man has a propensity at every moment to accept one of two propositions as true. [This suspension] is in man almost never so complete that he should be entirely indifferent; and even when he withholds [his judgment], he still always inclines more to the one side than to the other. One often withholds one's judgment so as not to need later to retract it, the more so the more one lays claim to the honour of well-founded insight, in so far as one's reputation might suffer by it. The greater authors are, the more cautious they are in their expressions on matters still in controversy, and they put forward their propositions problematically,",[]],"wiener-s05-004":["not assertorically. This withholding is always a precaution [*Zurückhaltung davor*] arising from one's setting a distrust in one's judgments, however well-founded. Distinct from *suspensio judicii* is the case where I do not treat of a matter at all, since its decision does not belong to my purpose; I then leave something *in dubio*. This is *judicium anceps*, and is childish; whereas *suspensio judicii* is manly, and is found in a man of experience. To the [AA: judicium ] an [ Textlücke: AA: -ceps ] is opposed the judicium [AA: praeceps ], which is still worse. This, again, is something other than the suspending [judgment]. When I suspend, I have not grounds enough for a determined judgment. I leave it *in dubio* when whether it is true or not is of no concern to my purpose. Provisional modes of knowledge make up so large a science that it could be a very useful chapter for logic, were it not too deep for logic to decide, for instance, how provisional judgments can be made into determined ones. That requires the most exact acquaintance with the objects themselves. Logic, therefore, cannot speak adequately of the nature, use, limits, requisites and conditions of provisional knowledge. That would rather become an *organon* of philosophy, which we do not yet possess at present. Every man judges provisionally in what he first judges, for instance from the face. The first appearance, bearing and dress that someone has in company must serve to judge him [*muß ihn beurtheilen*], though this is not sufficient. But the provisional judgment commonly makes, just because it is the first, so strong an impression on a man that the determined judgments afterwards, with the strongest grounds, cannot efface the provisional ones. We judge provisionally of a book by its title. Provisional judgments can, however, gradually come closer to determined judgments. In meditating, provisional judgments must come first. I take this along with me, and believe that it fits into the connection. Afterwards, on surveying the whole, the determined judgments may turn out quite differently. Our understanding provisionally detects something of what it is itself first to discover. We accept the truth, and have a scent of the key to our knowledge. Our understanding is thus directed by them [the provisional judgments], in that they give us the means of attaining the end of knowledge.",[]],"wiener-s05-005":["Determined is that which is sufficient for our assent. A provisional judgment must not be held to be determined. In refutation, the opponent may have passed a good provisional judgment. One must then, by modesty, bring him to suspend his judgment.",[]],"wiener-s05-006":["Provided only that we are conscious that it is a provisional judgment, we may always judge. *Praevia judicia*, that is, provisional judgments, are of great importance, since in them one has certain maxims for the provisional investigation of a matter. In every matter one must always judge something beforehand. For example, in searching for metal in mines I must already have a provisional ground why I dig in precisely this mine. In meditating we must judge provisionally where the truth may lie. A logic which could set out the maxims of provisional judgments would be of great importance; it would really be a heuristic of new truths, but it has not yet been discovered. One judges provisionally when, before judging determinately, one has certain grounds for directing one's investigation to the one object rather than the other. In the survey of objects, in which I seek a future knowledge, I judge something so that I can afterwards make trials of it. Before all discovery, investigation must be undertaken; for by pure luck one finds nothing without tracking and without guidance. As soon as one meditates on something, one already makes plans, whose execution is preceded by a certain half-formed judgment about the properties that are yet to be discovered. If all this were illustrated by examples of discoverers, showing how they had hit upon this or that through provisional judgments, it would give guidance for arriving at the discovery of further modes of knowledge. The judge in criminal law could make use of this as a matter of great importance. Provisional judgments are also entitled *anticipationes*, where one suspends one's determined judgment. One has not yet resolved upon determining judgments, and so in a certain measure anticipates them. Epicurus called anticipation the concepts of our understanding which he presupposes prior to experience. From these provisional judgments prejudices are very greatly distinct; they are exactly their opposite. For [in them], without judging provisionally, or instead of so judging, one determines. One must distinguish investigating and reflecting [*untersuchen und überlegen*]. Some propositions can be accepted without investigation, for instance whether the whole is greater than its parts. Although here we need not distinguish [*unterscheiden*; perhaps read *untersuchen*], it is nevertheless necessary to reflect,",[]],"wiener-s05-007":["that is, to seek out the connection of a mode of knowledge with the power of knowledge from which it is supposed to spring. Our faculty of knowledge is manifold, and all its powers are in play. They all run together, and each acts upon the object suited to it; and we always seek out the appropriate faculty of knowledge for it without its giving us any trouble. For example, the proposition that between two points only one straight line is possible is laid before the understanding for reflection, and intuition tests whether only the one line can here be drawn. This must always be done, even with modes of knowledge which seem not to need investigation. For if a mode of knowledge springs from the influence of a power of knowledge which has no validity whatever in respect of this object, the knowledge must be either rejected or suspended. I must see whether the senses, or the understanding, or imagination are here in play. For all errors rest on this, that sensibility flows into the understanding. When one believes that one has something through the understanding, and sensibility has had a secret influence upon it, errors arise. It is commonly said that nothing comes from nothing. We must have a material from which we make modes of knowledge. This matter we cannot ourselves produce. Everything must therefore come from experience; and so nothing is ever evident [as] if reason alone had made it. If you wish to convince yourself through the understanding alone, whence will you take [the material]? The reflection [*Ueberzeugung*; perhaps read *Ueberlegung*] here described is necessary even where investigation seems impossible, for instance with mathematical propositions. We accept many judgments without conviction and without attending to the power of knowledge which has an influence upon the judgment, and from this prejudices arise. Prejudice is the mechanism of reason in principles. A prejudice is a principle of judging from subjective causes which are taken for objective grounds. Subjective causes all lie in sensibility; objective grounds lie in the understanding. Accordingly, because sensibility has an influence, the judgment cannot be true. A prejudice is therefore a principle of judgment, reason in principles. I cannot say that it is a prejudice when, for example, a man says of his son that he has a good disposition but is led astray by others, although the son is in fact a bad man; rather, this springs from a prejudice. Erroneous judgments arising from prejudices are not themselves prejudices; genuine prejudices are therefore a source of many false judgments, being their ground and principle. In the example given, the proposition is taken from a principle, and yet has the effect of a principle. With a prejudice one must always reflect: where does this come from? For example, ‘the apple does not fall far from the tree’ might be called a prejudice, because it is a universal proposition and so a principle. Yet one might also say that it has arisen from older prejudices which children have regarding their parents. All prejudices are principles of judging; from them, therefore, a judgment must arise. Someone has found a proposition asserted in such and such a way by Leibniz. He now holds it to be well-founded, although he has no insight whatever into the proof. This is not a prejudice, but the inclination [to judge] from a prejudice. Prejudices of acknowledged merit give rise to strong presumptions that everything said, for example, by Wolff, Leibniz, etc. will be meritorious. Prejudice is a principle of judging not from objective grounds; when all the grounds of the judgment are drawn not from the object but from the subject, they are drawn from such sources. I may therefore only say to myself, ‘it seems so to me’, and not, ‘the object is so’. All grounds, then, ought properly to be objective. Objective grounds are opposed to subjective causes. With grounds I think only of something universal; with subjective causes a certain state in the man is at work. Habit brings it about that propositions to which we have long adhered easily become quite necessary to us, so that in the end we make of them a ground for judging; and thus one can make principles of judgment out of mere habits. For example, it is habitual to have a superstitious esteem for someone because he has always been so regarded in former times; from this there springs a principle of judging, that is, a propensity which has become our own through frequent exercise. From this it is evident how a principle arises from subjective causes. One judges the maxims of others critically, and holds one's own to be good. Here we have the prejudice *suum cuique pulchrum*, whose subjective cause is self-love. In the manners of others there is often nothing unseemly in itself, though we hold it to be so.",[]],"wiener-s05-008":["Here the understanding is merely misled, and takes these for objective grounds; and that then causes us to judge through the admixture",[]],"wiener-s05-009":["of sensibility. The chief sources of prejudices are accordingly subjective causes which are falsely held to be objective grounds. They serve, as it were, in place of principles, since prejudices must be principles. The chief sources of prejudices are, above all, imitation, habit and inclination.",[]],"wiener-s05-010":["The idea of them is a ground of judging from subjective causes; and each also counts as a principle of judging, that is, it is of universal influence. Thus:",[]],"wiener-s05-011":["1. Imitation. The human understanding seeks to extend itself; and in the ignorance found in children and the common man, it extends itself through the examples of others, by imitating the thinking of others. Imitation is thus a ground for holding to be true what another has said. For example: what all men assert must be true. To this head belong all proverbs.",[]],"wiener-s05-012":["2.) Habit is the second source of prejudices. It acts most strongly in the old, just as imitation occurs more in the young than in the old. Young people have not yet lived long enough to let habit become so important in them. But when someone is old, he cannot so easily take on another form. Indeed, habit acts so strongly in old people that they cannot be brought away from it at all, especially when it has arisen, besides, from imitation. Then the man is quite incurable, for imitation makes all thinking of one's own impossible. It brings it about that when, for example, an author wishes to put forward a new system in a science, he meets at the outset with many difficulties. For time must first pass before such a mode of knowledge stems the current of habits, gradually brings it to a standstill, and then gives it the opposite direction. Prejudices from habit, therefore, can be removed only by length of time.",[]],"wiener-s05-013":["3.) Inclination or aversion. Predilection [*Praedilection*], or inclination, is when, before one has investigated a matter, one is already favourably prepossessed in its behalf.",[]],"wiener-s05-014":["Imitation is most contrary to the free use of our reason, because here we early accustom ourselves to make use of the judgments of others instead of our own. The free use of reason may be divided into active and passive use; and judgments of imitation are a *habitus* of passive use. I use my reason actively when I myself derive something from the laws of nature. It is harmful to present certain men to the young as masters, so that they must repeat their language or expressions by rote. For from this arises a passive reason, and that is a *contradictio in adjecto*. All reason is by its nature a self-active principle of thought; and even when in thinking I take experience as basis, reason still makes universal rules of experience. The use of reason thus consists in self-activity. But men's laziness brings it about that they prefer to behave passively rather than raise their power of knowledge so far as to make use of their own powers. They accordingly have merely subjective and historical modes of knowledge, because they merely repeat by rote. Such conduct is a fertile soil for rank prejudices, and it hinders all improvement and progress in a matter; yet it is so universally prevalent, because it is so well suited to the human propensity to ease. Nothing, therefore, is more harmful than to accustom pupils to imitate the author, or rather to ape him.",[]],"wiener-s05-015":["Sentences [*Sentenzen*] and proverbs are a principle of imitation. Proverbs are expressions taken from common life and from the language of the rabble. The first source of proverbs is, as a rule, prejudices; for example, ‘The apple does not fall far from the tree’, and the like. Sentences are maxims [*Sinnsprüche*] which display wide reading. Since they therefore belong in some measure to learning, they are employed by those who wish to make a show and to display their learning. A formula is a rule whose very expression serves as the pattern of its application. By it we have only to come to the aid of the memory. Thus there are formulas in algebra, in grammar, in law [*Jus*], etc. etc. Even the greatest scholar makes use of such formulas. When he has first reflected upon the rules, he then makes formulas for himself, in order to proceed more easily in their subsequent application; so that, knowing the formulas, he knows at once all the acts [*actus*] which he has to observe. They are therefore of great importance; but mere imitation of formulas does not bring about such good results. – A saying [*Spruch*] is a proposition whose expression, in indicating its sense, has a certain measuredness [*Abgemeßenheit*]. When, therefore, an expression is pregnant, and in this full content there yet prevails such measuredness of expression that it seems impossible to state with fewer words a discourse that comprehends so much, that is a saying. Sayings serve as laws. Hence passages of Holy Scripture are entitled *dicta*. We cannot, therefore, give *dicta* from philosophers, as the disciples of Pythagoras, the *acusmatici*, did when they said: [ Textlücke: AA:αὐτὸς ἔφα. ]",[]],"wiener-s05-016":["Mechanism in matters of the understanding is in some respects of great assistance to the understanding, provided I have first thought the matter through for myself. But to accept things of this kind without conviction is also a fruitful source of errors. Such a mechanism prevails in formulas, that is, in universal propositions clothed in words that are quite precisely determined. Of this kind are",[]],"wiener-s05-017":["1.) proverbs, *formulae vulgares* [common formulas], which hold for the understanding of the common man. But to accept these without any examination is not advisable.",[]],"wiener-s05-018":["2.) Sentences, aesthetic formulas [*Aesthetische Formalien*], formulas of precept, in which a rule of conduct is contained. Such are found in poets, for instance Horace.",[]],"wiener-s05-019":["3.) *Canones*, formulas of science. Such are found in all the sciences, and they serve to enable the matter to be expounded more easily.",[]],"wiener-s05-020":["4.) *Dicta*. Formulas of belief in theological matters, where the words themselves are held sacred and are therefore accepted without any alteration whatsoever.",[]],"wiener-s05-021":["If all these have once taken root in me without mature reflection, they are a root of untold errors.",[]],"wiener-s05-022":["Sayings must therefore always be borrowed from someone else. – A sentence does not, indeed, always signify a *dictum*, but it resembles one in that it commends itself by the force of its thought. A sentence is [present] when what accords with reason, expressed in forceful words, commends itself to common sense [*sens commun*]. A saying is [present] when something seems to be drawn from the common understanding and yet expresses itself with a measuredness in the words belonging to it. *Bons mots* are universal propositions which are commended more by wit than by a rich power of judgment. –",[]],"wiener-s05-023":["A sentence has many advantages. For it serves as a foundation, and it is entertaining to the mind, since much is thought in a small content, and this little is suited to everyone's reason.",[]],"wiener-s05-024":["*Canones* are doctrinal sayings [*Lehrsprüche*], which have regard rather to the content of the expression. They are universal propositions which serve as foundations in the sciences. A canon can also be expressed by sentences, and it then pleases all the more. Proverbs and mottoes [*Denksprüche*] are the expressions most commonly used in the popular judgments of understanding and reason. Something may be an expression of sound understanding and yet not be popular; it is then a sentence, but not a proverb. Proverbs contain the current wit of the people, but expressed in a concise manner, so that it is distinguished from ordinary turns of speech. They reveal the common understanding of the people, and always have something provincial about them, for they are usually clothed in national images. They are *canones* for the commonest rabble. For they are the rules which took root at the very first formation [of the mind], and which therefore hold universally. Hence we also find that they have no place among people of refined education. But if someone in polite society brings forward a canon, for example from a classical author [*autor classicus*], it is very readily received. Prejudices are much tested [*geprüft*] when we seek to found wisdom upon sentences and proverbs, since we are always looking about to see what others have said. From this there always arise partisanships for the opinions of this or that famous man and philosopher. These were the prejudices from imitation. Habit shows its effect when we have long held one and the same way of thinking and are now suddenly required to depart from it.",[]],"wiener-s05-025":["He who would put forward anything new in religion, metaphysics, etc., has many difficulties to contend with. But time brings it about that a proposition, together with its grounds of proof, afterwards strikes the eye so clearly that people cannot but wonder how they could have failed to see it before. The first judgment upon a matter that is new is always made in accordance with the very prejudices which it was sought to root out by this matter. When we contend against a prejudice, it defends itself, as it were, of its own accord. For a man cannot so quickly be weaned from his attachment to some pronouncement; and it is with this attachment, not with reason, that we then have to deal. With reason the matter would go more easily. This lasts until at last the inclination becomes more general; then it is accepted by all. – The question arises: is it necessary and advisable for a teacher to leave prejudices untouched, or even to favour them, so that they may gradually take deeper root in the minds of his hearers? This is the same as asking: is it permissible to deceive people with a good intention? However good the intention may be, the means is still worse than the best intention that one can have.",[]],"wiener-s05-026":["We might, indeed, leave them untouched, since we may content ourselves with letting an error pass, provided only that we do not strengthen it. [Otherwise] we should merely bring a man out of his old habit, and could not so quickly accustom him [to a new one]. – Nevertheless it is advisable to expose all errors and prejudices when we are fully convinced of the correctness of our insight. For in the sequel people do see that we are right. We must therefore examine everywhere the procedure of men, and lay bare to them what they commit from prejudice. The disadvantage which we at first suffer from the opposition is amply made good. We have, indeed, a calling not to conceal [*cachiren*] but to promulgate [*promulgiren*] the universal commands of reason; and if we are silent, it seems as if we did not consider people worthy to know them. – Some men have no need of insight into certain things, for example into speculative matters, without which they can get along with a perfectly good conscience, and which have not the least influence upon their actions. But if their prejudices are of practical disadvantage, then put forward what you know better than they do, and believe that the disadvantage which you might suffer at the outset is smaller than the practical advantage to the others. We now come to positive and negative prejudices. We may, however, take both on the same footing, for with the negative we very soon understand the positive as well. They are prejudices of authority [*Ansehen*], of trust in the multitude, in the age of a people. In all these respects prejudices may prevail, that is, [in every case] where the senses run ahead of the understanding. Hence the understanding, which has been prepared for it beforehand, shows in its judgment a propensity [*propension*] to have regard, say, for the learning of a man, or, conversely, to place greater trust in the common understanding than in learning. Of this point we shall treat specially below. These prejudices are founded upon a propensity to assent to the opinion of others. All prejudices by which we imitate others, in that we found our prejudice upon the authority of other men, we may",[]],"wiener-s05-027":["entitle servile prejudices. There is, however, also a self-loving prejudice, when we regard the works of our own mind with a predilection [*praedilection*], and find the children of our understanding free from fault.",[]],"wiener-s05-028":["Prejudices of the authority of a person are servile prejudices. – In historical matters the authority of other persons is absolutely necessary, and in many cases constitutes the foundation of our prejudgments [*Vorurtheile*]. For things which rest on two testimonies I cannot work out from myself, since my own use of the understanding is not sufficient to determine the truth of history, which rests on experience. Here I may without prejudice build upon the reports of others; indeed in this case it is necessary. For since I cannot obtain them through my own experience, they must come from persons who stand in such authority with us that we give them credence. This acceptance of what another says is not founded on a prejudice; it is a condition sufficiently valid for our understanding to give it assent. But when knowledge has its ground of proof in nothing but reason, and does not take it from experience, the testimony of others cannot be a ground of conduct. Precisely this judgment, which runs ahead of our understanding and produces in us, without any examination, a propensity to hold something to be true, is the prejudice of authority: as when, for example, I should be inclined to believe in the immortality of the soul because Plato said so. Truth, in so far as it rests on reason, holds anonymously; that is, we must ask not who said it, but what he said. What matters here is not to what family the knowledge belongs, nor whether he who stated it was a man of great merit, but what he said. – Complaint is made that matters of state and religion have brought forth so many anonymous writings, in which arrows were forged against these matters. Certainly, if a man is in himself malicious, it is still worse when he acts anonymously. But as regards the matter itself, it must hold anonymously, however badly or well the man may conduct himself. When a noble and upright man says something bad, it is not on that account any less bad because he is upright. – The high esteem of a nation for a certain man is harmful because it brings people to idleness, so that they do not establish themselves firmly in their own knowledge, but let others hold sway over it. Locke says of this that the authority of great men serves in disputes, often *ad verecundiam*, when we wish to silence others with it because a great man has said it. This inclination towards the authority of great men arises from the habit of imitating what is prescribed to us as certain, and from want of insight, in that we are always seeking a guarantor upon whom we must be able to rely, who as it were has understanding for us, and to whom we can always appeal. – The prejudice of the authority of the person produces something peculiar in our inclinations, because it serves indirectly [*indirecte*] to flatter our vanity. The subjects of a mighty despot are proud, even though they live in the lowest stations, because they are all nothing in comparison with him, and one may tomorrow become as great as another is today. So too, when one man among others is exalted into a great scholar, this is often done only with the intention that he who praises him may be held equal to all other scholars. For since, after these eulogies, all others are nothing in comparison with the great man, the small difference among the others is not to be reckoned. It is thus envy of other men that leads someone to make a single man great. The person praised we no longer envy; but neither do we allow others to set themselves on a level with him. – Citations of learned and famous men cannot be regarded as grounds of proof. For the real question is always how much ground the assertion has in reason, and not who said it. Notwithstanding this, the inclination to cite other authors carries with it a certain weight in strengthening our assent to a matter. It is consequently an external criterion [*criterium*] of truth; that is, the agreement of universal human understanding is a ground for presuming that I shall have judged correctly. It is a kind of experiment [*Experience*] of the judgment upon more than one understanding. Yet here it often happens [*arrivirts*], for example in metaphysics, that men all labour under one and the same illusion [*Blendwerk*]. There is one science which has this peculiarity, that it cannot dispense with citations at all, namely law [*Jus*]. Here we see how uncertain all juristic judgments must be. Yet an original law [*ursprüngliches Recht*] could actually be conducted without citing. Only observe constantly, from experience, the disadvantage of this or that law. For the multiplicity of cases which may occasion the particular direction of the will can never be expressed quite determinately in a law. We must therefore take the aid of other eyes, since no one is so sharp-sighted as to examine every case exactly. If you have paid attention to the various outcomes of lawsuits, you will be in a position to decide more. – Citations always have something agreeable about them, namely where whole passages are quoted from other authors. It is undoubtedly the entertainment they afford that causes them to please us. Notwithstanding this, they must not serve as grounds of proof, since this is only an external criterion of truth.",[]],"wiener-s05-029":["Prejudice from the authority of the multitude is a prejudice either of sound understanding or of the learned. To it the rabble chiefly has a propensity, since with them the judgment of the multitude holds good. The rabble is not in a position to estimate the worth of an individual person, and therefore it holds to the multitude, but, *nota bene* [NB], not to every multitude. – Already through proverbs a prejudice of the multitude is adopted, in that it is presupposed that what all men say must surely be true. This, however, is only a historical judgment. But in matters of religion [the common man], since he does not venture to judge of them himself, trusts the learned, who count for us, as it were, as models in religion. This holds of all written religions. Here everyone believes his learned men, who study these books; and missionaries report of such religions that it is for this very reason difficult to make proselytes there. Here, then, the prejudice is founded upon a multitude of persons. It is, however, to be observed that what I accept from prejudice may also, by chance, be true. But that I accept it as a prejudice is false and logically reprehensible. Hence the knowledge is not blameworthy as though it were false, but because I have accepted it from prejudice. In respect of knowledge the common man has prejudices in favour of learning. But the scholar, again, has a prejudice in favour of the common estate. It is easy to see that the great mass always has a favourable prejudice towards the learned, and here it has, indeed, good ground. For the learned must track down knowledge and make it their chief business, whereas such things come in the way of the common man only on occasion. – Among the learned there is found a prejudice in favour of the common understanding, and a mistrust of the learned understanding. It arises in the scholar eager for knowledge when he sees that all his efforts fail to satisfy him. He believes that he has not taken the right road, and therefore turns back to the common understanding, because he believes he will find there the true key to a mode of knowledge that is obscure to him. This happens when he has pretty well traversed the circle of the sciences, and especially in those sciences which he could not render sensible without concepts, for example metaphysics and law. In natural science he has in no way a more favourable prejudice towards the common than towards the learned understanding, since he sees that here one can advance further by experiments. But as regards judgments on the existence of a supreme being or the nature of our soul, when the scholar has spent all his art, still does not satisfy himself, and finds so much that is merely customary [*Gebräuchliches*] in himself and in other scholars, he believes that the key lies hidden somewhere, and seeks it in the common understanding. But this belief is very deceptive. For how should these untrained eyes accomplish more than if they were cultivated? And how can such concepts of another world be brought to concepts? Nevertheless there are certain modes of knowledge which can be exhibited *in concreto*. For example, all moral questions can be given *in concreto*. Here the common use of the understanding judges more correctly than the speculative use of the understanding, because it has the matters before it in experience. In the speculative use, therefore, we must often call the common understanding to our aid, on account of the particular propositions which in the end arise from purely speculative ones. There are thus certain sciences in which merely speculative reason is not secure without determination [*Bestimmung*] by sound reason, and in which a certain preliminary opinion of the speculative understanding must probably mislead the warrant of the common understanding. For example, the jurists' proposition *damnum patitur dominus* [the owner bears the loss], examined by the common understanding, is highly inequitable. Hence the scholar cannot [but] take refuge in the common understanding in such cases. But this, then, is no prejudice on the part of the learned, only a method of putting his speculation to the test. For there are so many cases in the practical sciences which cannot possibly all be surveyed in speculation, and we must therefore take cases *in concreto* for the purpose.",[]],"wiener-s05-030":["Opposed to the prejudices of authority is logical egoism [*egoismus*], that is, the prejudice according to which we hold the agreement of our understanding with the reason of others to be impossible as a criterion [*criterium*] of wisdom. There are sciences in which we really,",[]],"wiener-s05-031":["without committing the fault of egoism, must often rely on our own reason, without testing our judgment by the reason of others and without needing this external criterion. For example, in mathematics the evidence [*evidenz*] is so great that no one can resist it, if only he follows the proofs set before him. But elsewhere this historical criterion of the assent of others cannot be wholly dispensed with. For though it is not by itself a criterion, it is nevertheless one criterion among others. For in discursive modes of knowledge through reason, where we set forth everything through concepts, the agreement of others can never be held to be dispensable, for the reason that errors are here so easy, which are not possible in an intuitive representation. The error which I committed arose from an illusion that arose from the position in which I came to know the knowledge [in question]; hence I cannot hold the judgment of others to be dispensable. For they can correct mine. For example, when I see something in the distance, I say it is a horse; another says it is a tree. Perhaps I have previously impressed the thought of a horse deeply upon myself, and through this illusion I believe that I see a horse in the distance. Egoism is accordingly the fault of believing that, when the question concerns the criterion of truth, we do not need the judgment of others.",[]],"wiener-s05-032":["Logical egoism is either indifferentism, that is, indifference towards the judgments of others, in that I hold the judgments of others to be unnecessary for judging my own; or self-conceit and arrogance, in which one claims that oneself alone, before all others, passes a correct judgment upon a matter.",[]],"wiener-s05-033":["Providence, however, has so ordered it that we put our judgments before universal human reason, and has implanted in us the impulse to do so. Many, indeed, believe here that these are nothing more than the springs of vanity. That may be; but the purpose of Providence is nevertheless attained, and we do in fact strive to do better. If it is not the case that we lay our thoughts before universal reason, we have cause to suspect the validity of our judgments, since we are unwilling to follow the wise precept of nature, that we should test our truth against the judgments of others. It is accordingly wrong in the state to forbid men to write books and, for example, to judge of matters of religion. For thereby they are deprived of the only means which nature has given them, namely of testing their judgment against the reason of others. Those who tyrannize so despotically grant the freedom to think in silence; but the worst of it [for them] is only that they cannot prevent anyone from doing so. I can always think what I will. But as regards logical egoism, it must also be granted that, since human nature is directed to the use of this external criterion, I have also a right to put forward my thoughts publicly. || V The prejudice of antiquity is one of the most harmful of prejudices. It is very natural that it is held especially by an old man. For since he can no longer forge himself into a new form, he defends the old as much as he can, and maintains himself, as it were, in his possession. Young people are more for the new, since they still find themselves courageous enough to acquire new modes of knowledge. – Yet there is also something universal in this prejudice: a certain opinion underlies it, namely that men believe throughout that everything grows worse with time, and that everything in the world grows old. || V This is, to be sure, in a certain measure true. But our lifetime is much too short to observe all this as it gradually grows old. One thing in nature is wholly unchangeable, namely the species, although every individual [*individuum*] grows old. In this way people imagine that the species is gradually being degraded, and that men will by degrees become nothing but clever oxen, || V that the old German honesty is quite coming to an end. But certainly men were not formerly greater, more virtuous or more intelligent than now; and a man who has not ruined his body by bad conduct will still beget a son just as healthy as Adam's. || V The predispositions [*Anlagen*] are the same as formerly. We are therefore not in a position to find excellent and remarkable things in departure from them. || V The prejudice of antiquity rises and falls from time to time. Now it seems to be rising greatly again; formerly it was novelty that rose more. || V If we content ourselves with the ancients merely in order to combine a historical knowledge [*Kenntniß*] of them with our other knowledge, that is useful and good. || V But if we believe that we find wisdom in them, we err very greatly; and we should ruin all the sciences if we were to go back to the learning of a foreign age and add what the ancients gathered as children to our practised judgment. Our taste, however, they may well form.",[]],"wiener-s05-034":["Prejudices often arise from opposite causes. For according as a head is disposed, it falls into a prejudice from an opposite cause. || V The prejudice in favour of the ancients arises from an unfavourable judgment against what is old, as does the prejudice in favour of learning in women, because we have an unfavourable prejudice regarding the capacity [*capricitaet*] of that sex. If we have an unfavourable concept of the ancients, we may nevertheless form a favourable representation of them, because we confuse admiration [*Bewunderung*] with astonishment [*Verwunderung*]. For when we have an unfavourable prejudice regarding a thing, and yet meet with something in it that exceeds our expectation, we are astonished. Admiration, on the other hand, is the agreeable sensation when we find something very great. For example, we are astonished when a child, of whom we have no such opinion at all, can already speak so cleverly. || V But in the end this astonishment may also degenerate into admiration. Astonishment is the state of the soul on beholding what is new and unexpected; admiration [is that state on beholding] the unexpected good. For example, when a woman begins to talk like a book, we are astonished, and believe that she is cleverer than she really is. – The ancients were far from having the prejudices in the sciences that we have. We should therefore not suppose that they would have the knowledge that we have. Hence we are astonished when we see that they thought as we do. This produces astonishment at an ancient, because in the age in which he lived he already said something so clever; and the astonishment in the end degenerates into admiration, in that we find more merit in him than he really has. || V For example, when someone quotes a passage from Plato, it would certainly be poorly said if a modern were to say it so; but it is surely a great deal, we think, that the man could already establish so much at that time; and so in the end we admire his proposition, and no longer the man. And from this the prejudice arises. For it is wrong that, instead of the man, whom we may well have reason to admire, we admire the proposition.",[]],"wiener-s05-035":["|| V The second cause of prejudices in favour of antiquity is that all the knowledge we have of antiquity is, with us, learning [*Gelehrsamkeit*], although the sciences of the ancients of which we now have reports contain very little learning, or none at all. Even the knowledge of the language of the ancients is learning, and always earns respect. Accordingly even the knowledge of the merits of the ancients earns respect; and here again, through an illusion, it comes about that the insights themselves earn respect, although only the knowledge of the man who knows something demands respect. || V The fact that the learned among the ancients also had insight into the proposition casts a reflected, favourable light upon it. For learning deserves favour, even though the proposition contains nothing profitable. || V For it is always an agreeable thing to hear 'Cicero said that', because it shows the author's wide reading in the ancients; in that I receive the exposition with favour, I receive the proposition with favour, even if it does not belong to learning. || V Since, then, the proposition is put forward in such a vehicle [*vehiculo*], and gains authority from having come to us from a distant place and time, this produces the prejudice of believing that it is better said than in more recent times. || V We have cause to form favourable judgments of antiquity without any prejudice needing to lurk therein. This ground, however, suffices only for a moderate respect. But when we have the ground, we often overstep the limits and accord them an immoderate respect. Time sifts all things: what is praised today is despised and rejected tomorrow. But what has not fallen out of use over a long time may be presumed to have an inner worth. The writings of antiquity have been sifted in this way, since they have survived only by distinguishing themselves from other writings. We therefore have cause to judge favourably of these writings. But this is no proof that the writings of antiquity have an absolutely great worth; they have a great worth only relatively to the other writers, and if we make this relative worth into an absolute one, a prejudice arises. If, then, we were to take the bad and the good books of the ancients together, and from them form a judgment of the knowledge of the ancients as a whole, how astonishing would the judgment be! || V If, for example, our descendants were to get into their hands the rubbish of books which one could wish did not exist at all, they would surely be unable to comprehend how, at a time when the true course of the sun was discovered, such nonsense could also be thought. But if all the bad books were lost, they would take us all for giants in judgment. So it is with the ancients too. || V If we adopt the opinion of the ancients, and entrust ourselves to them in such a way that we thereby surrender our own use of reason, then it is a prejudice",[]],"wiener-s05-036":["that arises here. It is characteristic of prejudices that they wholly obstruct the individual use of the understanding.",[]],"wiener-s05-037":["|| V Another cause of judging in favour of antiquity is gratitude. We are its pupils; for each generation is always the teacher of the one that follows. || V Gratitude seems to require that we pay honour to the ancients, and so it is believed that those to whom we owe thanks must be praised beyond measure. We ourselves expect, after all, some day to become the ancients. And just as we despise a man who mocks an old man, since he himself hopes to grow old, so we too should deserve contempt were we to mock the ancients. To make good this wrong, the extolling of the ancients is always carried beyond all bounds; for it is held to be virtuous to exaggerate even one's gratitude, since one has then at least done something that redounds to their honour. || V Judgments in favour of antiquity also arise from envy of one's contemporaries. A man who has always studied the ancients has neglected what is new, and therefore he continually admires the ancients alone, because they are all that he understands. || V Since he cannot surpass the moderns, he proceeds thus: he continually extols the ancients. || V The dead are no longer envied, but those still living are an object of envy. When, therefore, someone has diligently studied the ancients, he praises them highly, in order not to allow the moderns any superiority over himself. – || V No knowledge puffs up more than philological knowledge, and than what is entitled *polyhistory* [polyhistorie]. This exists when one possesses a kind of universality in respect of acquaintance with the ancients, although this acquaintance is not adequate, but merely historical. The *literati* at once find everything in the ancients, and will by no means allow that the moderns should have discovered anything. When the magnetic needle had been invented, a word was at once found in Terence which already indicated it. Philolaus was supposed already to have had the world-system of Copernicus. Philosophy beats down all such pride, and may well produce a misology towards what is science; for it lays bare so completely the abyss of our ignorance that we are wary of proceeding further along the road. But no philology can extend our understanding by a single degree; it serves only to ensure that we do not lose the connection, and the knowledge of the interconnection, of our acquaintance [Kenntnisse] with that of the ancients.",[]],"wiener-s05-038":["|| V The prejudice of novelty. What is new is never in itself a prejudice. The ancients laid down something as a foundation; but we hope that what is new will be better. This produces much vanity, just as the prejudice of antiquity produces much pedantry.",[]],"wiener-s05-039":["The prejudice arising from an accepted doctrinal system [Lehrgebäude] does not properly belong among the universal prejudices. A doctrinal system is unity in a body of knowledge that can be derived from one *principium* [principle]. In the end we acquire a certain *praedilection* [predilection] for the principle, judging that everything which occurs in the system deserves great respect; and this causes us often to close an eye when errors occur in the system. This is properly only a private prejudice of self-love.",[]],"wiener-s05-040":["The prejudice of mistrust of antiquity. All the negative prejudices can be recognised from the foregoing; for it is easily seen that a mistrust of antiquity arises from too great a love of myself, and so on.",[]],"wiener-s05-041":["Prejudices do not properly belong to logic at all. For logic has to do with the objective grounds of the understanding, and not with its subjective causes. The subjective causes are explanations of actual appearances [Erscheinungen], namely of how it comes about that men form one thing in this way and another in that, according to their understanding. [The topic] therefore properly belongs to anthropology. Logic considers only the objective universal grounds of reason, without regard to whether the understanding is corrupted or not. A prejudice is a mere matter of fact [Gegebenheit], and all *explication* of matters of fact belongs to psychology. It may nevertheless be taken in here, since it can find a place in the use of logical instruction; and we must attend to the causes that may lead us away from the right use of that instruction.",[]],"wiener-s06-001":["Everyone, and so also our author, commits the error of speaking of the *object*, where one ought to say that the knowledge is probable; that is, the object is called probable in so far as I hold it to be probable, although my knowledge may be true and certain. My knowledge is probable when the cases that determine the thing are more numerous than those that determine its opposite. If we bear this in mind, we may indeed say that a mode of knowledge is probable; but the logic of the probable is not a pure logic. It would be a science which had for its objects those *objects* for which the causes are in themselves possible, where we should then see how many causes",[]],"wiener-s06-002":["are present for the effect, and how many for its opposite. Thus there is ground to presume that that for which there are more grounds is probable, although [this ground] is not sufficient. All probability is a fraction whose denominator is the number of all possible cases, and whose numerator contains the number of favourable cases [Treffer]. For example, if someone is to throw 8 with 2 dice, he has 6 favourable cases out of 36 possible cases. The fraction is therefore 6/36, of which his opponent has 30 cases and he 6. The probability of the *object* rests on the relation that the grounds [for] an event bear to complete certainty. Probability, therefore, cannot be properly understood unless one knows what is required for complete certainty. For since for probability we must have a measure, and since the measure of every insufficient ground is the sufficient ground, the measure of probability is certainty. – Mathematics can here specify a definite measure by which it compares magnitudes, because magnitudes contain nothing but what is homogeneous. But in philosophy this is not feasible, since here the grounds of the possible favourable cases are all heterogeneous. If I take all the possible cases and say that the favourable cases must also be contained in them, the relation of the grounds of holding-to-be-true [Vorwahrhalten] is heterogeneous. For example, there are many testimonies in favour of an accused man. (1) The deed was done when he was not at home, and he was in fact not at home; this is *res facti* [a matter of fact]. (2) Another person had seen him at that time; here the ground is the credibility of that other person. The grounds in philosophy, then, always differ in *quality*, and cannot be numbered, but only weighed [ponderirt]. In philosophy the magnitude of the probability often cannot be so determined, because the grounds are not homogeneous. But here, in [such] knowledge, it is not the knowledge that is regarded as probable, but the *object*. The knowledge in itself may be true.",[]],"wiener-s06-003":["As regards the probability of knowledge, it consists in grounds of holding-to-be-true prior to all investigation. If I find in the knowledge no contradiction, but rather connection with other things, I do not [on that account] say that the *object* is true. The probability of the knowledge signifies little; what matters is whether the *object* is true. – Men often think, when they predict something probable, that they have won a real triumph, even though they had no grounds for holding it to be true. It came about by chance; the judgment was ungrounded, and the knowledge was therefore not at all probable, even though it was knowledge of a probable *object*. We must not always follow probable knowledge. It is only the beginning of the beginning of examination, and can lead us to inquire further into the truth. A mode of knowledge is doubtful when there are grounds for and against it. No doubt can be raised against it, because it maintains the opposite equally well. This indifference in the logical sphere is just what indifference is in the practical, the understanding being here entirely indifferent in respect of the knowledge. Knowledge is doubted [bezweifelt] when one has already declared oneself for it, but counter-grounds are brought against it from the other side. Doubt signifies a mere hindrance to holding-to-be-true. It is the state of the mind [Gemüt] when there is something that hinders us from assent. It may be considered objectively and subjectively. When the doubt is objectively distinct, it is an objection [Einwurf]; when it is objectively indistinct and obscure, it is a scruple. In ordinary usage *dubitatio*, subjective doubt, and *dubium*, objective doubt, are confused with one another. We say: I have many doubts about it. If these are not objective grounds, one may well doubt for oneself; but one cannot put them to another as grounds in order to induce him to doubt also. The grounds of subjective holding-to-be-true are to be located in upbringing, in that one cannot yet properly accustom one's head to adopting another opinion. – One has a scruple against a mode of knowledge when one cannot rid oneself of the hindrance to holding it true. This scruple may also be subjective. It is that which makes us uneasy in regard to modes of knowledge, so that we are unwilling to assent to the knowledge with our whole soul, because something in us opposes complete assent. – Scruples are not to be despised. For at the outset all our modes of knowledge are obscure, and so at the outset much is of course presented to us scrupulously, because there are grounds that conflict with the *object*. When a scruple becomes clear it becomes an objection; and before it has been made clear the scruple must therefore not be rejected. It shows great talent to be able to make another's scruples clear to him. Objections presuppose that modes of knowledge have already been taken as true; doubts do not presuppose this, and in them we can always proceed problematically. – Objections do not yet refute; they serve only to weaken the knowledge. They are therefore of use in all holding-to-be-true, in that the certainty of the knowledge is brought to greater perfection. [Once they have been met,] a thing can no longer be attacked, since the opponent has [already] set up all the grounds for the opposite. Law and religion, consequently, certainly require objections, since through them they attain to greater certainty. Doubts can be answered; objections are refuted. Every doubt must not only be answered, for then it is merely rebuffed; it must also be resolved, in that I must show how it could have arisen. If this is not done, the hindrance to holding-to-be-true is not yet wholly removed, and the ground for new doubts remains. From this it follows that the refutation of objections must be carried out in a friendly manner, so that instead of merely driving the other back, one must also inquire through what an otherwise sound understanding could have been brought to such objections, that is, through what illusion [Schein] the objections arose. If we then find a confusion, we must explain it to him, and so *nodum resolvere, non secare* [untie the knot, not cut it]. *Probabilitas* is probability [Wahrscheinlichkeit]; *verisimilitudo* is plausibility [Scheinbarkeit]. A mode of knowledge, as knowledge, may be entitled plausible; but the thing which I hold to be true, or probable, is not on that account *verisimile*, but probable or improbable. The distinction rests on this. In probability I compare the grounds of holding-to-be-true with certainty. If the holding-to-be-true is greater than half of the sufficient ground, the knowledge is probable; if it is less than half, the knowledge is improbable. For if I have more than half of the ground, I have more than there can possibly be grounds for the opposite at all, and therefore necessarily more than all the grounds possible on the other side. In mathematics one can determine by numbers the sufficient ground of holding-to-be-true in the case of probability, because the things are homogeneous in the grounds of their possibility; and when things are of the same kind, they need only be counted together [to see] whether my grounds amount to more than half. But the grounds of truth in philosophical modes of knowledge I can determine only discursively. For since the grounds here are not homogeneous, it comes about that I cannot say: here is half the sufficient ground. One cannot indicate how a ground stands to the sufficient ground, since between heterogeneous things no equal relation can be specified. Laws and examples from nature, without the testimony of others, are grounds of this kind. Where is the half to be found here? A logic of probability is therefore an impossible undertaking. If, indeed, we understand by it that part of logic in which the concepts of the probable, the doubtful, etc., are made intelligible, that can quite properly be done. But if degrees are to be specified, in order to judge whether something is probable or not, it is impossible; for in the case of certainty we do not gain insight into the grounds of certainty until we have actually attained certainty; and since in philosophy the grounds of holding-to-be-true are heterogeneous, we can only weigh them, that is, try to measure the grounds against our assent. We cannot, however, [strictly] measure them; the art of measuring probability falls to mathematics. – In the usual logics there are always certain rules according to which the understanding is to be employed in matters of probability. But the rules are more obscure to understand than [the case would be] if one sought to apply them; nor do they help us in use: reflection alone is required. – Understanding is the faculty of rules. Judgment [Urteilskraft] is the faculty of distinguishing whether a rule is to be applied in a given place, and therefore the faculty of subsuming under a rule. To this faculty I cannot give mere rules set over it. In the investigation of probability in philosophy, therefore, to which judgment also extends, there are no precepts of probability. There is indeed a mathematics of probability, and it is also practised. For example, in the schools, in calculation, instruction is given in the best way of arriving at the probability, in relation to the errors of other methods. Thus I calculate the altitude of a star 20 times. One time the result is 10 degrees 13 minutes, another time 13 degrees 11 minutes, etc. I add all these results together and divide by 20 [dividire damit 20], and so I have the probable altitude. Mathematics thus determines certain rules according to which the *object* can be known with probability. In philosophy, however, this is impossible. You may well give examples in order to exercise your judgment on them; but you will never be able to specify rules as to how far something is probable or not. Probability [probabilitaet] concerns things; plausibility concerns whether in the knowledge there are more grounds for than against the things. For when I compare the grounds of holding-to-be-true with the sufficient ground, and have no grounds for the opposite, plausibility results. For example, someone argues that the thinking being, the soul, will not cease even in death, because in life it has not been dependent on the body; and I object, say, the contingency of generation. I then ask: is it here probable? The objection amounts to nothing; consequently the knowledge is plausible. When, along with a ground for holding something true, there is no ground for the opposite, the knowledge is plausible. If I know a ground which, however, says just what the opposite asserts, the matter is doubtful. But when I compare the thing with the sufficient ground of truth, it is probable; for all probability consists in the approximation of holding-to-be-true to certainty. Objective doubts are the ground that makes a mode of knowledge false. Subjective doubt is the state of the mind in which we have just as many grounds for as against the thing. Subjectively, a man is in doubt; objectively, he has doubts. The principles [Grundsätze] of doubt are: (1) The principle of asserting. The [principle] of this maxim, namely, that all knowledge is to be treated with the aim of asserting something by means of it, is entitled the dogmatic principle. Dogmatic is that knowledge through which all things intended are asserted. The maxim of treating a mode of knowledge with the aim of seeing its complete uncertainty and the impossibility of arriving at certainty is (2) the sceptical maxim: the manner of philosophising in which one renounces all assertive knowledge, and regards all the grounds of one's judgment in such a way as to oppose to them equally strong counter-grounds. We treat something dogmatically when we decide the matter with complete certainty; sceptically, when we seek to show its complete uncertainty. Both, taken universally, are faulty. For many modes of knowledge are impossible, while dogmatism nevertheless seeks to make them certain: for example, concerning changes in the weather. From insight into the universal constitution of things one cannot settle dogmatically whether there is a simple being. Here we must often take another path in order to settle anything. To proceed sceptically destroys all our endeavour; it is an antilogical principle, an attempt to drive out ignorance [ein Versuch, Unwissenheit abzutreiben]. For if I bring knowledge to the point at which it finally destroys itself, that is as much as if we regarded all human knowledge as nothing. Yet, harmful as scepticism is, this does not prevent the philosophical method of treating what is uncertain from being good, namely, that I endeavour to drive the uncertainty to the highest degree, in the hope of discovering by this procedure, in the end, the traces of truth. It is the *suspension* of judgment, in order to attain certainty. This method, then, in which we not only doubt everything, but also seek out the cause of the conflict of the understanding with itself, in order thereby to make the truth plain, is the critical method. We thus have a dogmatic, a sceptical, and a critical method. –",[]],"wiener-s06-004":["To proceed dogmatically with all modes of knowledge, that is, to hope for decided certainty without taking into consideration the grounds of the opposite, produces an inadequate illusion [Schein]. For if I believe that nothing further can be found against the truth, I at once cease from inquiry; but the matter then also has little foundation. One may well attempt something dogmatically, but not proceed dogmatically. In the dogmatic method one must also proceed sceptically, that is, I try whether something cannot also be adduced in the matter which I might place on the side of an opponent. Scepticism, then, is where assertions are made dogmatically on both sides. This one can do within oneself; but which of the two can then decide further? Now comes the critical method: that is, I investigate the sources of the dogmatic and of the sceptical method, and then I begin to see on what grounds an assertion rests, and on what grounds its opposite. The critical method is therefore the intermediate method through which a mode of knowledge can attain certainty. It forestalls the dogmatic illusion, since it opposes scepticism to dogmatism; and since it has thereby weighed the grounds of both, it alone can decide how many grounds I have for holding-to-be-true. Scepticism is a deposition [Entsetzung] of all the instruction of reason, and contradicts itself. It gives out everything as illusion, and yet it distinguishes illusion from truth. Since it warns us to accept nothing as truth, it must have a criterion by which it can recognise truth; and since it gives the ground why we ought to doubt all this, the opposite must surely also be possible. It can therefore be refuted by itself. The doubters of ancient times were very remarkable. Pyrrho is the first doubter. He lived after the time of Socrates, and doubted all things, which he expressed thus: *non liquet* [it is not clear]. This is the Pyrrhonian",[]],"wiener-s06-005":["doubt. But academic doubt is also remarkable. For after Plato's death Speusippus continued his school; after him came Arcesilaus; the third school was that of Carneades. Plato employed the method of Socrates in his *dialogues*; but he was no sceptic at all, and did this only in order, through prolonged suspension, finally to elicit [eruiren] the truth. Speusippus remained faithful to him, and taught as dogmatically as Plato. But Arcesilaus set up a sceptical school; this is academic scepticism. He said: everything is so uncertain that it is even uncertain whether it is uncertain. This was called a veritable [ Textlücke: AA: Purgativ ] of reason, in that, in seeking to purge reason, he carried off reason itself along with [the rest]. The third school, that of Carneades, consisted of dreadful sophists and dialecticians, who denied tomorrow what they asserted today. Hypothesis, the author defines, is an opinion, namely, of philosophers, when they explain the world from appearances. But he does not yet sufficiently explain opinion.",[]],"wiener-s06-006":["The difference between opinion and hypothesis is this. Opinion is an incomplete holding-to-be-true on insufficient grounds, from which I derive nothing. Hypothesis, on the other hand, signifies the judgment of truth on grounds that are adequate. When something is assumed as a ground, from which I can see the sufficient ground of given consequences, it is a *hypothesis*. It is accordingly the holding-to-be-true of a presupposition, not of a mode of knowledge in so far as it is itself derived from grounds, but because it is mediately certain. Immediately certain modes of knowledge are experience and *axioms*. It is an axiom when I accept a mode of knowledge as certain because it can be known *a priori* from the nature of the thing. Empirical modes of knowledge are derived from sense.",[]],"wiener-s06-007":["A mode of knowledge is mediately inferred from another, or it is held to be true on account of a ground; and the latter is hypothesis. When I hold a mode of knowledge to be true because it is a serviceable ground for other grounds, it is a hypothesis. I assume something, and then I see what other knowledge [Kenntnisse] can be derived from it. I therefore accept it, not for the sake of the ground from which it is derived, but I hold it to be true for the sake of the grounds that can be derived from it. There is thus something reversed here in comparison with other modes of knowledge; on this the holding-to-be-true is based. I arbitrarily assume the knowledge as a ground; but through assuming it I can give grounds of other modes of knowledge which are certainly true; and in so far as it is accordingly connected with truth, it is entitled hypothesis. All holding-to-be-true of a hypothesis rests on this, that through it, as a ground, other modes of knowledge are explained as consequences. I therefore properly infer from the truth of the consequences to the ground, and so infer the truth of the knowledge properly from its consequences. For example, I say that there is a central fire in the earth, because volcanoes, earthquakes, and other *phaenomena* prove it. To infer from consequences to the ground is also a *criterium* of truth (p. 88): namely, when true consequences are derived from it, I infer the truth of the knowledge. But although it is one criterion of truth, it is far from being sufficient; the knowledge is true only when all the consequences of the knowledge are true. But since we cannot possibly derive all the consequences of the knowledge, this *a posteriori* way does in fact yield an uncertain inference that the ground is the true ground. From a false ground some true consequences can be drawn, because there is, after all, partly truth in it. A hypothesis therefore always remains a hypothesis, that is, it never becomes complete certainty. For I know only the adequacy of the knowledge to some consequences, and only its adequacy to all possible consequences would yield apodeictic certainty. The derivation of many consequences does not as yet bear at all on the certainty of a mode of knowledge, since other grounds may have just the same knowledge as their result. It therefore yields indeed a ground of probability, but not of certainty. Some sciences admit of no hypotheses at all, for example, mathematics. In natural science, however, they are indispensable. Yet if as many consequences as we can ever discover can all be derived from an assumed ground, the hypothesis grows steadily in probability, and indeed so greatly that, although it can never be apodeictically certain, there yet results from it an *analogon* of certainty; that is, we yield ourselves to it as to complete certainty. For example, with the hypothesis of the Copernican system no single observation has yet been met with which could not be derived from it. Here, then, there is an *analogon* of certainty. It is not, however, apodeictically certain; for it is still possible that the Creator made another arrangement of the planets, as the hypothesis of Tycho Brahe shows. Our conviction rests, however, on this, that however many appearances are found, they can all be derived from the system of Copernicus. We accordingly approximate to apodeictic certainty, and it is *comparative* bound up with it.",[]],"wiener-s06-008":["A hypothesis is not a mode of knowledge held to be completely true; but something in the knowledge must be completely certain, namely,",[]],"wiener-s06-009":["(1) The possibility of a presupposition must be completely certain. If an assumed ground is possible, the matter nevertheless still remains uncertain; for another ground may also obtain to which the knowledge fits. But the possibility itself must be completely certain in the hypothesis. For example, suppose I wished to assume a *commercium pnevmaticum* [spiritual intercourse], that is, the power by which a man might act upon other minds [Gemüter]. This hypothesis cannot be accepted at all; it is pure fiction, for the possibility of this presupposition is not even certain. When we have no knowledge [Kenntnis] whatsoever of the possibility of certain powers, we must not lay them down as hypotheses. The use made of [a hypothesis] need only approximate to certainty, but its possibility must be certain.",[]],"wiener-s06-010":["(2) The consequence [Consequenz] must be completely certain; that is, if I hold something to be certain, the consequences that I infer from it must be completely certain. Otherwise it is regarded as a pure *chimaera*. – For example, when in a case of theft one wishes to know the perpetrators, one frames a hypothesis, and then it must",[]],"wiener-s06-011":["(1) be possible that these people could have done it. If that is so, then",[]],"wiener-s06-012":["(2) the carrying out [of the deed] by those who stole must also be completely comprehended; that is, it must follow with certainty that they could have done it.",[]],"wiener-s06-013":["(3) Unity is required for a hypothesis. No more than one presupposition is necessary to explain a multiplicity of consequences. As soon as we make many presuppositions, every hypothesis loses in its probability. In the case of unity I infer: the more consequences fit one ground, the more probable it is that the ground is true and the right one. But if some, though not many, consequences can be derived from the hypothesis, and new hypotheses must constantly be framed to support it, there is little probability. – *Hypothesis subsidiaria*, an auxiliary hypothesis, is when something is assumed in order to come to the aid of a hypothesis which could not maintain itself without a new hypothesis. In such a case a hypothesis loses all its credibility. –",[]],"wiener-s06-014":["Of this kind was the explanation of Tycho Brahe, [offered] in order to explain the stationary points of the planets. He devised *cycli*; and to explain these deviating *cycli* he assumed *cycli* within the *cycli*, and so on further, so that it is evident that this goes on without end. In philosophy everything that has been said of hypothesis becomes clearly evident, and very true and correct consequences are often derived from hypotheses. Every man who makes experiments first frames hypotheses, in that he believes that this or that experiment will have such and such consequences. He must not at once lose heart when an investigation fails, just as the alchemist still continues to work on the hypothesis of making gold.",[]],"wiener-s06-015":["In philosophy, and above all in natural science, they [hypotheses] cannot be dispensed with, since we take that which can serve as the true cause of things to be in fact their cause. We assume one cause, and all the other causes, according to the analogy of things. It is, for example, a hypothesis that rock crystals form themselves prismatically. There are, however, sciences in which no hypotheses whatsoever are permissible, namely in metaphysics. An example is the hypothesis that the human soul can act in the world even without the body. This would at the same time concede that it can appear after death and act upon the souls of other men. To frame such hypotheses is not permitted, because I cannot prove the possibility of such efficacy. The possibility must always be given through experience. At the least, some similarity to this power must be shown us, so that I may have some reason for accepting the proposition. Persuasion. It might be said that holding something to be true on grounds of which one does not oneself know whether they are subjective or objective is persuasion. We are prepossessed in favour of something, and yet we call it persuasion, because we can give no account of our holding it to be true. We cannot say whether the cause of this holding-to-be-true lies in our senses and concerns our inclination, or whether the understanding has derived it from knowledge of the object. Where that [account] is not available, we are conscious that it is mere persuasion. Persuasions often precede conviction. This seldom happens, however, with one who has gained much insight and learned much in order to attain certainty, because he can readily distinguish persuasions from convictions. Further, one who has often been deceived and has grown wary is not so easily persuaded. There are nevertheless many people who feed themselves largely on hope. That is not good, for the mind must be prepared for everything. It betokens a shallow way of thinking when a man lets himself be so easily persuaded, or persuades himself of something. This is a chief reason why men find no difficulty in religion in readily accepting this or that proposition. They easily persuade themselves, although they can give no ground for it. Many a man cannot do this. He must have something that satisfies him, and he does not straightway put forward grounds of which he does not know whether they are subjective or objective. We see, then, that we ought not to blame any man because he will not at once give his assent to a thing, for in this respect men differ greatly. The one can easily persuade himself of it. The other may wish a hundred times over that the thing were so, and yet his understanding will not at once applaud, because he demands objective grounds. – There is something on which assent rests that is a mixture of inclination and understanding. This many a man does not trouble to separate properly, so as to inquire which of the two produces his assent. – Many men call conviction what is really persuasion. Persuasions, however, are not always false; it is only that this man holds the thing from persuasion and not from conviction. A man may know some grounds in the object through the understanding, and yet many subjective grounds are added to them: for example, the example of famous men who, as is said, were no fools, the great antiquity of the matter, and much else. All this together combines and produces the effect that is entitled persuasion. Men can accept through persuasion things that are certain, but also things of no importance. When someone is wholly unwilling to accept certain things, the cause lies not in the heart but in the head. Parents, for example, readily persuade themselves that their children are innocent and are led astray by the neighbours' children. Whence arises this propensity? They imagine it, even though a stranger sees that the boy is himself a good-for-nothing. The propensity of inclination is so powerful that they content themselves with subjective and actually half-made grounds. Another demands sound grounds and is not so easily put off. This should make us fair-minded when others will not at once accept what seems to us so clear, since we cannot pass off our grounds as first principles [of] custom.",[]],"wiener-s06-016":["Science. A whole [Inbegriff] of knowledge. It is divided into aggregate and system. An aggregate is common knowledge; a system is a science. A system rests on the unity of the idea, that is, on the way in which the manifold of a knowledge is arranged side by side. It therefore presupposes an idea of the whole in which the science is contained. In the case of an aggregate one also thinks to arrive at the whole, by continually adding parts. In an aggregate, therefore, the parts precede the whole; in a system the whole precedes the parts. This distinction is very important. All metaphysics is mere aggregate and rhapsody, because we have never yet had the idea of the whole, [the idea] how far man goes beyond reason, and upon what means he builds what he asserts. Metaphysics is therefore a fixed rhapsody. The metaphysician rummages out everything that he believes stands in connection with the whole. But he has as yet no concept of the whole, and the whole is unknown to him. –",[]],"wiener-s06-017":["Sciences are either historical or rational sciences. It is not right that the author should understand by sciences merely rational knowledge. For historical matters also can be given systematic form, namely, by my laying down beforehand an idea according to which the manifold in history is to be ordered. Unfortunately, however, historians are commonly rhapsodists. The idea might be this. Human actions proceed from human nature, in order completely to fulfil its vocation [Bestimmung]. If I take as my idea how human nature has developed in the various ages, and how it gradually approaches its whole vocation, that is, the fulfilment of all the ends that are set before mankind on earth, I then bring into my head a system according to which I can order history. Certainty is either",[]],"wiener-s06-018":["1.) empirical certainty. This rests either on one's own experiences or on those of others, when on the strength of their testimony I hold the thing to be certain. It is also entitled historical certainty. It is, however, just as much empirical as is my own experience. For I must often trust my own observations less than those of many another whom I know to be an attentive man, [knowing also] that I am likely to overlook much in the matter.",[]],"wiener-s06-019":["The certainty is all the stronger. For I do not attend to many things as closely as another does.",[]],"wiener-s06-020":["2.) Rational certainty is always apodictic certainty, that is, [certainty] that something cannot be otherwise, but must necessarily be so. Empirical certainty is never apodictic. Mathematical certainty is apodictic and intuitive; philosophical certainty is apodictic and discursive.",[]],"wiener-s06-021":["Arbitrary truth. It seems strange that the author should speak of arbitrary truth. For a thing is not yet true merely because I say it; the truth must lie in the object. We had better speak, therefore, of arbitrary propositions. They are propositions in which I will that something shall be so, and which therefore really rest on my will. They are commands of my reason, in which the knowledge depends on the arbitrariness of my knowledge. I decree, for example, that the circle shall be divided into 360 [degrees], that we shall count with 9 figures and the 0. These are arbitrary propositions; for one can also count with 4 figures, as Leibniz did. – Proof is divided into acroamatic proof, which is discursive, and mathematical proof, which is intuitive. The former is carried out from concepts, the latter from the construction of concepts. Empirical proofs we are not here considering at all. One must not, therefore, be quick to triumph in the certainty of one's propositions; for no proposition in philosophy can have mathematical certainty. I cannot, for example, exhibit to anyone a whole world in intuition. In every proof there are to be found",[]],"wiener-s06-022":["1.) the proposition that is to be proved;",[]],"wiener-s06-023":["2.) the ground of proof; and",[]],"wiener-s06-024":["3.) the consequence of the proof, that is, how the knowledge follows from the ground of proof. The ground of proof is entitled *argumentum*. The inference also is sometimes called *argumentum*. In philosophy one must always examine whether the ground of proof is possible in respect of the knowledge [in question].",[]],"wiener-s06-025":["Such inquiries are very important: whether a ground of proof can be taken from this or that source, whether a mode of knowledge relates to experience, or whether we come to partake of it through reason. In metaphysics, which examines the consequences themselves, everything turns on this, whether we shall arrive at consequences through the ground of proof. Logical modes of knowledge, the author says, are indemonstrable, being certain even without [our] assent. Otherwise 'indemonstrable' also signifies a matter that requires a proof but has none. Indemonstrably false is a knowledge whose falsity cannot be proved. An indemonstrable proposition is an immediately certain proposition. What is certain without any ground of proof is immediately certain. However much in a knowledge may be mediately certain, one thing in it is always immediately certain. We shall therefore have indemonstrable propositions in all sciences. They are not merely such as do not need proof, as the author holds; rather, in philosophy every proposition is indemonstrable as soon as it is immediately certain. Proof is either direct, that is, positive, or apagogic, that is, negative. The direct proof shows that the proposition is true; the apagogic, that its opposite is not true. It merely refutes the opposite. In mathematics it is very evident, but in philosophy it must be used very sparingly. For the apagogic proof never achieves as much as the direct proof. The latter goes back also to the sources from which a proposition arises. The apagogic proof shows me only an error, and that I should run into an absurdity if I did not accept the proposition. I have thereby indeed proved the proposition, but not comprehended it. Such proofs are not to be employed in philosophy for this further reason, that I can often [ Textlücke ]refute my opponent, because we both on that account judge [ Textlücke: AA: falsch ]. We are both in the wrong; and when I have refuted the other, he can refute me *apagogice* just as well, without any contradiction, and neither can thereby prove his proposition.",[]],"wiener-s06-026":["Historical belief. Our author relates belief merely to testimony. We distinguish, however, between believing something and believing someone. We can believe something without anyone having told it us. We believe someone when we have accepted something on his testimony. Proofs from experience can indeed establish that the thing is, but not the absolute necessity of the thing; for experience yields only what is contingent. Proofs *a priori*, on the other hand, yield apodictic certainty, whether they proceed from concepts or from the construction of concepts. When I know an object not from mere concepts but *a priori* in the intuition that corresponds to the concept, I construct the concept; and that is a demonstration. Such a proof carries evidence with it, that is, the proof is intuitive. This is mathematical proof. Philosophical proof is never intuitive, but always discursive, that is, it is always carried out in words. Since philosophical proof is not known in intuition, it cannot be entitled demonstration. Both proofs are apodictic, but with this difference, that the one is so in intuition, the other in pure concepts. We must therefore never use the word demonstration of philosophical proofs.",[]],"wiener-s06-027":["It has become the custom to speak of demonstration in the case of apodictic proofs that have nothing intuitive about them. 'Demonstration' comes from *monstrare*, to show, to lay before the eyes. Strictly, therefore, it can be used only of proofs in which the object is exhibited in intuition, and in which the truth is known not merely discursively but also intuitively. Here, then, we shall set bounds to philosophy, and never call its proofs demonstrations, however apodictic they may be. In mathematics one makes no progress at all if one seeks to prove everything from mere concepts; the concepts must necessarily be constructed. – The Greeks were the first to invent demonstrations. No people knew what it was to demonstrate before this arose among the Greeks. It is said that the Greeks learned their wisdom from the Egyptians. But the Egyptians are children compared with the Greeks. They had, indeed, knowledge of many kinds, but not sciences. The Greeks were the first to enlighten the human understanding. A number of stories are recorded in the authors which led to the demonstration of this or that proposition. Had they merely learned these [propositions] from the Egyptians, they would have had no need to demonstrate them further. The Egyptians had only empirical surveyors, who could measure well according to certain rules, without proofs. No people knows what demonstrations are except those who have learned it from the Greeks. All who have not learned it from them regard them as circumlocutions; and yet demonstrations are the sure step that the extended insight into mathematics has taken. Without them all its endeavours would be deceptive, like all philosophical endeavours, in which human reason often deceives itself. In regard to belief, a distinction must be drawn between believing something and believing another. Believing something relates merely to the knowledge and to the ground of holding it to be true. A knowledge may arise from reason, and the ground of holding it to be true, though logically insufficient, may yet be practically quite sufficient. That is to say, although logically, before the pure understanding, the ground of holding this knowledge to be true is not wholly valid, the knowledge can yet be practically accepted as true. This is rational belief, which is entirely distinct from knowing. It signifies a holding-to-be-true that is logically insufficient but practically sufficient, and both [are] from grounds. For example, in mathematics it is said at the outset that the diameter is to the circumference as 100 to 314; this is proved only later, in trigonometry. Here, then, the holding-to-be-true rests merely on the fact that famous men discovered it. But since one already knows that in mathematics an error cannot easily remain hidden, [the learner] has good reason to believe firmly that it is true; yet he cannot say 'I know it'. – To believe something on the testimony of others where the knowledge rests entirely on reason is a complete misuse of reason, for the testimony of others must rest only on experience. But since in mathematics errors cannot easily persist and long remain hidden, I can safely rely on the diameter's being to the circumference as 100:314, although I only believe it and cannot say 'I know it'. We can also accept certain propositions of reason on belief. Great mathematicians accept propositions in mathematics without having gone through the demonstration. In philosophy, however, this cannot be done, because there errors so easily lie hidden. Historical belief, in which we hold something to be true because we believe another, can become certainty. – Belief, then, is either rational belief or historical belief. Rational belief is that which, from grounds of reason, is sufficient not logically but practically. Historical belief is that in which I hold something to be true merely on the testimony of another, even, it may be, without grounds. A rational belief is one that is merely assumed, although we have as yet no certainty whatsoever of the matter; but it has practical grounds. For instance, [the belief] that the soul is immortal can impel me to order my life better. This practical ground can never rise to a ground that is entitled knowing; for knowing signifies that the holding-to-be-true is also logically sufficient. There can, however, be a holding-to-be-true so strong as to be as unshakeable as the greatest certainty. But whoever would speculate must have modes of knowledge that are sufficient also according to logical grounds.",[]],"wiener-s06-028":["Historical belief can be real knowing. Belief based on testimony is accordingly not to be distinguished from knowing, although rational belief must always be distinguished from knowing. That historical belief can also be knowing we see from this. If I ask someone, 'What is the capital of Spain?' and he were to say, 'I believe Madrid', I should reply, 'You must not believe that, but know it.' Should it be said that one cannot know it without having been there oneself, the answer is this. Even if I am there myself, I can learn it in no other way than by the inhabitants telling me; so that I still accept it on the testimony of others. Nor does the fact that it is a testimony at all prevent there being certainty in it. For we can accept something on the testimony of others just as well as on our own experience, since there is just as much that is deceptive in our experience as in the testimony of others. Our thinking, in holding an experience to be true, is exposed to many dangers. The testimony of others that we accept is indeed exposed to as many dangers as our own experience is liable to errors. Yet we can attain certainty through the testimony of others just as well as through our own experience. Belief [of this kind] is thus one and the same with knowing. When, therefore, we contrast belief with knowing, it is only a practically sufficient holding-to-be-true. What we know through reason we cannot always know [in the strict sense]; what we know through historical belief we can know. – Concerning what can I question a witness? I can hold something to be true when the other has infallible reason, or when his experience bids him say it. I can hold something to be true by means of a witness of infallible reason, in truths of reason, or on account of what he has from his own experiences. Since there are not so many errors to be met with in experience as in speculations, it is easier to believe what experience reports than what another has known through reason, since reason is not so infallible. Mathematics has an infallible reason; as soon, therefore, as a proposition has been asserted by it, it is infallible. – But since the concept of the infallibility of reason belongs only to the highest perfection, we can accept no testimony as infallibly true save the divine testimony itself. It is objected here: can a man not, then, bear witness to divine oracles? Answer: he can bear witness to nothing save through an experience of the inspiration that he has had; and here it is always still possible that the man may err. Here, therefore, there can be no believing *subjective*; all grounds must be carefully examined. – And thus, on the testimony of another, I believe much that he gives out as his own experience, [but which is] also [taken] on the testimony of others. For example, when someone relates what he has read, he bears witness to his own experience from a book. Here, then, I rely 1.) on the statement of another, and 2.) on the experience itself that the other has had.",[]],"wiener-s06-029":["One science, namely jurisprudence, is of such a kind that, although it rests entirely on reason, much use is nevertheless made in it of testimonies. In philosophy what matters is not what testimonies one adduces but what grounds. The matter well deserves closer inquiry: why, although *jus* is of such a kind as to rest on reason, the judgment of others is nevertheless taken into account. The reason is perhaps that *jus* rests on laws, and these contain a great manifold of cases, which require a special determination. Here errors are possible, in that something is held to be universal which admits of exceptions. When, therefore, one wishes to have a law that is entirely valid and serviceable, one believes that others will not so easily have overlooked anything. That is why jurists are entitled *juris periti* [those experienced in law], although it is a rational science. It is, however, a lamentable state of affairs when it comes about in this way. For however practised men may be in attending to all cases, it is still possible that they have overlooked some. – All citations, when they are meant to furnish grounds of proof, involve historical belief. If I were not willing to hold something to be true on the testimony of another, I should not cite him, that is, call him as a witness. I can of course relate something, for example, that Plato maintained this or that, and adduce the passage; but in that case I am not citing Plato. – Citation in respect of a rational science is a *heterogeneum*; the two do not go together. It is consequently a great infirmity when one finds oneself compelled to add historical belief to the grounds of reason. – In all empirical modes of knowledge historical belief is indispensable. What we do not know, or cannot ourselves experience, we must know through the experience of others; and by its nature historical belief applies only to the experience of others. In matters that concern reason I can call no one as witness. But in what others have experienced, this declaration of others regarding their experience, on account of which I hold the matter to be true, is the testimony that produces historical belief. For the credibility of a witness there is required:",[]],"wiener-s06-030":["1.) The competence of the witness consists in his having been able to tell the truth. To this belongs",[]],"wiener-s06-031":["a. that he has sufficient skill in making experiences [Erfahrung anzustellen]. It is not so easy to make experiences in all cases, and practice is required for it. Moreover, in experience there is much deception of the senses. The common man is not a proper witness, for he cannot rightly make experiences. When fear comes over him, he takes one thing for another, and overlooks much.",[]],"wiener-s06-032":["b. that he has been in circumstances in which he could make experiences. The common man in particular cannot do so in all inner circumstances. He is often, for example, drowsy, fearful, distracted, and so forth.",[]],"wiener-s06-033":["c. that he also has the skill to declare his experiences so that his meaning can be well understood. This is not so easy. Some relate things very precisely. Others can give only a faint outline of their experience, and cannot render an account of all its circumstances.",[]],"wiener-s06-034":["2.) The sincerity of the witness, [that is,] that he has been willing to tell the truth. The common man is too rude to set a great value on truth. A mouthful of lies, he thinks, matters little when something is at stake for him. Misfortunes they always relate with exaggeration, so that everything is overdrawn and lapses into the horrible. In short, they seek to arouse wonder. Here one must make them attentive by threatening them with the oath. They then begin to hesitate and become very doubtful. – The same holds also of ancient times. Not a single historian among the ancients confines himself so precisely to the truth; they always aim at writing finely. They accept all rumours without examining them. Herodotus, for example, has many old wives' tales. Nor do they regard it as very important to tell the truth. Livy, for example, seeks to write floridly, and has generals deliver speeches that were never delivered. – We in our times are already more compelled to speak the truth, by circumstances, above all by experimental physics, and by the endeavour to determine appearances exactly through observations and experiments, in order to penetrate the laws of nature with greater certainty. Only at the beginning of the last century did men begin to see that it is necessary to tell the whole truth. Everyone then had to be entirely exact in his reports, and if anyone deviated even a little from the truth in his writings, he was exposed and dishonoured. On this account it is customary to employ all manner of cautious expressions. Jurin, for example, always says 'in so far as this is true', etc. This exactness in reporting experience passed afterwards from natural science into history; for it was seen to be necessary not to violate the truth in the smallest point. We now have facilities for learning the truth. The printing presses at once show where [an author] has erred in his writing; so too the gazettes. Little stories can no longer be told, as in Rome. For whereas formerly someone came from one country to another after three years, now, owing to the organisation of the posts, news passes from one place to another in a few days. For example, the old tales of Apollonius of Tyana and Alexander of Paphlagonia came to Rome, growing on the way. In Rome they found acceptance and were believed, as Philostratus also writes of them. Now, however, the post has put a stop to the monstrous growth of rumours. But in ancient times truth was not so exactly determined. There are many honest people; but they often think it unnecessary to be so precise when the untruth does no harm, or brings amusement with it, or when they even believe it to be a report of great utility. This is the so-called *pia fraus* [pious fraud], if that is not a contradiction. – From all this it is evident that historical belief carries with it an astonishing [degree of] credibility, since the witness must have such various qualities in order to tell the truth. – Every man has his own particular interest, which cannot always be judged. Many a one seeks merely to embellish the matter by his narrative, and so forth. Since, then, we cannot believe a witness without such critical grounds in judging a testimony, historical belief in important cases rests on such grave misgivings that it must be very thoroughly examined. For in how many ways is the human will disposed! It is therefore better to dispense with this belief entirely, when it lacks the requisite qualities, if one cannot divine why men speak thus and not otherwise. Nevertheless certainty can be grounded on the testimony of other men, as history and geography are so grounded.",[]],"wiener-s06-035":["The ear-witness bears witness to his experience of the testimony of another concerning a thing. The eye-witness bears witness to his own experience of the thing. One who relates a report that he has read somewhere is an ear-witness. One who presents something that the hearer could not know from his own experience is an ear-witness. In the case of common report, of tradition, or of rumour, the witness himself cannot be determined. The witness is a multitude of persons, where it is believed that the multitude will be credible. A tradition exists in so far as one person has transmitted his testimonies orally to another. It is thus a series of subordinated testimonies.",[]],"wiener-s06-036":["If the concept 'unbelieving', which also occurs in theology, is meant to signify a reproach, it cannot consist in difficulty in giving credence to a testimony; for that is no fault. Rather, so far as it is a reproach, the ground must be moral. The former may even redound to a man's honour, if he is slow to believe or unbelieving, as, for example, in regard to a general rumour; this cannot be to his moral detriment. In all narratives it is good and praiseworthy to be unbelieving, and one must there, where possible, proceed critically. Belief can be divided twofold, into theoretical and practical. In theoretical belief one may be unbelieving. In moral belief he is called unbelieving who has no moral interest in accepting something as true, although he sees how important this or that proposition is. Moral belief may therefore be presupposed in every man, and moral unbelief is consequently very blameworthy. One may also call unbelieving him who rejects the proof of the existence of God from mere reason, and also him who no longer believes in virtue, when he does nothing from a moral interest but everything from hypocrisy. Here the man abandons all resolve to virtue, because he believes in none.",[]],"wiener-s06-037":["Of practical knowledge. Here a twofold division is to be noted, whereby an ambiguity which the words may occasion is forestalled. A knowledge is entitled practical in contrast to theoretical knowledge, and also in contrast to speculative modes of knowledge. – When a proposition is a commanding proposition, an *imperativus*, and says that something ought to happen, it is a practical proposition. It states what free actions would be good for a certain purpose. An *imperativus*",[]],"wiener-s06-038":["[A practical proposition] is, therefore, a proposition which impels to a possible free action, in so far as that action would be good and necessary in respect of a certain purpose. All practical propositions, when they are opposed to theoretical propositions, are *imperativi* [imperatives]. These differ among themselves. In sciences which are nothing but science or skill, we adopt certain arbitrary purposes, and in accordance with them presuppose what ought to be done. For instance, in geometry, as when I say: to measure a straight line, take, etc. Theoretical propositions, on the other hand, do not say how something ought to be done, but how the thing is. For instance: a straight line is the shortest way, etc. These can be distinguished at a glance from practical propositions, since the practical are always *imperativi*; and they are very easily distinguished from such propositions as testify to the properties of the action. – Secondly, however, practical modes of knowledge are also opposed to speculative modes of knowledge. Modes of knowledge can be theoretical and yet be either practical or speculative. For although, being theoretical, they do not say what ought to be done, practical propositions can none the less be derived from them; and in so far they are opposed to speculative propositions. For instance, ‘There is a God’ is a theoretical proposition, but it is practical *in potentia* [potentially]: you need only act as if there were a supreme lawgiver of your actions. Thus modes of knowledge, although in themselves theoretical, are not distinguished from other practical modes of knowledge, namely because they are practical *in potentia*. – Speculative propositions are all those from which no rules and *imperativi* for our actions follow. In natural theology [some] propositions are merely speculative; for instance, whether the omnipresence of God occupies a space or consists merely in an influence upon created beings. This has no influence upon our actions, and is therefore mere speculation. But whether the divine will is also a holy will is a proposition of practical importance, from which rules for our conduct can be derived. Theoretical propositions are accordingly either speculative or objectively practical propositions, that is, propositions which are practical *in potentia*. We might also say: all knowledge is either practical or theoretical. That knowledge is practical in which the propositions are expressed as *imperativi*, in that they indicate the necessity of a free action. Theoretical modes of knowledge contain the knowledge of an object, of what it is; but the use of them is always either a speculative or a practical use. And this distinction is not so much a distinction in knowledge as in the use of knowledge. Some [modes of knowledge] we possess merely for speculative use, others for practical use and for rules of our conduct. The use of moral propositions can be a speculative use. Those who reason subtly about the *principia* of virtue make merely a speculative use of them. The ancient philosophers, who philosophised about the highest good, never applied it in practical use. Not all modes of knowledge can be applied to practical use. – Who, for instance, can make a practical use of mathematics or of natural science? We are, however, glad to seek to apply our modes of knowledge to practical use. For through practical use our modes of knowledge acquire ever more truth; and the final end of all our knowledge seems, after all, to rest on this, that it should contain a rule of what accords with our highest ends. If no consequences for our conduct follow from a mode of knowledge, it seems to be without use. Hence all speculation, too, looks to the practical, even though this is not at once perceived. He who drew the first mathematical figure did, after all, think that some use might one day be made of it; otherwise it would have been merely a pastime of idleness. But suppose that by mathematics we sought merely to cultivate our reason: that would be as good as a practical use. For reason can prescribe rules to our conduct, and this certainly belongs to practical use. Hence the esteem in which our knowledge is held rests chiefly on practical use; for it is in expectation of practical use that we cultivate knowledge. This value of knowledge we can sometimes regard as a mediate value, as a means to other ends, or as an immediate value; and the latter is then entitled dignity. No thing in the world has a dignity. [Dignity] is the final end for the sake of which the world is so ordered. Much in the perfection of man has a mediate value, for instance many of his properties. But one thing in him has an immediate value, and that is his dignity. Mathematics has many uses: by it calendars can be made, fields measured, etc. That is its mediate utility, its market price. But in this respect it is not to be preferred to many other means, since everything that is merely a means is without [inner] worth. Plato, however, already says that this mediate utility is far from being the highest end of mathematics. For its propositions have in themselves an inner excellence which far surpasses all the fields that are to be measured. Longinus says: that is not sublime the contempt of which is sublime. For instance, wealth is not sublime, for the contempt of wealth is sublime. Thus mathematics is not sublime in virtue of determining man's fields and property; but it has an inner worth. There is in mathematics a splendour, something that elevates our reason, and in which we feel our reason in its full strength. Through the thoroughness of its proofs and its *methodus* it has a sublime worth. But if we now consider this again aright, we see that this is still not the true dignity, and that only a good will constitutes true dignity. For what would all skill avail if man chose to employ it with malice? The will to employ this skill [well] is his true worth. All the modes of knowledge which contribute to the dignity of man are far from constituting his highest worth; this consists rather in making good use of all his talents. The true dignity of man rests, therefore, upon morality. Hence that [ Textlücke ]is entitled practical which serves to improve our will; and for this reason philosophical morals is entitled practical philosophy, since practical matters occur in that science. – If modes of knowledge are so constituted that nothing practical can be drawn from them, and that therefore no actions whatsoever follow from them, such knowledge has a merely speculative use. –",[]],"wiener-s06-039":["The whole doctrine of practical use which the author treats does not belong to logic at all. For nothing belongs to logic save the logical form of all modes of knowledge, that is, the form of thinking without regard to the content. Logic can therefore contain only propositions which can be demonstrated. Experience of things, in which empirical principles have to be laid at the foundation, does not belong to logic, since it cannot be demonstrated. Practical knowledge, however, differs from speculative knowledge in its content. [Hence it is not the case that] logic can have to do with the practical. – The author treats of desires and aversion. In logic, however, we must think as though we had no will at all; otherwise it would become a practical science. What we have, therefore, is the science of thinking, not of willing. –",[]],"wiener-s06-040":["He speaks further of living and dead modes of knowledge; dead, namely, are those which might move the will, since they are practical, and yet do not move it, and are therefore mere speculation. – But it is at once evident that all this does not belong to logic. He speaks of indifference, when something is indifferent, etc.",[]],"wiener-s07-001":["Up to this point we have spoken of knowledge in general, in so far as it is distinct or clear, certain or uncertain. We have treated of the kinds of proof and of holding-to-be-true, of belief and knowing, and so forth. The author now divides knowledge into its elements, which should have been done at the very beginning. Logically, all the beginnings in thinking are divided as follows:",[]],"wiener-s07-002":["1.) Knowledge is a simple knowledge, a concept.",[]],"wiener-s07-003":["2.) Modes of knowledge, when combined, become a judgment.",[]],"wiener-s07-004":["3.) That judgments are combined, and that inferences arise from them. The ancients said: *Quot sunt operationes mentis? Resp. tres. apprehensio simplex, Judicium et Ratiocinium* [How many are the operations of the mind? Answer: three – simple apprehension, judgment and reasoning]. As regards the concept, we shall begin with what is the very simplest in our knowledge, namely, that knowledge is a representation, *repraesentatio*. A representation with consciousness is *perceptio*. I have a delusion, and think that I am conscious of it. In so far as, in such a representation, I also attend to the object, it is *cognitio*, knowledge. Knowledge is twofold: *intuitus*, intuition, and *conceptus*, concept. *Intuitus* is a single representation, *repraesentatio singularis*.",[]],"wiener-s07-005":["In *perceptio* we do not refer our representations to an object. Through sensation, a feeling of well-being, pain, no object is known. It is only a kind of representation, but it is not distinguished from [or by] a particular object. In general, the relation of a representation to the subject is entitled sensation; its relation to the object, knowledge. Logic is concerned solely with knowledge, not with sensation",[]],"wiener-s07-006":["*Conceptus* is *repraesentatio communis*, [a representation] which is common to many things. Whoever first sought to have a representation of the colour red had to see the colour red. But when, in the colour red, he compared vermilion, c ramoisin [crimson] and ponceau [poppy] red, he became aware that there is in the colour red something general, which was also contained in other representations",[]],"wiener-s07-007":["of the colour red; and he thought under ‘red’ that which was common to many objects, and that was a concept. A concept is thus a representation which is common to many things. In *intuitus* I consider single things, for instance the sun, the earth. But if I think a certain genus of planets, it is *repraesentatio communis*, that is, *conceptus*. – A concept is distinguished from an intuition in that all intuition is single. He who sees the first tree does not know what it is that he sees. If he becomes aware that these objects have something in common, he thereupon leaves out everything in which they differ and takes together what they have in common; and so he has *repraesentationem communem*, that is, *conceptum*. It is evident from this that we ought not to say *conceptus communis*, since that would be a tautology, every *conceptus* being also *communis*. – Every *conceptus* is either *empiricus* or *purus*. A *conceptus empiricus* is one which is produced through the comparison of objects of experience. A *repraesentatio empirica* is one which springs from the senses. It can become a *conceptus* if I take what is common to several empirical representations. We see that in the distinction between *empiricus* and *purus* everything turns on the origin of the concept; this is therefore already a metaphysical enquiry. For logic does not ask whence concepts come, but how they can be formed and ordered according to the laws of the understanding. That there is a concept belongs, therefore, to logic; but whether it arises independently of experience or from experience is not a question for logic. – There are many *conceptus empirici*; that is, we often compare experiences and form from them a *repraesentatio communis*, and this is then entitled *empiricus conceptus*. – To the *empiricus* is contradistinguished the *conceptus purus*. *Purus* is the concept which has its origin, independently of experience, in the mere understanding; for instance, the concept of cause, of magnitude, of quality, etc. Whether there are such concepts is an enquiry which belongs to metaphysics, since this is *cognitio pura*. – Our moral philosophy has many [such] *conceptus*. The concept of virtue is not met with in experience; and even if the world displayed nothing but vices, we should still have a concept of virtue. – This *purus* may spring from the understanding; and, even though its ground lies solely in the understanding, its object can still be represented *in concreto*. For instance, cause and effect, [concepts] of the understanding. We can indeed distinguish in the understanding the things,",[]],"wiener-s07-008":["[and] sense [the things] of which we speak in [speaking of] effect, cause, etc.; but the concept of causality lies solely in the understanding. The question now arises: can the object of this concept of the understanding be met with in experience? Answer: Yes. This is done by means of examples. An example of causality is: fire destroys wood. –",[]],"wiener-s07-009":["Or this *conceptus purus* is so constituted that its object cannot be given in any experience. It is then a concept not of the understanding but of mere reason; that is, the concept is not to be understood from experience, but I go beyond the point to which experience leads me. – The *conceptus purus* is in general entitled *notio*. A *notio* is thus already more than a concept; for instance, the concept of possibility cannot be exhibited in experience. A *notio* whose object can be given in experience is entitled *notio intellectualis*. When the object cannot be given in any experience, it is entitled *notio rationis*, or *idea*. Thus the *notio*, in so far as it cannot be given in any experience and corresponds to no experience, is, properly speaking, an *idea*. The concept of the whole can be given in experience; it *est notio intellectualis* [is an intellectual notion]. But if I frame a further concept, for instance the concept of a world which is to be the unproduced whole comprehending all things, in which all the parts stand together in connection, this cannot be given in experience. For if the world is eternal, what can comprehend its entire eternity? The concept of the world is therefore merely an idea. – The concept of a part is a *conceptus purus intellectualis, seu notio*. But the concept of a part which is not composite is a *notio rationis*, an *idea*. So long as my reason represents to itself something divisible, this can always be divided further. But my reason demands in the end the last part, which cannot be further divided, that is, which is simple. This concept cannot be exhibited in experience; it is therefore a concept *a priori*, an idea. The concept of God is an idea. For experience cannot teach us whether all possible perfections may be anywhere united. – The whole of morals rests on ideas. Virtue [in its perfection] we cannot meet with among men at all. And yet my reason must have a concept of virtue as it must be in its complete perfection. We can indeed perceive virtue in experience, but much must still be added; it is therefore an idea. – The doctrine of ideas is very important, but belongs properly to metaphysics. It has hitherto been wrongly expounded. Yet if ideas are not rightly distinguished, we cannot rightly determine the use of our modes of knowledge. Ordinary authors use the word ‘idea’ quite wrongly. For instance, people speak of the idea of the colour red, although this is not even a *notio*. They speak of the idea of a triangle, although it is only a *notio*. In metaphysics it is shown that *rationes* are either intellectual or sensitive. *Rationes intellectuales* are necessity, cause, etc., which are given the name of categories. *Rationes sensitivae* are those in which an object of the senses is given which I can know not in empirical intuition but in pure intuition. The concept of a triangle is thus a *notio sensitiva*. The ideas exclude all our [empirical] knowledge [Kenntnisse]. They are derived solely from reason, yet they bear upon experience as rules according to which most experiences can be instituted.",[]],"wiener-s07-010":["How do concepts arise? That is, how do representations become concepts? Logic does not concern itself with how the *data* for a mode of knowledge must be constituted, but solely with what the understanding does in regard to them. It attends, therefore, to the form, and not to the object. How, then, does it come about that a *repraesentatio singularis* becomes *communis*? Answer: *per comparationem, separationem seu abstractionem* [through comparison, separation or abstraction]. I compare things, attend to what they have in common, and abstract from everything else; and this is then a *conceptus*, through which all these things can be thought.",[]],"wiener-s07-011":["It is a misuse in logic to retain the expression ‘abstract’ in such a way that we say *aliquid abstrahere* [to abstract something]. It is as if, in order to have the concept of a tree, I were to take the concept of the leaves and of the trunk by themselves, abstract from every difference in the tree, and say: what has a trunk and leaves is a tree. No; I do not abstract the leaves and the trunk; I retain them, and separate them from everything else. I must attend to what [the objects of] a mode of knowledge have in common, and abstract from that in which they differ, for instance from the largeness or smallness of the tree. The word must not, therefore, be used so as to mean *aliquid abstrahere*. I disregard the remaining [features of] things. Abstraction, accordingly, adds nothing; it cuts away everything that does not belong to the concept, and marks only what it has in common with other representations. *Comparatio* is thus required for distinguishing a concept: I must attend to the agreement and identity of things, which is entitled wit; and secondly, *abstractio*. – It is therefore wrongly expressed when we speak of *conceptibus abstractis*, *abstractis* being meant to signify *qui abstractus est a re* [that which has been abstracted from the thing]. This is false, *nam non abstrahimus a re, sed separamus conceptum ab aliis rebus, conceptum abstrahimus a ceteris* [for we do not abstract from the thing, but separate the concept from other things; we abstract the concept from the rest]. We must abstract from many things. If in scarlet I think purely the red colour, I already abstract from many colours; if in scarlet I think merely an extended being, I abstract from many more things; if I think merely that it is a thing, I abstract from still more. Properly speaking, however, we ought to say that the use of the concept is abstract. For every use of a concept is either *in abstracto* or *in concreto*. I know a concept *in concreto* when I apply it to what is contained under it. I think the concept *in abstracto* when I think it in general. From this we see that a mode of knowledge is abstract not because [rather: when] the concept is thought *in abstracto*, without being applied to objects. For instance, we speak of education and treat merely of the early formation of man, without speaking of its application to the rearing of children. Here the concept is expounded not *in concreto* at all, but merely *in abstracto*.",[]],"wiener-s08-001":["The form of a concept consists in its common validity: *Repraesentatio, quae pluribus est communis* [a representation which is common to several things]. It is this, then, that constitutes the *conceptus*. – I cannot say *conceptus communis*, since that would be a tautology. A tautology is an enunciation in which I make the concept distinct through itself. For if a representation is not a *repraesentatio communis*, it is no concept at all. Since this, then, is already contained in it, I must not add it. – But the use of a *conceptus* can be *singularis*. For what holds of many things can also be applied to a single case. I think a man *in individuo*; that is, I use the concept of man in order to have an *ens singulare* [singular being]. I can employ a concept in so far as it is applied to many objects; the concept is then used as a *repraesentatio communis*, that is, used *in abstracto*, for instance ‘house’. If, now, I say of all houses that they must have a roof, this is a *usus universalis*. It is, however, always the same concept, and is here used quite universally; for to have a roof holds for all houses. The use of the concept thus extends universally to all. A particular use, on the other hand, extends only to many; for instance, some houses must have a gateway. Or I use the concept only for a single thing; for instance, this house is finished in such and such a way. We therefore divide not concepts into *universales*, *particulares* and *singulares*, but judgments, as we shall presently hear. In my judgment I can compare the thing with all, with some, and with a single thing. This serves to determine our expressions exactly, and to prevent our speaking of everything in universal concepts. In this manner all concepts are universal, and can always hold, in a certain way, of other things. For instance, ‘plant’ holds of all grasses, but also of some bodies. The understanding is the faculty of concepts, and may also be so defined. We said above that the understanding is the faculty of rules. But this is the same thing; for when I give a concept, I always give a foundation of rules.",[]],"wiener-s08-002":["Concepts arise *per comparationem, reflexionem et abstractionem*. In one consciousness I comprehend many representations, in which I compare what is merely a repetition of the other. Through reflection, then, we know what many things have in common; thereupon, through abstraction, we take away that in which they do not agree, and there then remains the *repraesentatio communis*. No concept, therefore, comes to be without comparison, without perception of an agreement, and without abstraction. If I could not abstract, I should have no concept, since something other than what is common to the single representations would always come to mind. For instance, if someone, at the expression ‘house’, always thought only of the jug [Krug] he had seen, he would always retain an *intuitus*. We see that no concept comes to be through leaving out and abstracting; but this completes it, and causes it not to remain *singularis*. The positive element in the arising of a concept is comparing and reflecting; the negative, abstracting. Many a man finds it hard to abstract, even though he can compare and reflect. Hence one has no end of trouble in dealing with such people. Habit has already brought them to the point where it costs them great effort to leave out what does not belong to the concept. That most men have such bad principles is due to their thinking in so vulgar",[]],"wiener-s08-003":["a manner, and to their being unable to abstract the decorous, the seemly, etc., from a concept of virtue.",[]],"wiener-s08-004":["An abstract concept is abstract only in its use. It is less abstract when I state *in concreto* what I have said *in abstracto*. For instance: body is an extension which has a figure. I now give examples; that is, I take all the things which I had left out in the concept. The concept is used *in abstracto* when I leave out all the differences that are common [gemein] to the thing. The concept, consequently, always arises through abstracting; but the concept itself is not abstract, only its use. The use of a concept *in concreto* has its degrees, until finally the concept becomes *maxime in concreto* [concrete in the highest degree] and belongs only to an *individuum*. In the same way, I can also go ever further in abstraction; for instance, man, animal, living being, etc.",[]],"wiener-s08-005":["Every concept contains under it further possible concepts, and contains what is common to the different representations of several things. If, then, a concept contains something which is common to several things, it is itself contained in other possible concepts. It is a part of them, but contains only what they have in common, and leaves out what is different in them. Every universal concept is thus contained in the concepts from which it is abstracted. For instance, the concept ‘metal’ belongs to gold, copper, etc.",[]],"wiener-s08-006":["Conversely, these things are contained under it; and it is precisely this that constitutes the serviceableness of a concept. In intuition I have no other serviceableness than [that it gives] only a single object. Now the ground always contains the consequences under it. Accordingly every universal concept is a ground of knowledge [Erkenntnisgrund] of many things; and, equipped with a concept, I have a ground of knowledge of many things. The concept itself, however, is also contained in the things; for it constitutes only a part of their reason [Vernunft].",[]],"wiener-s08-007":["A concept is entitled a higher concept in so far as it contains others under it; and every concept is consequently a higher representation of others, since it always contains many under it. A lower concept is one which is contained under others. For instance, man: he is an animal. The concept of animal belongs to all men, and to still more things besides; consequently ‘man’ is a lower concept than ‘animal’. – All *conceptus* stand in such *relatio* to one another that one *conceptus* is always, relatively to another, *superior* and *inferior*, in so far as one is contained under the other; and from this there finally results a series of subordinated concepts. The *conceptus infimus* cannot be determined. For as soon as I have a concept which I apply to *individua*, it would still be possible that among the *individua*, although I make no further distinction, still smaller differences are to be found. In this gradation one concept is always again higher than another, until I reach the highest. The lowest knowledge is intuition, since it always relates to something individual [etwas eigenes]. The *conceptum summum* I can specify, since there must after all be a concept in which I can leave out everything. For if I wish to frame a higher concept, I must always abstract; but if I can no longer abstract, no higher concept can be framed, as with [the concept of] something. – The *conceptum superiorem* we entitle, in respect of its *inferior*, a *genus*. If, then, we compare many *conceptus* with one another, we can call each concept the *genus* in respect of its *inferior*, and *species* in respect of its *superior*. *Genus* and *species* thus make in themselves no difference in concepts, but only in the relation of concepts. ‘Scholar’ is a *genus* in respect of philosophers, but ‘scholar’ is a *species* in respect of man. We can now think a series of *genera* and *species*, among which some must be *genera superiora*, until we finally come to a *genus summum*, namely, something, object.",[]],"wiener-s08-008":["Every *species* is at the same time a *genus* in respect of the *species* contained under it. The *species infima* [lowest species] is lowest only comparatively, and is the last in use. It must always remain possible to find yet another *species*, through which the former would then in turn become a *genus*. But a *species* that is applied immediately to *individua* [individuals] may be entitled *species infima*. We may here take into consideration the extension and the content of a concept. *Sphaera* is the extension [Umfang] of a concept; it concerns the multitude of things that are contained under the concept. We consider the concept according to its content [Inhalt] when we look to the multitude of representations that are contained in the concept itself. The greater the extension of a concept, the smaller is its content, that is, the less it contains in itself. For example, the concept ‘man’ has a large extension; but the concept ‘Negro’ contains more, namely the concept of black colour as well, and consequently has more content. Now the higher a concept is, the more must we abstract from the manifold, and the more does its content dwindle. A concept is the higher the more things it is contained in, and the more it contains only what belongs to many things. Since in the case of the highest concept I must leave out so much that nothing remains to me save what is common to all things, this concept, for that very reason, contains the very least in itself. Consequently the lowest concepts will be determined from the highest; and this at once involves the correct *determination* of all things. A thoroughly determined concept is the lowest of all, since it is not common to several things. A higher concept is one that is undetermined *multimode* [in many ways]; and the highest concept is that which is undetermined in all respects. The logical *sphaera* always grows, just as a sheet of gold leaf stretches as it loses in thickness; and for this very reason it is so hard for man to ascend, and to think things that are without content. The nearer, on the other hand, concepts approach to experience, the fuller or more *concrete* is the representation. The question arises which is the better, a very concrete or a very abstract representation, that is, whether we should approach the *individua* or the *genus summum* [highest genus]. Answer: the one is as good as the other. It is admirable when our representations have a large *sphaera*; for a concept is then valid everywhere for what is contained under it, and the universality of knowledge accordingly always rests on the *sphaera*. Nonetheless the concept loses in content; and this defect is unavoidable, and, in respect of the human understanding, to be regretted. Every lower concept is also entitled *angustior* [narrower], and every concept that has a higher *sphaera* is entitled *latior* [wider]. The *genus* is therefore always the *conceptus latior*, the *species* the *conceptus angustior*. When two concepts stand side by side and have the same *sphaera*, they are entitled *conceptus reciproci* [reciprocal concepts]. The chapter on *definitions* is the most important of all in logic; for the greatest demand that can be made of a philosopher is always that he should define his concept. All logical perfection of our knowledge consisted, [as we have seen,] in our concept's being distinct. The second perfection was that it should at the same time be complete, that is, that its distinctness should rest not merely on some clear marks, but on as many as, taken together, make up the whole concept. Now when the concept is exhaustive [ausführlich], no mark is indeed lacking. But although none is lacking, one may perhaps be superfluous, inasmuch as one mark may already be contained in another. It is an imperfection in a concept when we needlessly multiply marks, and by tautologies make one mark into many. To the logical perfection of a concept, therefore, it also belongs that the concept be precise [abgemessen], that is, that it contain no more marks than are needed to make up the concept. ‘Abgemessen’, *praecisus*, is perhaps not good Latin. But *determinatus*, which the author entitles ‘abgemessen’, means nothing at all. For an *indeterminatus* concept is one that contains too little; *determinatus* would consequently be a concept to which more and more is always being added, and which is therefore not exhaustive. *Praecidere*, however, is used of plants, for pruning; and so it is also with our concepts, which we prune, as it were, of their superfluous marks. If we take *completudo* [completeness] and precision together, we may call a concept *adaequat* [adequate]. It is adequate to the object when its marks contain neither more nor less than just what makes up the concept. That the concept be not too small, it must be complete; that it be not too large, it must be precise. A *definition* is therefore a *conceptus distinctus adaequatus*. Wolff, however, takes the word ‘adequate’ quite otherwise, and calls a concept adequate when I have not merely clear marks, but know the marks themselves distinctly. These, however, are intensive degrees of distinctness, of which we are not here speaking. If the marks are clear, we already have a definition after the fact. If I can also define the marks, this is then a definition of the parts of the definition. If with him that concept is already entitled adequate whose marks are merely distinct, he has thereby already reached the end. But we can surely go on to make the marks of marks distinct. With us, on the other hand, the expression ‘adequate’, or *conceptus adaequatus*, completes the definition and fits the object. Our definition consequently says just as much as *definitio est conceptus distinctus completus praecisus* [a definition is a distinct, complete and precise concept].",[]],"wiener-s09-001":["A definition has a certain perfection beyond which nothing can go. It might be objected: Do you not, in the definition, give marks of marks? Answer: We can also define the marks of the marks, and make a new definition out of them. Since definitions thus constitute the greatest perfection of a concept, we must know exactly all their *requisita* [requirements], and see how far it is feasible to make them, and how far we can get with them. All our concepts are either given or made concepts. A concept is given in so far as it does not spring from my choice [Willkür]. It may, however, be given either *a priori*, in the understanding alone, or *a posteriori*, through experience. I have many concepts which are given me through the nature of my understanding, and which I have not invented: for example, the concept of cause, of time, etc. In like manner many concepts are given us through experience: for example, that water is a fluid body.",[]],"wiener-s09-002":["To the *conceptus dati* [given concepts] are opposed the *conceptus factitii*, the made concepts, which are likewise made either *a priori* or *a posteriori*. A concept is made *a priori* when it is made through pure reflection, without the objects being given through experience: for example, I represent to myself a chiliagon without ever having seen one. Such a concept is also entitled *conceptus fictitius*, a feigned concept [gedichteter Begriff]; ‘a devised concept’ [ausgedachter Begriff] seems to me better. I can also make a concept *a posteriori*, in such a way that the object is given me in experience. For example, I have a piece of metal; this is always given *a posteriori*, not made. But if I wish to have a distinct concept of it, I must test the metal for all its properties, and in this way I find them through various experiences which do not lie in the concept. The nature of metal is therefore a concept made *a posteriori*.",[]],"wiener-s09-003":["To make a concept *a posteriori* thus means to trace given experience further, and so to extract an adequate concept. This, then, would be nothing more than to enlarge the concept.",[]],"wiener-s09-004":["All our concepts, in so far as they are given us, whether *a priori* or *a posteriori*, can be defined only *per analysin*, by dissection. For since the concept is given, I can make it distinct only by making clear the marks that lie in it; and that is just what analysis is. If this analysis is complete, complete distinctness results from it. If, further, the marks are no more than are needed, the analysis is precise; and in this way a definition arises.",[]],"wiener-s09-005":["But when concepts are not given but made, so that we are not merely to bring about the distinctness of the concept but are to make the concept itself, we shall achieve a definition only *per synthesin*. For example, if I wish to represent to myself a spirit, I must make the concept for myself. I say: I will represent to myself a thinking being which is not combined with any body. Here I have, through the definition, first made the concept. Made concepts, therefore, can be defined in no other way than *per synthesin*. The whole of mathematics defines *per synthesin*; it defines not given but made concepts. ‘I will think a figure’, says the *mathematicus*, ‘which shall look thus and thus, and be entitled so and so.’ We may also have a concept *a priori factitium* whose *materials* have indeed been given by experience, while the concept itself has been made: for example, let us think an impregnable fortress, such as does not exist at all. The materials, such as ditch, stone, lie in experience; the concept itself is *factitius*. So it is also with him who invents a new instrument.",[]],"wiener-s09-006":["There are, however, *factitii conceptus* *a posteriori* also. If I wish to define an experience, by explaining through experience what is not contained in my concept, this too must be done *per synthesin*. I wish to obtain for myself a wider concept than I had; and in this way I finally arrive at a concept consisting of marks which I have gathered together *per synthesin* from experience. It is therefore a universal rule that all given concepts can be defined only analytically, and all made concepts, whether made by the understanding or by experience, only synthetically. When can I say that a concept is defined in analysis? When all its marks are complete and precise. When can we be certainly convinced that our analysis is complete? Answer: We can never be so, since this can be ascertained only through trials, in which we may still always overlook a great deal. All analytic definitions are consequently attended with uncertainty. If a concept is an empirical concept, it is still less possible to know [merklich zu wissen] that I have analysed it correctly. Through analysis we never have apodictic certainty. All analytic definitions are known to us in such a way that no sure *criterium* can be given of them.",[]],"wiener-s09-007":["Synthetic definitions, in so far as they have a feigned object, can never err, since I say at my own choice: this is what I will think. Synthetic definitions of concepts drawn from experience are altogether impossible. We cannot make any object in experience complete, and state all the marks that make up its concept. For experimental",[]],"wiener-s09-008":["physics is still discovering, from time to time, new properties of bodies.",[]],"wiener-s09-009":["Analytically, I make a given concept distinct by merely expounding [exponiren] the concept; *per synthesin*, on the other hand, I make a distinct concept for myself. When we are to define a concept analytically, it is merely given to us, and we have only to make it complete and precise. If this analysis is incomplete, it is indeed no definition; but it is nonetheless already a degree of distinctness, which can be carried further and can approach ever more nearly to *completudo*. Before a concept becomes complete, it must necessarily first be incomplete; and, secondly, it is not always possible to attain to complete concepts. Can there be a distinct concept that is not yet complete? I can certainly make use of a complete [incomplete?] analysis too, since I do already have some clear marks. Hence it is not always possible, in the case of every concept, that I should have defined it; and it is not necessary, in order to speak of an object, that one should have defined it *per analysin*. For where I cannot say everything, I must say only as much as I can say with truth. Definitions are consequently not so necessary as is believed; and this is to be noted because definition is in most cases extremely difficult, and analytic completion cannot always be expected. That we may be sure, however, that in [incomplete] analysis we know enough is evident from this, that an incomplete analysis is already a part of the definition, and is consequently still true and correct. For example, if I say that virtue is a readiness [Fertigkeit] in morally good actions, and I leave out that it is also a mastery over our inclinations, I then ask: Can nothing whatever be inferred from the first, incomplete concept? On the contrary: from it most philosophers have deduced the whole of their moral philosophy. From every mark of a thing I can draw consequences. If I know only some marks in the thing, some consequences can be drawn; but if I know everything, all consequences can be drawn. To begin with the definition, then, as is done in most philosophies, and to engage in nothing until one has the definition in hand, is in fact to make all inquiry impossible; for I must, after all, begin from what is imperfect. The endeavour of philosophers is, indeed, to attain through definitions to the greatest perfection. But we must not say that what has received no definition",[]],"wiener-s09-010":["deserves no treatment at all. For of many things we can give no complete concept. For example, jurists have never yet been able to give a definition of equity.",[]],"wiener-s09-011":["Concepts that are given *a priori*, independently of experience, can be defined analytically. For here the concept concerns an object which I am to know only through the understanding; consequently whatever I am to say of it must lie in my understanding, since we cannot go outside our understanding and seek elsewhere. Thus the whole of metaphysics and moral philosophy has to do with analytic definition. But although these two sciences are the true *objects* of analytic definitions, it is nonetheless evident that definitions are not always indispensably necessary. Rather, in analytic definition we must never begin by saying: this or that is to be a definition. For it requires proof that the analysis is complete: I must first show that the marks lie in the concept, and then show that, taken together, they make up the concept.",[]],"wiener-s09-012":["It would be most excellent, and would give great value to our modes of knowledge, if in philosophy we had all such concepts adequate to the thing, which at the same time did not overstep precision; for that is the attainment [Erlangung] of our sciences. But since we are not in a position to accomplish this, we must make do with as many clear marks of a concept as we can find in our reason. Such incomplete concepts, which occur in physics also, we call *descriptions*.",[]],"wiener-s09-013":["To wish to say outright that this alone makes up the concept is too dictatorial; for we must first say how we arrived at the analysis. What Wolff attempts in his philosophy is therefore altogether mistaken, and for that reason each of his definitions in philosophy is also false. The concept is commonly analysed incompletely, although it cannot be denied that he gives fairly clear marks and often comes very near to the whole concept; and his definitions in philosophy and in natural right can nonetheless be used very well, since they display much acuteness. He had learned the practice of putting the definition first from mathematics, where it is indeed quite proper, since mathematics has synthetic definitions. One finds the same thing even in company. People speak, for example, of right, and someone says: ‘Define right for me.’ He wants me to have fathomed the whole depth of the concept, whereas we could reason about what we know and what does, after all, also lie in the concept.",[]],"wiener-s09-014":["Hence it comes, too, that such definitions are always 99 / 100 false, and Baumeister's defined philosophy swarms with errors. Of mathematical definitions not a single one is false, since the concepts are made at choice; all analytic [definitions], however, are of given concepts. Can empirical concepts be defined analytically? When the natural scientist defines water, for example, as a fluid body of such a kind, without taste or colour, it is plain how unsteady the definition is. Anyone who does not otherwise know water will not come to know it by this means. But it is not at all necessary to define empirical concepts *per analysin*. For of what use is such a definition to me? When I say the word ‘water’, the other understands me. But for teaching him to know it, the definition is quite unfit. For so very little lies in the concept of water that I at once go beyond the concept and must gather new marks through experience; and that is to define a concept synthetically, not analytically by exposition. The empirical concept can indeed be defined analytically according to what lies in it; but that is never what is sought, since so little lies in it. Nor, again, can it be defined synthetically, since we cannot know all the possible marks that experience can teach of an object. Consequently empirical concepts can never be defined.",[]],"wiener-s09-015":["The result of all that has been said is therefore this. Our concepts are either given or made. The given concepts are either empirical concepts or concepts of the understanding. A given empirical concept can be properly explained neither *per synthesin* nor *per analysin*: in the former case because I can explain it only [ Textlücke ] through new marks, in the latter because too little lies in it to distinguish it from others. With concepts of the understanding, and indeed given ones, no proper analytic explanation is possible either; this, however, lies not in the thing but in us, since it is difficult to state the marks of a concept precisely. Arbitrary concepts can be made both *a priori* and *a posteriori*, but only synthetically. Only *conceptus arbitrarii* (*factitii*), which are made *per synthesin arbitrariam*, can be defined synthetically; and to these belong all mathematical concepts. Indeed, what is more, with a *conceptus arbitrarius* we must always proceed by putting the definition first, since it is precisely through the definition that the concept is brought forth, and through it that I declare what I mean to say by such a concept, which I have made at my own choice. Without definition, consequently, such a concept would be nothing.",[]],"wiener-s09-016":["The nominal definition is that distinct concept which suffices for distinguishing a thing from others. The real definition is that distinct concept which suffices for knowing and separating out from it everything that belongs to the thing. It consequently suffices internally to explain the thing, and to understand what belongs to it.",[]],"wiener-s09-017":["It is essential, and contains the concept of the essence of the thing. The nominal definition, on the other hand, is only externally sufficient, in comparison with others, for discerning the thing's identity with, or difference from, others. It signifies scarcely more than what the expression ‘nominal definition’ says: a certain attestation of the designation of the thing, serving to make the name of the thing distinct, without the thing itself being thereby better understood. It does not comprise the essence of the thing, but only a comparison of some marks of difference with others. It can therefore have merely *comparative* validity. For example, if I say: man is an animal that has the capacity to speak, I can here distinguish him from all animals (though not yet from the starling). Nonetheless, in comparison with other animals the concept is already sufficient.",[]],"wiener-s09-018":["How can I know that my concept suffices to distinguish the thing from all possible [things]? Answer: Only those marks can suffice absolutely which, taken together, make up the whole essence of the thing; for the whole essence of a thing cannot be common to two things. And this is the real definition. Nominal definitions are valid only *in tantum* [in so far], to the extent that I make use of them. Real definitions are therefore definitions proper. When Wolff says that the origin [of the thing] can also be comprehended from the concept given in the real definition, this is false; for the origin must afterwards be inferred from the real definition, since the real definition makes possible the judgment how the thing might have arisen. His examples throw very good light on what we have said. When, for instance, he gives as a real definition: a circle is a curved line which moves round a fixed point and whose parts are equally distant from the centre, the first part is superfluous, since it can be inferred from the last. The origin is only a consequence of the real definition; and that is indeed all that Wolff himself says. Otherwise the real definition has nothing to do with origin, but only with the inner possibility of the thing. If I know a thing by an *attributum proprium* [proper attribute], I can also define it by such an *attributum proprium*: for example, man is an animal that judges. Since judgment is an *attributum proprium* of man, it must necessarily flow from the whole essence of man. If it flowed only from a part of the essence, several things of different essence might, as regards this part, agree with that essence. The definition therefore really holds for the whole essence, since it cannot be understood otherwise than of the whole essence. Through the real definition the thing can at all times be distinguished, and from all others. It is very difficult to distinguish the two [kinds of definition], and what was only a nominal definition is often taken for a real definition. With synthetic objects the definitions are always real; for the object is given *a priori* only through this one definition, and through it alone is it possible to think it. But to make real definitions of given, analytic objects is very difficult. I cannot even know whether my nominal definition is sufficient, since I can never find the marks that distinguish the thing from all other possible things, being unable to compare it with all possible things. Only when I know that my marks exhaust the whole concept, that is, when I can give a real definition, is a nominal definition certainly sufficient too. Such is the case with all definitions in natural science. In moral philosophy, metaphysics, etc., on the other hand, nothing but real definitions must occur, since this is, after all, the goal towards which we must all direct our analysis, namely to achieve a complete concept. Definition [Description?] is properly a sum, a *recension* or *exposition* of marks, in so far as they contain the materials for a possible concept. Describing is subject to no determinate rule: one may be as circumstantial, or as brief, as one pleases. We can accomplish much by it, inasmuch as we thereby first make things recognizable. Thus descriptions always precede the definitions of such concepts as are empirical; indeed, since we cannot define empirical concepts, they are capable only of description. Concepts *a priori*, on the other hand, when they are made *per synthesin*, as in mathematics, must always be defined. A given concept of reason, however, need not always be defined; one is then often content with an incomplete concept. But descriptions are altogether unsuited to concepts of reason. Lavater, for example, who through the liveliness of his imagination indulges in excursions rather than exhausting the matter, gives very many effects of love when he describes love. But from all this we cannot extract precisely the concept of what love is. Descriptions are not at all suited to the speculative sciences, but only *in praxi*, where I can combine a lively image with my concept.",[]],"wiener-s09-019":["Distinctness is what is most important of all in a definition. Then follows *completudo*. Precision, however, is not so important as *completudo*. This precision is difficult in many cases, because we unwittingly take a mark twice, regarding as different things that have different names. For example: a body is an extended, divisible being. Here extension already lies in divisibility. In descriptions this whole matter is not taken at all strictly, since there we seek only the proposition for a possible definition, which perhaps we have no intention of making. Thus in the catechism I can give many descriptions of God according to His attributes, although in the end I see that the precise concept of God is [that of] a being of all beings. Even in a definition nothing much depends on it if there is some want of precision, and there is too much in the definition. That can do no harm at all, except that it offends against the canon: *quod fieri potest per pauca* etc. Precision is only a law of economy, and facilitates the consciousness of *completudo*. How do we judge a definition that is presented to us? Answer: We ask:",[]],"wiener-s09-020":["1.) Whether the definition, as a proposition, is true; whether what is said of the thing in the definition really belongs to it. For example: ‘ground is that from which I know why something is’ is a true proposition.",[]],"wiener-s09-021":["2.) The concept must be distinct. Although something is true as a proposition, it is not on that account distinct as a concept. Thus the above example is not in the least made distinct to me by the definition. For ‘why’ means as much as ‘through what ground’. I should consequently have said: a ground is that from which I know through what ground something is true. This is therefore *idem per idem*. That is a cardinal fault, and a very common one; most explanations in conversation are of this kind.",[]],"wiener-s09-022":["3.) If the concept is true as a proposition and is also distinct, I can ask whether the distinct concept is also complete: whether the marks, taken together, make up the whole concept. This is very difficult. For how can we know that nothing more is lacking to our concept, and that we have left nothing out? We do not, indeed, observe that anything is still lacking. But that is no sufficient ground for calling the concept complete; for how often do we fail to observe something which we nonetheless find afterwards?",[]],"wiener-s09-023":["4.) The exhaustive concept must also be determinate [bestimmt, i.e. precise]. This can still be done, and if it is lacking, it is no great fault. Precision is the reduction of the definition *ad minimos terminos* [to its lowest terms]: just as 12 / 24 can be reduced to 1 / 2, so I must also be able to prune [praecidiren] the concept. It is very fine when we can do this, and know how to express all our definitions in sententious propositions. This is the elegance of a mode of knowledge.",[]],"wiener-s09-024":["Accordingly a false definition may contain true propositions, and for that very reason be possible, although it cannot be held to be a concept adequate to the thing. Only I must not convert it; otherwise an error arises, since the one mark does not exhaust the whole thing.",[]],"wiener-s09-025":["By what is a concept made distinct? By clear marks. But if these marks are tautological, that is, if one is contained in the other, the concept is not made in the least distinct, although it sometimes appears to be so, because the tautology lies concealed in it. To this we must pay close attention in examining a definition.",[]],"wiener-s09-026":["How, then, are definitions found? It goes without saying that this concerns only analytic definition. For in the synthesis of an arbitrary concept, where I make a concept, the definition need not be found, since I state plainly what I have devised in the understanding. Such definitions of the mathematician are properly only declarations of what he intends; and mathematical definitions consequently require no rule at all, since he need only tell everyone distinctly what concept he wishes to establish and fix. Yet mathematicians do sometimes mistake their concept, and this less often in ancient times than in modern. Euclid never did so. But Wolff brings all philosophy into mathematics. When he gives the concept of similarity, he analyses the concept; but there may here be great danger that he has not made his analysis complete. Yet everything the *mathematicus* says must be indubitably certain. He should therefore have said: a similar figure, which I wish to think, is etc. Here, consequently, the definition must always be synthetic.",[]],"wiener-s09-027":["The rules for analytic definition are as follows. Seek true marks. You wish to analyse; therefore attend to the marks, and seek true ones that really belong to the thing. Those propositions which are found through the analysis of a concept, and through which I become conscious of the marks of the concept, might be entitled elementary propositions, since they set out the elements for the definition. For although no definition yet results from them, they are nonetheless the representations [required] for it, etc. For example, when I say: virtue is a readiness, a freedom in acting, etc., I am here seeking out through analysis one mark after another, in order thereby to arrive at the definition.",[]],"wiener-s09-028":["Now we analyse the concept, and seek as many elementary propositions as we can. But then we must also see whether one mark does not already lie in this or that other; and here we must take care never to posit a mark twice in the definition. When I have sought out true propositions, and have seen in doing so that these marks are different marks, I gather them all together again, compare them with the whole concept, and so put them together again. Herein lies the whole difficulty: dissection can well enough be accomplished, but to know whether the marks are present completely is extremely difficult, and we have reason to place great distrust in it. Since, therefore, we cannot always assert that our analysis has been complete, we must attend to our definition with fear and trembling. And since the matter is so difficult, I would say: I shall define by way of trial; I shall lay down a definition as it were as an experiment. I will not rely on it as on something free from error, and I will regard it as though it were not a definition. Here, then, I can maintain that even if it is no definition, it must nonetheless be (1) a true proposition and (2) a distinct concept, even though not a completely distinct concept.",[]],"wiener-s09-029":["I shall be able to make good use of this presumptive definition [*praesumtive definition*]. For I shall always be able to say: *cui competit definitum, ei competit definitio* [whatever the thing defined belongs to, the definition belongs to also]. For instance, he who is virtuous has a firm resolve to act. This *definitio* always belongs to the *definitum*, the virtuous man. But I shall not be able to say conversely: *cui competit definitio, illi competit definitum* [whatever the definition belongs to, the thing defined belongs to also]. I cannot say conversely that he who has a firm resolve to act is virtuous; [someone may have such a resolve] and yet I do not call him virtuous. I see, therefore, that from a presumptive definition I can indeed form any direct concept, but I shall not be able to convert it, because I have not named everything, and have not completely known all the marks. I can say, for example, that man is a rational animal; but not every rational animal is a man, for something is lacking in the definition. If at last I believe that I have all the marks completely together, and then combine them, I have nothing further to attend to save that my definition be precise. This last is good and neat, but does not constitute an essential requisite [*requisitum*] of definition; so that even without precision such concepts are sufficiently serviceable. If I have sought only the first element, namely, merely true propositions in the definition, I define *obscurum per aeque obscurum* [the obscure by what is equally obscure], and these might be entitled tautological propositions. Much can be accomplished with definitions, provided only that they are true propositions, even though the concept is not an exhaustive one. But whoever would reciprocate [the definition and the thing defined] must have a complete concept.",[]],"wiener-s09-030":["General rules of definition.",[]],"wiener-s09-031":["1.) Definition should not proceed in a circle. To explain in a circle is to define by a mark which presupposes the concept as *definitum*. For instance, if I define ground as a cause why something is, my mark is ‘why’, that is, ‘from what ground’, a mark which already signifies the concept of ground. This *circulus vitiosus* [vicious circle] occurs very frequently. A mark is taken, and without further examining whether the concept is by itself, independently of the concept [defined], definitively clear, *idem per idem* [the same by the same] is explained.",[]],"wiener-s09-032":["2.) *Definitio nec sit latior, nec angustior suo definito* [the definition should be neither wider nor narrower than what it defines]. I require a sum of marks which, taken together, exhibit the whole [concept]. We therefore seek neither more nor fewer [marks] than are needed in order that *definitio* and *definitum* may be reciprocable, that is, that the one may be put in place of the other. For instance, if I say that a body is an impenetrable being which is heavy, the *definitio* is *angustior definito* [narrower than the thing defined]. For through the mark of heaviness the concept of body is too restricted and narrow; it applies only to the bodies which we know. Consequently, when the definition contains too much determination, it becomes *angustior definito*. But the more undetermined a concept is, the greater is its *sphaera*, *et latior est definitio definito* [and the definition is wider than the thing defined]. For instance, if I say ‘figure’, this applies to many things; if I say ‘a triangular figure’, it is already more restricted, etc. The concept is *latior definito* when it is not sufficiently determined through the definition; it is *angustior* when it is too much narrowed through so much determination.",[]],"wiener-s10-001":["Here we can be quite brief. A universal concept has a *sphaera*, and has lower concepts under it. These lower concepts we examine in so far as they are distinguished from one another. Division [*Eintheilung*] is thus the complete representation [of these lower concepts], in so far as they are considered according to their difference, wherein, taken together, they are equal to the *sphaera* of the whole concept. For instance, all beings on the earth are either organised or not organised; the organised are plants or animals. Logical division is therefore nothing but the division of the *sphaera* of a concept. This dividing is something other than the partition [*Theilen*] of a concept. In dividing, I distinguish the manifold under the concept, that is, its *sphaera*; in partition, I analyse the concept. [In division] I do not dissect the concept itself; I divide only the *sphaera*, the lower concepts in so far as they are contained under the universal.",[]],"wiener-s10-002":["The greater the *sphaera* of a concept, the less it contains in itself; and conversely, it contains the more under it, the less it has in itself. In every division of a concept, therefore, we divide this concept into its parts without partitioning the concept; that is, we divide the multitude of things which are contained under the universal concept. This can be continued; and there will be the more members of the division, the greater the *sphaera* of the concept, and the smaller its *sphaera*, the fewer members of the division there will be. Since, then, every concept has a greater *sphaera* the fewer parts it has, it follows that a concept can be the more divided, the fewer parts it has. For instance, we represent many things under the concept of animal. There are animals which move on land, in the air, and in the water, and the mark common to all of them is motion. Here we have not partitioned, that is, analysed, the concept of animal; for then we should have had to say that an animal is a matter which lives, etc. No, we divide the concept: we merely see how many species are contained under it. In every division we represent, first, that a multitude of different things completely fills the *sphaera* of the concept, that is, is equal to it, and that consequently no concept is lacking as a part; secondly, that these different concepts, which are all to be contained in the *sphaera* of the concept, are distinct from one another, so that one part of the *sphaera* is not contained in another. Accordingly, to keep to our example, we say: all animals, divided according to motion, are such as can move either on the earth, or in the air, or in the water. This ‘either … or’ expresses that they are distinct, and that one species is opposed to the other; while the word ‘all’ expresses that the marks together make up the concept. –",[]],"wiener-s10-003":["In every division, then, we have a *sphaera* and *membra dividentia* [dividing members]. The latter must be opposed to one another, and must also, taken together, fill the whole *sphaera* of the *conceptus*. For instance, if I say that all men are either virtuous or vicious, this will not do; for there are men who have no character at all, a savage, for example. There occur in logic many rules which it is well to point out, in order to show that we understand all the acts of the understanding which take place in its operations. But these rules are of such a kind that we can very well dispense with them as precepts, since everyone already observes them of his own accord, and can afterwards also state them well enough *in abstracto*. Such, for instance, is this rule of division: the divided concept must be neither wider nor narrower than all the members of the division, when these can be taken together through an opposition; that is, no member must be lacking in the division. For if a member is lacking, the concept would have a greater *sphaera*, since I should not have enumerated all the members; that is, it would be wider. But if I state more members than there really are, the divided concept has a smaller *sphaera*. Here we see how difficult it is to give a rule of the understanding *in abstracto*; *in concreto*, however, everyone thinks it without being conscious of it. In such cases, therefore, we must not think of the rules *in abstracto*, but of the examples *in concreto* which we have before us. The members of the division must also be opposed to one another, for otherwise I should not be able to say ‘either … or’. From all this we see what has already been noted in the *prolegomena*, p. 14. The common and sound understanding is very well acquainted with the rules, but knows them only *in concreto*; the speculative understanding, on the other hand, can also prove them *in abstracto*. In mathematics such rules are quite in place; but mathematics is also a science which cannot be thought at all without abstraction. A divided concept must not contradict any member of the division; as, for example, if I say that all triangles are round and quadrangular. In a division the members must be contained under the concept, that is, the concept which is divided must be contained in them. This is not the case here, since neither round nor quadrangular triangles are contained under the concept of triangle.",[]],"wiener-s10-004":["We must distinguish division, subdivision, and codivision [*Nebeneintheilung*]. Division in general is the representation of the manifold concepts which are opposed to one another and which fill the whole *sphaera* of the concept. This can now be continued. I can divide a concept in one *respectus* [respect], and again in another. All [men] are, as regards character, virtuous and vicious; as regards sex, men and women; as regards age, young and old; as regards knowledge, learned and unlearned, etc. This is codivision, in which I divide each time only a given *conceptus*. But I can also subdivide the concept: I then divide the members of the division. Every *subdivisio* is thus a division of a *membrum dividens*. For instance, men are learned or unlearned. The learned are learned in reason or learned in experience. Those learned in reason are philosophers or mathematicians, etc. For one who assumes *species infimae* [lowest species], subdivisions will at last come to an end. But since we have just shown (p. 373) that, by the nature of the case, every species can always contain *species inferiores* [lower species], we shall never arrive at concepts whose subordinate concepts could not in turn be divided. Subdivision must therefore proceed to infinity, although, it is true, many a subdivision comes to an end *comparative* [comparatively] for us.",[]],"wiener-s10-005":["Codivision likewise proceeds to infinity. For instance, I can divide the triangle with respect to its sides [*laterum*] into equilateral and non-equilateral, and with respect to its angles into right-angled and oblique-angled. More [codivisions] can hardly be given here. But in respect of the things of nature innumerable codivisions can be given.",[]],"wiener-s10-006":["*Quaeritur* [the question is]: How, properly, does all division proceed? Is division through our pure understanding always a pure division into two parts only, and then into subdivisions; that is, is it always a dichotomy, so that all other divisions are only subdivisions? Or can I from the very outset divide a concept into more than two parts? *Resp.* [Answer]: It is evident that every immediate division is a dichotomy. When we divide into several parts, it is polytomy, which is in every case a subdivision. For instance, if I want to divide an apple into many pieces, I must first divide it into two pieces, whether these differ in size or not. Thereafter I can divide it into more parts; but these are then already subdivisions. Every immediate division is consequently a dichotomy. Dichotomy is at once achieved through the mere word ‘not’. For instance, triangles are either equilateral or not equilateral; the [non-]equilateral are either *aequicrura* [isosceles] or *scalena* [scalene]. Consequently, when mathematicians divide triangles into *aequilatera*, *aequicrura* and *scalena*, they have brought the subdivision under the immediate division, and have divided wrongly. But since this [procedure] is so lengthy, because with many subdivisions we at last lose sight of the *conceptus divisus*, it is not always observed.",[]],"wiener-s11-001":["Judgment is, *generaliter* [in general], the representation of the unity in a relation of several modes of knowledge. A judgment is the representation of the manner in which concepts universally and objectively belong to one consciousness. When we think two representations as combined together as modes of knowledge, and as together constituting one knowledge, this is a judgment. In every judgment, therefore, there is a certain relation of different representations, in so far as they belong to one knowledge. For instance, I say: man is not immortal. In this knowledge I think the concept of being mortal through the concept of man; and it is thereby that this knowledge, which constitutes the unity of two different representations, becomes a judgment.",[]],"wiener-s11-002":["To every judgment there belong matter and form. The matter is the modes of knowledge *generaliter*, and the concepts; the form must constitute the combination",[]],"wiener-s11-003":["and unity of the representation. If, for instance, I say: God is just, evil is punished, and another were to ask, ‘Are these rhapsodies that you are producing?’, I should answer, ‘No! Because God is just, he punishes evil’; and this then constitutes the form through which the knowledge was brought to unity. All acts of the understanding which occur in a judgment are reducible to four; and all judgments are considered according to these.",[]],"wiener-s11-004":["Namely, first, as regards their quality, they are divided into affirmative, negative, and infinite judgments. The logicians [*Logici*] do indeed say that infinite judgments can be employed as affirmative; but that is a proposition which can be set forth in a special remark. In principle, however, [the infinite judgment] is as regards its form something different; and at the outset we must divide in accordance with the difference in the act of our understanding. How much is thought under [each member] belongs to a remark.",[]],"wiener-s11-005":["2.) As regards quantity, our judgments are divided into universal, particular, and singular judgments. And although the logicians show that singular judgments, so far as their matter is concerned, are equivalent to universal judgments, a singular judgment is none the less different from universal ones; and this distinction must be made at the outset, although we can afterwards say that singular judgments belong with the universal. The *actus* of the understanding are obviously different, although we see that the one employment of the understanding is as valid as the other.",[]],"wiener-s11-006":["3.) As regards relation, judgments are divided into categorical, hypothetical, and disjunctive judgments.",[]],"wiener-s11-007":["4.) As regards modality, into problematic, assertoric, and apodeictic judgments. Each of these four functions thus yields a special kind of judgment.",[]],"wiener-s11-008":["Quality is the relation of concepts in so far as they stand to one another in a relation of unity. According to it judgments are divided into affirmative, when I positively combine one concept with another; negative, when I separate one concept from another; infinite, when I limit one concept through another. For instance, ‘men are mortal’: here I affirm mortality of man, or I think men as standing under the concept of mortality. ‘No man is mortal’: here I deny mortality of man; when I think man, I think him as distinguished from everything that is mortal. Thus *anima non est mortalis* [the soul is not mortal] is a negative proposition; whereas *anima est non mortalis* [the soul is non-mortal] is an infinite proposition. All affirmative propositions show their affirmation through the copula *est*, which indicates the relation of two concepts. When the copula *est* stands *simpliciter* [simply], it signifies the connection of two [concepts]. When the copula *est* is affected by the *non*, it signifies the opposition of the two concepts, and indicates that the one concept does not belong to the other, or is not contained in the *sphaera* of the other. For instance, in *anima non est mortalis* I represent that the soul does not include mortality in itself. But if I say *anima est non mortalis*, I say not merely that the soul contains nothing mortal, but also that it is contained in the *sphaera* of all that is not mortal. Something special is thus being said here: namely, that I not merely exclude a concept from the *sphaera* of another concept, but also think it under the whole remaining *sphaera* which does not belong under the concept that is excluded. I do not properly say *est immortalis*; what I say is that the soul can be counted among all the concepts whatsoever that may be thought outside the concept of mortality. And this is what properly constitutes infinite judgments. –",[]],"wiener-s11-009":["Affirmation and negation are accordingly qualities in judgment. Not every judgment that is negative is a negative judgment; that is so only when the negation affects the copula. A judgment in which the negation affects not the copula but the predicate, as happens in an infinite judgment, so that the copula is free of all negation, is accordingly an affirmative judgment. Consequently all infinite judgments are affirmative, since the negation affects only the predicate. Yet although every infinite judgment has the nature of an affirmative one, there is always a negation present: not, indeed, of the judgment, that is, of the relation of the concepts, but of the predicate.",[]],"wiener-s11-010":["The relation is indeed the same as in an affirmative judgment; but the negation is always present, and consequently [infinite judgments] are distinguished from affirmative ones. In logic this matter appears to be a subtlety [*subtilitaet*]; but in metaphysics it becomes important not to have passed it over here, for there the difference between reality, negation, and limitation is greater. In limitations I think something positive, yet not merely positive, but also negative: it is something positive that is limited. – They are entitled *judicia infinita*, because they are unbounded. They always say only what [the subject] is not; and of such predicates",[]],"wiener-s11-011":["I can form innumerable ones; for the *sphaera* of the predicates which, affected with *non*, can be asserted of the subject is infinite.",[]],"wiener-s11-012":["The principle of all possible *praedicata contrarie opposita* [contrarily opposed predicates] must come from the thing [*Sache*] itself. This is the principle of complete determination. But the complete determination of a thing is impossible, since an infinite knowledge would be required to search out all the predicates which belong to a thing; I can therefore proceed to infinity, and yet not completely determine the thing. For instance: the soul is corporeal, not corporeal; the soul is mortal, not mortal. In logical employment these [infinite judgments] can here count as affirmative.",[]],"wiener-s11-013":["For every thing is distinguished from others through determination, and all other things, affected with *non*, can be asserted of it. For instance, the mark of a stone is hardness. Now I can go on to infinity and say: a stone is not metal, not wood, etc. But do I thereby say anything new? For what does it help me to know that everything else outside the concept is not stone? The *sphaera* of all this remainder is infinite, and for this reason these judgments are entitled *judicia infinita*. The quantity of judgments distinguishes them as universal, particular, and singular. For instance: all men are mortal; some men are mortal; Caesar is mortal. Here I have a *judicium universale*, *particulare* and *singulare*. In every *judicium singulare* the predicate holds of the subject without exception. When I say ‘Caesar is mortal’, no exception can here occur, because the concept Caesar is a singular concept, which does not comprehend a multitude under it, but is only a single thing. Consequently it holds just as much without exception as the *judicium universale*, since it has no *sphaera* at all from which anything could be excepted. The universal [judgment] holds without exception because its *sphaera* comprehends everything; and consequently the *judicium singulare* is, in its employment, equivalent to the universal. Yet there is a difference, which must be attended to, although in formal employment the two may go together.",[]],"wiener-s11-014":["The logicians have a practice of making the matter clear by means of certain vowels [*vocales*], in the following verses:",[]],"wiener-s11-015":["*Asserit A Negat E sed universaliter ambo.* [A affirms, E denies, but both universally.]",[]],"wiener-s11-016":["*Asserit I Negat O sed particulariter ambo.* [I affirms, O denies, but both particularly.]",[]],"wiener-s11-017":["That is, the *universaliter affirmans* [universal affirmative] is the vowel A; for instance, all men are mortal. The *judicium universaliter negans* [universal negative] is to be signified by E; for instance, no man is mortal. The *particulariter affirmans* [particular affirmative] is I; for instance, some men are learned. The *particulariter negans* [particular negative] is O; for instance, some men are not learned. This is afterwards employed in syllogistic, where unintelligible words which mean nothing, such as *celarent*, *barocco*, are used in order to show through the three vowels of the word the quality of the propositions. – There are likewise certain scholastic sayings which have to be committed to memory. It is asked: *quae, qualis, quanta est propositio?* [what, of what kind, of what extent is the proposition?] *Quae propositio* refers to relation, whether the judgments are categorical, hypothetical, or disjunctive; *qualis propositio*, whether they are affirmative or negative; *quanta propositio*, whether they are universal or particular propositions. It is from this that [the doctrine of] the quality and quantity of judgments has arisen.",[]],"wiener-s11-018":["The author [Meier] here treats further of the condition of judgments. This is the determination of the subject in a categorical judgment which contains the ground of [the judgment's] truth. For instance, if I say ‘a man is punishable’, I see that without a condition this does not belong at all to the concept of man. But if I say ‘a man who is vicious is punishable’, then being vicious is the determination of the subject. Properly speaking, this does not belong to logic, since logic is not concerned at all with content. – The analysis [*Zergliederung*] of a judgment consists in appraising a judgment according to the three *modi judicandi* [modes of judging]. This was required in the schools in former times, when it was asked: *quae, qualis, quanta?*",[]],"wiener-s11-019":["As regards the relation of judgments, all judgments are categorical, or hypothetical, or disjunctive. The matter of all categorical propositions consists in a concept in which the concept of the subject belongs to the concept of the predicate. For instance, in ‘man is mortal’ the subject, man, belongs to the concept of the predicate, being mortal. – In hypothetical judgments the matter consists of two judgments. For instance: if God is just, evil will be punished. By a *propositio hypothetica* I mean to say that if the one is assumed, the other must also be assumed: one judgment holds under the condition of the other. – *Propositiones disjunctivae* are, as regards their matter, two or more judgments which are always considered in opposition, and of which I represent that all of them, taken together, make up everything that can be said by the judgment. If one of them is true, all [the others] are false. If all but one are false, that one must be true; for since everything has been thought that can be thought, and all the rest are false, this one must be true. The matter of disjunctive judgments is thus various judgments, which, however, are considered in opposition, so that all the judgments taken together make up the whole judgment. It is evident that they simply constitute a logical division. For if, for instance, I say that all triangles are either isosceles, or equilateral, or equiangular, the triangle is divided into as many members as it properly can be.",[]],"wiener-s11-020":["The form in judgments of relation is that there is considered either the relation of a subject to the predicate, or of a ground to its consequence, or the relation of two or more judgments in so far as they disjunctively fill the *sphaera* of a concept. – Categorical judgments constitute the *basis* of all the others; in them the relation of the subject to the predicate is indicated. The hypothetical judgment is composed of two problematic judgments; the disjunctive, of two or more judgments. In the hypothetical judgment I consider the connection of two judgments as ground and consequence. In the disjunctive, all the categorical judgments are members of the division. It is to be noted, however, that the judgments in hypothetical and disjunctive judgments cannot be converted back into categorical ones. – The matter of a categorical judgment consists of two concepts; the form, in the relation in which the one concerns the subject and the other the predicate. For instance: all men are mortal. – In the hypothetical judgment the matter consists of two judgments. For instance: if the soul is corporeal, there is no hope of the necessity of another life. The ‘if’ expresses the relation. When a judgment is thought in this manner, without saying what is true and what is not true, it is a problematic judgment, which is assumed in order to see whether the other will follow if I assume it. It is thought merely in respect of its possibility. I see only whether the judgment is possible, even though the thing itself is not possible; for men have arrived at all sorts of strange propositions and have maintained them.",[]],"wiener-s11-021":["This, then, is a judgment which, as regards modality, I do not regard as true; and it is entitled problematic. I say: there is no other life. I will simply think this, in order to see how the proposition I think would stand in connection with the other. The matter of hypothetical judgments thus consists of two propositions. Now I say: however it may stand with the propositions [themselves], this much is certain, that if I assume that the soul is corporeal, it cannot possibly be said that it is immortal. In all hypothetical judgments there are two judgments which are thought problematically, not assertorically, and which constitute the relation of a concept as ground to consequence. – It used to be said in the schools, and the author says it, [that something is asserted] *per thesin*, that is, assertorically, or *per hypothesin*, that is, problematically. *Per thesin* is to say something categorically; *per hypothesin*, to say something problematically, where, however, the propositions are none the less categorical in so far as the relation and the *nexus* of the ground with the consequence is indicated. In all hypothetical judgments there are two problematic judgments. Their form consists in the *consequentia*, according to which the one judgment is the consequence of the other. The ground in the hypothetical judgment is entitled the *antecedens*, the consequence the *consequens*. The matter of hypothetical judgments consequently consists of the *antecedens* and *consequens*; the form, on the other hand, is the *consequentia*, that is, what we infer from the other.",[]],"wiener-s11-022":["It is well if we can always have quite distinctive words for our concepts, and need not make do with circumlocutions. Thus judgment [*Urtheil*] and proposition [*Satz*] are in actual usage distinct. But when the logicians say that a judgment is a proposition clothed in words, this means nothing, and the definition is quite worthless; for how will they be able to think judgments without words? We prefer, therefore, to say that a judgment considers the relation of two concepts in so far as it is problematic, whereas by propositions we understand an assertoric judgment. In judgment I test my proposition: I judge before I assert. In the proposition, however, I posit [*setze*] and assert something, and it is precisely in the assertion that the proposition consists. We therefore maintain that the consequence [*consequenz*] of hypothetical judgments is entitled a proposition, because this inference is certain and is consequently a positing [*position*].",[]],"wiener-s11-023":["Some hold that hypothetical propositions can quite well be converted into categorical ones. It can be done; but then it is no longer the same assertion. That is to abolish the hypothetical proposition and to put another in its place. It may seem to be all one whether I say ‘all men are mortal’ or ‘if something is a man, it is mortal’. But they differ. For in the second judgment it is problematic whether anything is mortal: being mortal is not asserted categorically, but holds only if being a man holds. Consequently the case is quite different with categorical propositions from what it is with hypothetical ones. In the hypothetical proposition it is not yet asserted at all that something is, but only that it is if something, namely the ground, is assumed.",[]],"wiener-s11-024":["In categorical propositions, however, there is no condition laid down. They are therefore judgments essentially different from one another. In all hypothetical judgments I have two *modi*, a *modus ponens* and a *modus tollens*. The *modus ponens* states: if the *antecedens* is true, the *consequens* is also true. The *modus tollens* states: if the *consequens* is false, the *antecedens* is also false. This comes up later in syllogistic.",[]],"wiener-s11-025":["The disjunctive judgment contains the relation of various judgments in so far as, as *membra dividentia*, they are equal to the *sphaera* of a *cognitio divisa* [divided knowledge]. For instance, all triangles are, as regards their angles, either right-angled, or acute-angled, or obtuse-angled. – I represent the various members as opposed to one another, and as together making up the whole *sphaera* of a *cognitio divisa*. This is in fact nothing but a logical division; but in the division there need not be a *conceptus divisus*: there may also be a *cognitio divisa*. For instance: if this is not the best world, God either could not or would not create a better. This is the division of the *sphaera* of the knowledge which is given to me. These are problematic judgments; but of them it is asserted assertorically that, taken together completely, they make up the *sphaera* of the concept.",[]],"wiener-s11-026":["As regards the modality of judgments, the ancients did not take the division so precisely as we do, but entitled every connecting concept [*Verbindungsbegriff*] modality. For instance: the world exists necessarily. Here, for them, the word ‘necessarily’ was the modality. But can logic judge whether a thing is necessary or not? No; for it has nothing to do with things and their necessity. It can therefore only ask whether a judgment is expressed with necessity or not. I ask only about the necessity which is to be found in the judgment. If possibility is determined from the form, the judgment is problematic; if [truth] is actually present, it is assertoric; and if it is further combined with necessity, it is an apodeictic judgment. An assertoric judgment can be merely contingently true, or apodeictically true. Contingent truths are empirical propositions; for experience shows me only how a thing is, not that it must be so. Apodeictic propositions, on the other hand, are propositions *a priori*, in which I at the same time know their necessity. *Judicia exponibilia* [exponible judgments]. For instance, if I say ‘few men are learned’, I can derive from it: 1.) some men are learned, for few are at any rate some; 2.) many men are not learned, for ‘few’ is the opposite of ‘many’. These two propositions are enclosed in one proposition, which contains an affirmation and a negation, but is none the less expressed in the form of affirmation. Such are exponible judgments.",[]],"wiener-s11-027":["Something not logical but aesthetic may enter into a judgment. A judgment is 1.) something logical, which concerns the understanding; 2.) something sensible, which concerns the senses. For instance, if I say ‘the worth of virtue is the greatest’, that is logical; but [if I say] ‘the worth of virtue surpasses everything, to our wonder’, or if I add an ‘O!’ or ‘Ah!’, these additions are something aesthetic in the judgment. It would look very ridiculous if anyone sought to bring aesthetic into mathematics. In the same way, whoever sought to bring mockery into philosophy would be writing not purely logically but aesthetically as well. These must be carefully distinguished from one another and not confounded; for the impression of the aesthetic often blinds us in respect of the logical. When the truth of the matter is in question, everything that can stir the affections must be omitted. That is left to those who seek to obtain assent surreptitiously by finely decked-out expressions, and not to earn it by grounds.",[]],"wiener-s11-028":["Practical judgments contain an ought: the necessity why something is to happen for some purpose. Theoretical judgments contain a being or a not-being; [in them] I state whether [things] are in a certain respect or not. A judgment that is capable of proof is provable, *demonstrabel*. An unprovable judgment is one that is not capable of proof. Among all judgments there must be some that are not capable of proof and are immediately certain, that is, are unprovable propositions. Those, on the other hand, that still require a proof are provable. – However many propositions there may be that are mediately certain, there must in the end be some that are immediately certain. For example: between two points I can draw only one straight line. –",[]],"wiener-s11-029":["Empty judgments. Of those judgments that are immediately certain, some rest on identity, for example: every body is extended. When I analyse [entwickele] the concept of body, I find that in it I think the concept of extension. Here, then, there is an identity of body and extension. For impenetrable extension is a part of the concept of body and is contained in it. –",[]],"wiener-s11-030":["When the identity is *explicata* [explicit], an analytic proposition of this kind, resting on identity, is a tautological proposition; for example, man is man. But when I say that man is a rational being, the concept is the same only *implicite* [implicitly]. Every proposition that is implicitly identical is provable, since it can be analysed into its parts. A proposition that is explicitly identical, on the other hand, is altogether *indemonstrabel*. Such a proposition may be entitled an empty proposition; for example, every body is bodily. Propositions that are implicitly identical are not, however, empty propositions. For through them a mark that lay hidden is made clear; for example, all bodies are extended. Here I need only make the identity distinct *per analysin* [by analysis], and I see that it is contained in the concept not tautologically but mediately. – It is, however, quite false, and unworthy of so great a man, when the author says that in the end all our modes of rational knowledge [Vernunfterkenntniße] rest on empty propositions. For they would then rest, in effect, on nothing. No; they rest on propositions that are true but implicitly identical, and a great part of them rests on *analysis*, through which I have insight into the relation between *subject* and *praedicat*. Propositions are entitled empty when they are empty of sense, but also when they are empty of fruit and without use. Identical, tautological propositions are not empty of sense, but they are empty of fruit. All judgments serve only to give distinct concepts of things. For example: every man is a rational being. This is a distinct concept of man. Here I have made clear a mark of the concept; that is, I have made it distinct through its predicate: I have judged. Consequently we can have no clear marks save through a categorical judgment, and judgments therefore serve to attain distinct concepts. A judgment, however, is empty if it produces no distinct concept. It is not empty of sense, but logically empty. Nothing useful is achieved through such judgments, since they give no distinct concept and do not fulfil the ends of the understanding. Battology, which resembles tautology, is the useless employment of many words, where the concept with many words < bricht ab >",[]],"wiener-s11-031":["In a new hand, to the end, on an attached sheet:",[]],"wiener-s11-032":["A system, then, is a combination of certain modes of knowledge [Erk.] which by their nature necessarily follow upon one another. Its requirements are: (1) definitions; (2) *principia acroamatica*, [established] only through words, not through intuition; (3) postulates: here I know how to set to work; (4) *theoremata*, which are both capable of proof and in need of it: what can be proved must be proved; (5) *problemata*, practical propositions which are provable and must also be proved. A problem has three parts: (1) *Quaestio*, (2) *Resolutio*, (3) *Demonstratio* (which is often not necessary); (6) *Scholion*, an elucidation or application. A *lemma* is episodic; for example, God in moral philosophy. *Lemmata* are necessary. The members of the system are named differently according to their principal purpose: definitions, *axiomata*, *theoremata*, *problem*; *principia minus principalia*, *corrollaria*. [What is the] difference between *theoremata* and *corrol.*? *Scholia* serve to arouse interest and to embellish; they extend only the [system's] usefulness. *Qualitates occultae* [occult qualities]: for example, whence the generative power of animals? From a vivifying force. And whence this? etc. – [this is] tautological. So too with sympathy, antipathy, etc. Conjecture would rather have a false ground than none; but it hinders further inquiry, and is therefore harmful. In this way preachers and moralists have erred.",[]],"wiener-s12-001":["An inference from a judgment is of another kind. It is mediate where there are concepts in addition which do not lie in the judgment; for example, all bodies are divisible, because they are composite. But [to infer] 'all bodies are divisible, therefore some are also' is a *consequentia immediata* [immediate inference], since [the two judgments] are one in matter and differ only in form; for example, all men are mortal, therefore some mortal things are men; or [all men are mortal, therefore] no man is immortal. Here the *conclusum* and the *judicium concludens* are one. An inference of the understanding is also to be distinguished from a *ratiocinium*, in which the *judicium* requires reason, since [the judgments] differ *materialiter*; for example, all men are mortal, therefore Caius also [is mortal].",[]],"wiener-s12-002":["*Judicia immediata* are [obtained] (1) *per judicia aequipollentia* [through equipollent judgments]: some men are not7 ° virtuous, therefore not all men are virtuous; 'nothing is without a ground' and 'everything has its ground' are substitutions. (2) [*Per judicia*] *subalternata*, which likewise differ only in form; for example, all men are mortal, therefore some [men are mortal]: they differ in quantity. (3) *Per judicia opposita*: 'some men are learned', 'some are not' differ in quality; 'some men are not mortal', 'all men are mortal' [differ in] quantity and quality. These are (a) *contradictoria*, when the one affirms or denies universally and the other denies or affirms particularly. This is opposition in the proper sense, where the affirmation of the one cancels the other, *affirmando unum negat alterum*, and conversely. (Proofs are apagogic, *indirecte*, in this way; only one must see whether [the judgments] are really *contradictoria opposita*. In metaphysics one constantly comes, with the opposite,",[]],"wiener-s12-003":["upon absurdities.) Many prove [a proposition] because its opposite leads to an absurdity, and triumph; but the opponent can often do the very same. (b) *Subcontrarie opposita*: some men are learned, some are not. Both are perhaps true. They differ in quality; in quantity they are one. Here no inference *indirecte* can be made. Here both can be true, but both cannot be false. The inference is therefore valid that if the one is false the other is true, but not conversely. (c) *Contrarie opposita*: both are universal and differ only in quality: all men are learned, no man is learned. Both can be false, but not [both] true; they are the opposite of the preceding.",[]],"wiener-s12-004":["(4) *Per conversionem judiciorum*: all men are mortal, therefore some mortal [beings are] men. [If] the quantity must be lost, [the conversion] is entitled *conversio per accidens*; if the quantity remains, it is entitled *simpliciter talis*; for example, no man is without enemies, etc. Universal affirmatives convert only *per accidens*, but [universal] negatives convert also *stricte talis*. – All particulars, whether affirmative or negative, can be converted *simpliciter*: some men are learned, etc.",[]],"wiener-s12-005":["(5) *Consequentia immediata per contrapositionem*, where the quality is also altered: all men are mortal, [therefore] something mortal is not a man. All affirmatives can be contraposed: all men are mortal, [therefore] nothing that is not mortal is a man. With *immediatis*, then, the alteration concerns only the form: [that in] b is *quoad qualitatem*, [that in] c *quoad relationem*. – *Contraoppositio* shows merely the modality of judgments, the necessity of an affirmative judgment; for example, all men are mortal: if this is true, then necessarily what is not mortal is not a man.",[]],"wiener-s13-001":["Here there is a *nota intermedia* [intermediate mark]: Caius is mortal because he is a man. From this [arises a] *consequentia mediata*. An inference of reason [Vernunftschluß] is [the knowledge of] the truth of a judgment through the subsumption of its condition under another judgment. It requires: (1) a rule, the *major* (*maxima*); (2) the *minor*, which is the subsumption of the knowledge under the condition of the rule: the predicate of the subsumption belongs to the rule (to subsume is to know that something falls under the condition of a rule); (3) the predicate of the rule [asserted] of the subsumption. Two premisses [ Textlücke ]. The premisses are the matter, the conclusion the form. (The matter of a categorical [inference consists of] two concepts; of hypothetical and disjunctive inferences of reason, of two judgments.) The division is not by quantity, for the *major* is always universal; quality does nothing here; nor [is it] into problematic, assertoric and apodeictic, for the *Conclusio* is always apodeictic. The division is: (1) categorical; (2) hypothetical, where the *major* expresses the relation of ground to consequence; (3) disjunctive, where the *major* expresses the members of the division. The ground of the division is the *relatio majoris*. – The author, however, calls only categorical inferences regular inferences, but < bricht ab >",[]]};
